/**
 * Teach Me explanations for accounting, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["accounting"] = {
 "|The fundamental accounting equation is:|Assets = Capital − Liabilities|Assets = Capital + Liabilities|Liabilities = Assets + Capital|Capital = Assets + Liabilities": "The accounting equation says that everything a business owns (assets) was financed either by the owner (capital) or by outsiders (liabilities). So Assets = Capital + Liabilities. Every transaction changes at least two items but the two sides always stay equal; this is why double entry works. For example, if capital is ₦300,000 and liabilities are ₦100,000, assets must be ₦400,000: B.\nThe trap: A (Assets = Capital − Liabilities) looks like the rearranged form, but the correct rearrangement is Capital = Assets − Liabilities.\nMemory tip: \"What we OWN = what we OWE the owner + what we OWE outsiders.\"",
 "|Which of the following is an example of capital expenditure?|Purchase of office stationery|Payment of electricity bills|Purchase of a motor vehicle for business use|Payment of staff salaries": "Capital expenditure is money spent to buy, add to or improve a non-current (fixed) asset that will serve the business for more than one year. Revenue expenditure is spent on running the business day to day and is used up within the year. A motor vehicle for business use will be used for several years, so it is capital expenditure and goes to the balance sheet: C. Stationery, electricity and salaries are revenue expenditure charged to the profit and loss account.\nMemory tip: Capital = Lasts (years, balance sheet); Revenue = Runs the business (this year, profit and loss).",
 "|The trading account is used to determine the:|Net profit or loss|Gross profit or loss|Total assets of the business|Cash balance": "The trading account compares net sales with the cost of goods sold. Cost of goods sold = Opening stock + Purchases + Carriage inwards − Closing stock. Sales minus cost of goods sold gives the gross profit (or gross loss), which is carried down to the profit and loss account: B. Net profit is found later, in the profit and loss account, after deducting expenses such as rent and salaries.\nThe trap: A (net profit) catches students who forget that the trading account stops at gross profit.\nMemory tip: Trading = buying and selling = GROSS; Profit and loss = expenses = NET.",
 "|In double-entry bookkeeping, a credit entry in the sales account indicates:|A decrease in sales revenue|An increase in sales revenue (income)|An asset purchased|An expense paid": "Double entry rules: debit increases assets and expenses; credit increases liabilities, capital and income. Sales is an income account, so a credit entry in the sales account records an increase in sales revenue: B. A decrease in sales (for example returns) would not be credited to sales; returns inwards are debited to a separate returns inwards account.\nMemory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits = Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits = Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|A bank reconciliation statement is prepared to:|Show the profit of the business|Reconcile the balance per cash book with the balance per bank statement by accounting for timing differences|List all debtors of the business|Calculate the tax payable": "The cash book balance and the bank statement balance rarely agree on the same date because of timing differences (unpresented cheques, uncredited lodgements) and items the business has not yet entered (bank charges, standing orders, direct credits, dishonoured cheques), plus any errors. A bank reconciliation statement explains these differences so that the two balances can be agreed: B. It is also a check on errors and fraud.\nMemory tip: Reconcile = make two friends (cash book and bank statement) agree.",
 "|The balance sheet shows the financial position of a business:|Over a period of time (e.g., one year)|At a specific point in time|For all past years|For future projections only": "The balance sheet lists assets, liabilities and capital as they stand on one date, for example \"as at 31 December 2025\". It is a snapshot, not a film: B. The trading and profit and loss account, by contrast, covers a period (\"for the year ended…\"). Under IFRS, the balance sheet is now called the statement of financial position.\nThe trap: A (over a period) describes the income statement, not the balance sheet.\nMemory tip: Balance sheet = \"AS AT\" a date; Profit and loss = \"FOR THE YEAR ENDED\".",
 "|Which of the following is a current liability?|Bank loan repayable in 5 years|Creditors (accounts payable)|Office equipment|Goodwill": "A current liability is an amount the business must pay within twelve months, such as trade creditors (accounts payable), bank overdraft and accrued expenses. Creditors for goods bought on credit are usually paid within one to three months, so they are current: B. A bank loan repayable in 5 years is a non-current (long-term) liability, while office equipment and goodwill are assets, not liabilities. Today trade creditors are often called trade payables.\nMemory tip: Current = due within a year.",
 "|Carriage outwards is classified as a:|Current asset|Cost of goods sold|Selling expense (revenue expenditure)|Capital expenditure": "Carriage inwards is the cost of bringing goods IN from suppliers, so it is added to the cost of purchases in the trading account. Carriage outwards is the cost of delivering goods OUT to customers; it is a selling and distribution expense (revenue expenditure) charged to the profit and loss account: C.\nThe trap: B (cost of goods sold) is where carriage INWARDS goes; students who mix the two lose this mark.\nMemory tip: Inwards goes into the Trading account; Outwards goes out to the Profit and loss.",
 "|The following accounts appear in a trial balance. Which would appear in the trading account?\n1. Purchases  2. Wages  3. Opening stock  4. Insurance  5. Sales|1, 2 and 5 only|1, 3 and 5 only|All five|2 and 4 only": "The trading account contains the items that make up sales and the cost of goods sold: sales (less returns inwards), opening stock, purchases (less returns outwards), carriage inwards and closing stock. From the list, purchases (1), opening stock (3) and sales (5) belong there: B. Insurance is an expense for the profit and loss account, and ordinary wages also go there; only wages described as direct or productive wages are charged in the trading account.\nMemory tip: Trading = Stock, Purchases, Sales (SPS) plus carriage inwards.",
 "|Provision for doubtful debts is created to:|Write off all bad debts immediately|Provide for the anticipated loss from customers who may not pay, applying prudence|Record interest on loans|Calculate depreciation on fixed assets": "The prudence concept says losses should be provided for as soon as they are foreseen. A provision for doubtful debts is an estimate (often a percentage of debtors) of debts that may not be paid. It is charged to the profit and loss account and deducted from debtors in the balance sheet, so debtors are shown at the amount likely to be collected: B. Debts known to be irrecoverable are written off as bad debts, not provided for.\nMemory tip: Bad debt = already lost (write off); Doubtful debt = may be lost (provide for).",
 "|Under the straight-line method of depreciation, the annual charge is:|Higher in early years and lower in later years|The same amount each year|Based on the asset's use or output|Lower in early years and higher in later years": "The straight-line (fixed instalment) method charges the same depreciation every year.\nAnnual charge = (Cost − Residual value) ÷ Useful life.\nExample: a machine costing ₦500,000 with a residual value of ₦50,000 and a 5-year life gives (₦500,000 − ₦50,000) ÷ 5 = ₦90,000 every year: B.\nThe trap: A (higher in early years, lower later) describes the reducing balance method, the other method JAMB tests most.\nMemory tip: Straight line on a graph = the same each year.",
 "|In the context of final accounts, 'accruals' are amounts that have been:|Paid in advance for services not yet received|Earned or incurred but not yet paid or received|Written off as bad debts|Transferred to reserves": "An accrual is an amount that belongs to this period but has not yet been settled in cash. An accrued expense has been incurred but not yet paid (for example December electricity unpaid at 31 December); accrued income has been earned but not yet received. Under the accruals concept both are brought into this year's accounts: B. An accrued expense is a current liability; accrued income is a current asset.\nThe trap: A (paid in advance) describes a prepayment, the opposite adjustment.\nMemory tip: Accrued = Arrears (owing); Prepaid = Paid early.",
 "|The gross profit margin is calculated as:|(Net Profit / Sales) × 100|(Gross Profit / Sales) × 100|(Gross Profit / Cost of Sales) × 100|(Net Profit / Capital) × 100": "Gross profit margin = (Gross profit ÷ Sales) × 100. It shows how many naira of gross profit are made from every ₦100 of sales. Example: sales ₦200,000, gross profit ₦50,000 gives 50,000 ÷ 200,000 × 100 = 25%: B.\nThe trap: C (gross profit ÷ cost of sales × 100) is the mark-up, not the margin; in the example the mark-up is 50,000 ÷ 150,000 × 100 = 33⅓%.\nMemory tip: Margin on sales, Mark-up on cost.",
 "|Which of the following is recorded in the debit side of the cash book?|Payments made to suppliers|Cash sales and cash received from debtors|Drawings by the owner|Bank charges": "The cash book is a ledger account for cash and bank. Its debit side records money coming IN (receipts): cash sales, cash or cheques received from debtors, capital paid in, and so on: B. The credit side records money going OUT: payments to suppliers, drawings, expenses and bank charges.\nMemory tip: Debit = Deposits in; Credit = Cash out.",
 "|What is meant by 'working capital'?|The total assets of the business|Current assets minus current liabilities — the liquid funds available for day-to-day operations|Fixed assets minus long-term liabilities|Owner's equity minus drawings": "Working capital (net current assets) = Current assets − Current liabilities. It is the fund available to run the business day to day: to buy stock, pay wages and settle creditors. Example: current assets ₦180,000 and current liabilities ₦120,000 give working capital of ₦60,000: B.\nMemory tip: Working capital keeps the business working: CA − CL.",
 "|A company's acid-test ratio is:|(Current Assets / Current Liabilities)|(Current Assets − Stock) / Current Liabilities|(Total Assets / Total Liabilities)|(Net Profit / Sales) × 100": "The acid-test (quick) ratio is a stricter test of liquidity than the current ratio because it leaves out stock, which may take time to sell.\nAcid-test ratio = (Current assets − Stock) ÷ Current liabilities.\nExample: current assets ₦90,000, stock ₦30,000, current liabilities ₦40,000 give (₦90,000 − ₦30,000) ÷ ₦40,000 = 1.5 : 1: B.\nThe trap: A (current assets ÷ current liabilities) is the current ratio, which includes stock.\nMemory tip: Acid test burns away the stock.",
 "|Which accounting concept requires that the same accounting method be used from period to period?|Accruals concept|Consistency concept|Prudence concept|Going concern concept": "The consistency concept says that once a business chooses an accounting method (for example straight-line depreciation or FIFO for stock), it should use the same method from year to year. Then profits can be compared fairly between years. A change is allowed only for a good reason, and its effect must be disclosed: B. Accruals (A) is about recording income and expenses when earned or incurred, prudence (C) about not overstating profit, and going concern (D) about the business continuing.\nMemory tip: Consistency = Same method, every year.",
 "|When a business sells goods on credit, the double entry is:|Dr Sales / Cr Debtors|Dr Debtors / Cr Sales|Dr Cash / Cr Sales|Dr Sales / Cr Cash": "A credit sale creates a debtor (the customer owes the business) and increases sales income. Assets increase by a debit, and income increases by a credit, so the entry is: Debit the customer's (debtors) account, Credit sales account: B.\nThe trap: C (Dr Cash, Cr Sales) is the entry for a CASH sale; in a credit sale no cash comes in yet.\nMemory tip: Debit the receiver; credit the giver: the customer receives the goods, the sales account gives them.",
 "|The purpose of control accounts is to:|Replace ledger accounts entirely|Provide a summary total that should equal the total of individual ledger accounts, enabling quick error detection|Record only cash transactions|Show the bank balance": "A control (total) account is a summary account for a whole ledger. The sales ledger control account totals all credit sales, cash received, discounts, returns and bad debts posted to the individual debtors' accounts. Its closing balance should equal the total of the individual balances; if not, there is an error to find in that ledger. Control accounts therefore check accuracy, help locate errors and help detect fraud: B. They do not replace the individual accounts.\nMemory tip: Control account = one total that checks many accounts.",
 "|Drawings by the owner of a sole trader are:|An expense in the profit and loss account|Deducted from capital in the balance sheet|Added to capital|Credited to the cash account only": "Drawings are cash, goods or other assets taken by the owner for personal use. They are not a business expense, so they are not charged in the profit and loss account. In the balance sheet they are deducted from capital:\nClosing capital = Opening capital + Net profit − Drawings: B.\nThe double entry is Debit drawings, Credit cash (or purchases, for goods taken).\nThe trap: A (an expense) is the commonest slip; drawings reduce what the owner has in the business, not the profit.\nMemory tip: Drawings draw down capital, not profit.",
 "|Which of the following is an intangible fixed asset?|Machinery|Land and buildings|Goodwill and patents|Motor vehicles": "A fixed (non-current) asset is held for long-term use in the business. Tangible fixed assets can be seen and touched: land and buildings, machinery, motor vehicles. Intangible fixed assets have no physical form but still bring future benefit: goodwill, patents, trademarks and copyrights: C. Machinery, land and buildings, and motor vehicles in the other options are all tangible. Under IFRS, fixed assets are now called non-current assets, and purchased intangibles are shown separately from property, plant and equipment.\nMemory tip: Intangible = cannot be touched (Goodwill, Patents, Trademarks).",
 "|A credit balance in the suspense account indicates:|Debit entries exceed credit entries in the trial balance|Credit entries exceed debit entries in the trial balance|All accounts are balanced|No errors exist in the books": "When the trial balance totals do not agree, the difference is entered in a suspense account on the side that is SHORT, so that the trial balance agrees while the errors are found. If the debit total is bigger, the credit side is short, so suspense is credited: a credit balance means debit entries exceed credit entries: A.\nExample: debits ₦150,600, credits ₦150,000; suspense ₦600 is entered on the credit side.\nThe trap: B reverses the rule, and it is tempting because students match \"credit balance\" with \"credits bigger\".\nMemory tip: Suspense fills the gap on the smaller side.",
 "|Under the FIFO (First In, First Out) method of stock valuation:|The most recently purchased stock is assumed to be sold first|The oldest stock is assumed to be issued/sold first|All stock is valued at average cost|The highest-cost stock is sold first": "FIFO (first in, first out) assumes that the goods bought first are issued or sold first. So the cost of sales is taken from the oldest prices and closing stock is valued at the most recent purchase prices. When prices are rising, FIFO gives a higher closing stock and a higher profit than LIFO: B.\nThe trap: A (most recent sold first) describes LIFO, the mirror method.\nMemory tip: FIFO is like a queue: the first to come in is the first to go out.",
 "|Which of the following would NOT cause a trial balance to disagree?|Posting ₦500 to the wrong side of an account|Omitting a transaction from the books entirely|Transposing figures (e.g., writing ₦369 as ₦396)|Making an arithmetical error in a ledger balance": "The trial balance only shows errors that make total debits differ from total credits. If a transaction is omitted completely, neither the debit nor the credit is recorded, so the totals still agree: this is an error of omission: B. Posting ₦500 to the wrong side, a transposition in one account (₦369 written as ₦396, a difference of ₦27, always divisible by 9) and a wrong addition in one ledger account all make the totals disagree.\nMemory tip: Both sides missing, both sides balanced.",
 "|The return on capital employed (ROCE) ratio measures:|How efficiently a company uses its capital to generate profit|The proportion of profits paid as dividends|The liquidity of the business|The debt-to-equity ratio": "Return on capital employed (ROCE) measures how well a business uses the capital invested in it to earn profit.\nROCE = (Net profit before interest and tax ÷ Capital employed) × 100.\nExample: profit ₦120,000 on capital employed of ₦800,000 gives 120,000 ÷ 800,000 × 100 = 15%: A. It is a profitability ratio, not a liquidity or gearing ratio.\nMemory tip: ROCE = Return On the money the owners and lenders put in.",
 "|Which of the following is a source document for accounting entries?|Trial balance|Balance sheet|Invoice|Profit and loss account": "A source document is the original paper or electronic record of a transaction, and it is the evidence from which entries are made in the books of original entry. Examples are invoices, receipts, credit notes, debit notes, cheque counterfoils, paying-in slips and petty cash vouchers. An invoice records a credit sale or purchase: C. The trial balance, balance sheet and profit and loss account are prepared later from the ledger.\nMemory tip: Source = where the transaction story begins (the first piece of paper).",
 "|The journal (journal proper) is used to:|Record only cash transactions|Record transactions that do not fit into any specialised book — mainly adjusting entries, opening entries, corrections of errors|Summarise all ledger account balances|Record bank transactions exclusively": "The journal proper (general journal) is the book of original entry for transactions that do not belong in a specialised book: opening entries, purchase or sale of fixed assets on credit, correction of errors, transfers and year-end adjustments such as depreciation and bad debts. Each entry shows the account debited, the account credited and a short narration: B. Credit sales, credit purchases and returns go to their own day books, and cash and bank go to the cash book.\nMemory tip: Journal = the \"odd jobs\" book, always with a narration.",
 "|Goodwill in a partnership arises when:|The business makes large profits every year|A new partner joins or an old partner retires, requiring the existing goodwill to be valued and shared between partners|The firm buys expensive machinery|The business pays all its debts": "Goodwill is the value of a business above its net assets, coming from its reputation, location, loyal customers and skilled staff. It is built up while the firm trades, but in partnership accounts it must be valued and adjusted whenever there is a change: admission of a partner, retirement or death of a partner, or a change in the profit-sharing ratio. Then the old partners are credited with the goodwill they helped to build: B.\nMemory tip: Goodwill is valued when the partnership changes.",
 "|Which accounting convention states that assets should be recorded at their original purchase price?|Consistency|Materiality|Historical cost convention|Realisation": "The historical cost convention says assets are recorded at what they actually cost when acquired, not at their current market value. Cost is objective and can be proved from invoices. Depreciation is then charged on that cost. Example: land bought for ₦2,000,000 stays at ₦2,000,000 in the books even if it could now sell for ₦3,500,000: C. Consistency (A) is about using the same method each year, and realisation (D) is about when revenue is recognised.\nMemory tip: Historical cost = history's price (what you paid).",
 "|In a partnership, the appropriation account deals with:|Calculating gross profit|Distributing net profit among partners (salaries, interest on capital, share of residual profit)|Recording daily transactions|Calculating working capital": "After net profit is found, the partnership appropriation account shows how it is shared, following the partnership agreement (or the Partnership Act 1890 if there is no agreement). Add interest on drawings to net profit; then deduct interest on capital and partners' salaries; the balance (residual profit) is shared in the profit-sharing ratio: B.\nExample: net profit ₦90,000, interest on capital ₦10,000 and salary ₦20,000 leave ₦60,000; shared 2:1, that is ₦40,000 and ₦20,000.\nMemory tip: Appropriation = \"Who gets what\" of the net profit.",
 "|When goods are returned by a customer, the entry is:|Dr Sales Returns / Cr Debtors|Dr Debtors / Cr Sales Returns|Dr Purchases / Cr Sales|Dr Cash / Cr Debtors": "When a credit customer returns goods, sales are reduced and the customer owes less. The returns inwards (sales returns) account is debited, and the customer's (debtors) account is credited: A. The source document is the credit note sent to the customer, and the book of original entry is the returns inwards day book.\nThe trap: B is the same pair of accounts with the sides reversed.\nMemory tip: Returns INWARDS: goods come in, so Debit returns inwards; the customer gets credit.",
 "|Which of the following is a feature of a limited liability company?|The owners are personally liable for all debts|The company is not a separate legal entity|Shareholders' liability is limited to the amount unpaid on their shares|The company cannot sue or be sued": "A limited liability company is a separate legal person: it owns property, can sue and be sued in its own name, and continues even if shareholders change. Shareholders can lose only what they have paid or agreed to pay for their shares; once their shares are fully paid, their personal assets cannot be taken for the company's debts: C. In Nigeria, companies are now registered under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 2020.\nMemory tip: Limited company = limited loss for the shareholder.",
 "|In accounting, 'prepayments' are:|Expenses incurred but not yet paid|Expenses paid in advance for goods or services not yet received|Income earned but not yet received|Amounts owed to suppliers": "A prepayment is an expense paid in advance: part of the payment belongs to a future period. Example: rent of ₦120,000 paid on 1 October for 12 months; at 31 December only 3 months (₦30,000) belong to this year, so ₦90,000 is a prepayment. It is deducted from the rent expense and shown as a current asset: B.\nThe trap: A (incurred but not yet paid) is an accrued expense, the opposite adjustment.\nMemory tip: Prepaid = Paid before; it is an asset because the business is owed the service.",
 "|The average cost of stock method (AVCO) values closing stock at:|The cost of the oldest items in stock|The cost of the most recent items|The weighted average cost of all units purchased during the period|The market value of stock": "The weighted average cost (AVCO) method values each unit at the average cost of all units available.\nAverage cost = (Cost of opening stock + Cost of purchases) ÷ (Units of opening stock + Units purchased).\nExample: 100 units at ₦20 and 300 units at ₦24 cost ₦2,000 + ₦7,200 = ₦9,200 for 400 units, an average of ₦23 a unit. In the perpetual version a new average is worked out after each purchase: C.\nMemory tip: AVCO = Average of everything available.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a non-current (fixed) liability?|Bank overdraft|Trade creditors|Long-term bank loan repayable in 7 years|Accrued expenses": "A non-current (long-term) liability is a debt that is not due for payment within the next twelve months. A bank loan repayable in 7 years is the clear example: C. A bank overdraft, trade creditors and accrued expenses are all current liabilities because they must be settled within a year. Debentures and long-term loans are the other common non-current liabilities.\nMemory tip: Non-current liability = owe it, but not this year.",
 "|The purpose of depreciation is to:|Reduce the market value of an asset|Allocate the cost of a tangible fixed asset over its useful economic life, matching cost with revenue generated|Increase profits shown in the accounts|Replace the asset at the end of its life": "Depreciation is the part of the cost of a fixed asset that is used up in a period through wear and tear, use or passage of time. Charging it each year spreads the cost of the asset over its useful life and matches that cost against the revenue the asset helps to earn: B. Depreciation is not a way of valuing the asset at market price, and it does not by itself set aside cash to replace the asset.\nMemory tip: Depreciation = spreading cost, not saving cash.",
 "|An imprest system of petty cash means:|Petty cash is held in coins only|The petty cash balance is restored to a fixed amount at the start of each period by reimbursing the total spent|Petty cash can be spent on any amount|Petty cash replaces the main cash book": "Under the imprest system, the petty cashier starts with a fixed float (the imprest), say ₦20,000. At the end of the period the chief cashier reimburses exactly what was spent, so the float is restored to ₦20,000 for the next period. If ₦14,500 was spent, ₦14,500 is refunded: B. This controls small payments, because cash in hand plus vouchers must always equal the imprest.\nMemory tip: Imprest = refill to the same level each time.",
 "|Which of the following ratios measures a business's ability to pay its short-term debts?|Return on capital employed|Debt/equity ratio|Current ratio (Current Assets / Current Liabilities)|Net profit margin": "Liquidity means the ability to pay short-term debts as they fall due. The current ratio = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities. A ratio around 2:1 is traditionally thought comfortable; below 1:1 suggests the business may struggle: C. ROCE and net profit margin measure profitability, and debt/equity measures gearing (long-term financing).\nMemory tip: Current ratio tests the Current (short-term) debts.",
 "|In accounting, the matching concept means:|Assets must always equal liabilities|Revenue earned in a period should be matched with the expenses incurred to generate that revenue in the same period|All income must be received in cash|Capital equals profits": "The matching concept says that expenses should be recorded in the same period as the revenue they helped to earn, whether or not cash has been paid or received. It is closely linked to the accruals concept and is the reason for adjustments such as accruals, prepayments, depreciation and closing stock: B.\nMemory tip: Match the cost to the sale it made.",
 "|Which of the following errors would be revealed by a trial balance?|Error of omission|Error of commission|Error of principle|Arithmetic error causing unequal debit and credit totals": "A trial balance shows only errors that make the debit and credit totals unequal: wrong additions (arithmetic errors), entries posted on one side only, posting to the wrong side of one account, and different amounts posted on the two sides. Errors of omission, commission, principle, original entry, complete reversal and compensating errors do not affect the totals, so they are not revealed: D.\nMemory tip: \"Can a TB see it?\" Only if one side is different from the other.",
 "|The concept of 'going concern' in accounting assumes:|The business will close at year end|The business will continue to operate for the foreseeable future, justifying long-term asset valuation|All debts will be paid immediately|Assets will be sold at market value": "The going concern concept assumes the business will continue operating for the foreseeable future and has no intention or need to close down. That is why fixed assets are shown at cost less depreciation instead of what they would fetch in a forced sale, and why prepayments and long-term debts are carried forward: B.\nValuing assets at what they would fetch in a sale (D) is the break-up basis, used only when the business is expected to close.\nMemory tip: Going concern = the business keeps going.",
 "|In a sole trader's accounts, the owner's drawings are:|Added to profit in the profit and loss account|Deducted from capital in the balance sheet|Shown as an expense in the trading account|Treated as a liability": "Drawings are what the owner takes out of the business for private use. They are not an expense and are not a liability of the business to an outsider; they reduce the owner's interest in the business:\nClosing capital = Opening capital + Net profit − Drawings.\nExample: capital ₦500,000, net profit ₦80,000, drawings ₦30,000 give closing capital of ₦550,000: B.\nMemory tip: Drawings come off capital, never off profit.",
 "|Which document is prepared first in the accounting cycle?|Trial balance|Balance sheet|Journal entries|Profit and loss account": "The accounting cycle runs: source documents → books of original entry (journals and day books) → ledger → trial balance → final accounts (trading, profit and loss account and balance sheet). The source document is evidence, not something the accountant prepares; the first record the accountant prepares from it is the journal entry: C. The trial balance comes only after posting to the ledger.\nMemory tip: S-J-L-T-F: Source, Journal, Ledger, Trial balance, Final accounts.",
 "|Debentures in a company's balance sheet are classified as:|Current assets|Non-current liabilities (long-term borrowing)|Shareholders' equity|Current liabilities": "Debentures are long-term loans to a company, usually carrying a fixed rate of interest and secured on the company's assets. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners, so debentures are shown as non-current (long-term) liabilities, not as part of shareholders' equity: B. Debenture interest is an expense charged before profit, whereas dividends are an appropriation of profit.\nThe trap: C (shareholders' equity) catches students who confuse debentures with shares; shareholders own the company, debenture holders lend to it.\nMemory tip: Shares = owners; Debentures = lenders.",
 "|The accounting equation after a credit sale of ₦50,000 on 30-day terms becomes:|Assets increase by ₦50,000; Liabilities decrease by ₦50,000|Assets (debtors) increase by ₦50,000; Revenue (equity) increases by ₦50,000|Assets decrease; Equity decreases|No change in accounting equation": "A credit sale of ₦50,000 increases an asset (debtors) by ₦50,000 and increases sales income by ₦50,000. Income adds to profit, and profit belongs to the owner, so it raises capital (equity). The equation becomes:\nAssets + ₦50,000 = Capital + ₦50,000 + Liabilities, still balanced: B. Liabilities are not affected because no one outside is owed money.\nMemory tip: Income raises capital, so both sides of A = C + L rise together.",
 "|The current growth in the volume of trading and financial dealings in Nigeria is helped by|increased financial activities|government intervention|credit as a factor in business|paying for goods in cash": "Bookkeeping grew because trade grew, and trade grew largely because of credit. When a seller lets a buyer take goods now and pay later, far more business can be done than if every sale waited for cash; banks, suppliers and customers all extend credit to one another. Credit dealings also create debtors and creditors that must be recorded, which is why careful accounts became necessary: C. Option A only restates the question (growth in financial dealings), so it does not explain anything. This is a debatable item; the answer marked follows the factor textbooks actually give. Paying in cash (D) limits trade rather than helping it grow. Memory tip: Credit Creates Commerce (and the need for accounts).",
 "|Cost accounting entails provision of information|for investment purpose|for decision making|to shareholders|to stockholders": "Financial accounting reports to outsiders (shareholders, lenders, government) on past results. Cost accounting is aimed at managers inside the business: it collects, classifies and analyses the cost of products, jobs and departments so that management can set prices, control waste, budget and choose between alternatives. Its purpose is therefore to provide information for decision making: B. Shareholders and stockholders (C and D) are mainly served by financial accounts, and investment information (A) is only one narrow kind of decision. Memory tip: Cost accounting = Control and Choices for managers.",
 "|The main objective of accounting report is to provide information about|a company's shareholding|an entity's management|the efficacy of assets|a company's economic resources": "The conceptual framework says general-purpose financial reports give users information about the entity's economic resources (assets), the claims against them (liabilities and equity), and how these changed through performance and cash flows. Users such as investors and lenders use this to make economic decisions: D. Reports also help users judge management's stewardship, which is why B is sometimes argued, but management is not the subject of the report, the resources are; this item is debatable. Shareholding (A) and \"efficacy of assets\" (C) are too narrow. Today the IASB Conceptual Framework (2018) describes the objective in these same terms. Memory tip: reports describe Resources, Claims and Changes.",
 "|If liabilities amounted to ₦12,045, other assets ₦36,800 and equity ₦26,896, the cash at hand would be|₦2,241|₦2,214|₦2,141|₦2,114": "The accounting equation is Assets = Liabilities + Equity.\nStep 1: Total assets = ₦12,045 + ₦26,896 = ₦38,941.\nStep 2: Assets other than cash are ₦36,800, so cash = ₦38,941 − ₦36,800 = ₦2,141: C.\nCheck: ₦36,800 + ₦2,141 = ₦38,941 = ₦12,045 + ₦26,896.\nThe trap: A (₦2,241), B (₦2,214) and D (₦2,114) use the same digits, so a careless subtraction or copying slip lands on one of them; always add back to check. Memory tip: A = L + E; the missing asset is the gap between the two sides.",
 "|Use the following information: \nFixed assets ... ₦85,600\nSales ... ₦197,000\nStock ... ₦34,300\nSalaries and wages ... ₦37,000\nPurchases ... ₦127,700\nShare capital ... ₦120,000\nCreditors ... ₦16,050\nMotor expenses ... ₦10,500\nDebtors ... ₦25,000\n\nWhat is the cash balance?|₦12,095|₦12,590|₦12,950|₦21,800": "Cash is the balancing figure in the trial balance.\nStep 1: Credit balances (income, capital, liabilities): sales ₦197,000 + share capital ₦120,000 + creditors ₦16,050 = ₦333,050.\nStep 2: Debit balances (assets and expenses) other than cash: fixed assets ₦85,600 + stock ₦34,300 + salaries and wages ₦37,000 + purchases ₦127,700 + motor expenses ₦10,500 + debtors ₦25,000 = ₦320,100.\nStep 3: Cash = ₦333,050 − ₦320,100 = ₦12,950: C.\nThe trap: A (₦12,095) and B (₦12,590) contain the same digits in a different order, so check your subtraction carefully. Memory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits are Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits are Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|Use the following information: \nFixed assets ... ₦85,600\nSales ... ₦197,000\nStock ... ₦34,300\nSalaries and wages ... ₦37,000\nPurchases ... ₦127,700\nShare capital ... ₦120,000\nCreditors ... ₦16,050\nMotor expenses ... ₦10,500\nDebtors ... ₦25,000\n\nDetermine the total of the trial balance.|₦335,050|₦333,050|₦323,050|₦230,550": "A trial balance lists every ledger balance, debits in one column and credits in the other, and both columns must total the same.\nStep 1: Credits are sales ₦197,000, share capital ₦120,000 and creditors ₦16,050 = ₦333,050.\nStep 2: Debits are fixed assets, stock, salaries, purchases, motor expenses and debtors (₦320,100) plus cash ₦12,950 = ₦333,050.\nSo the trial balance totals ₦333,050: B.\nThe trap: A (₦335,050) differs by only ₦2,000, the kind of figure produced by a single addition slip. Memory tip: one side you can fully add is the total of both sides.",
 "|The accuracy of journalizing is checked by|ensuring that debit totals equal credit totals|posting all journal entries to ledger accounts|adding all figures in the debit column|comparing accounts in the ledger against the journals": "The journal is a book of original entry in which each transaction is recorded with its debit and its credit before posting. Because every entry must have equal debits and credits, the journal is proved by checking that the total of its debit column equals the total of its credit column: A. Posting to the ledger (B) is the next stage, not a check; adding only the debit column (C) proves nothing; and comparing the ledger with the journal (D) checks that posting was done correctly, which is a later check. This item is debatable because D is also a form of verification. Memory tip: Journal check = Debits equal Credits; Ledger check = trial balance.",
 "|Hauwa Ltd bought 10 bags of rice for ₦500 each. The company was given 5% and 12% trade and cash discounts respectively. What will be recorded as discount received in the company's book?|₦250|₦570|₦600|₦850": "Trade discount is deducted on the invoice and never entered in the books; only cash discount is recorded as discount received.\nStep 1: Gross price = 10 × ₦500 = ₦5,000.\nStep 2: Trade discount 5% = ₦250, so the invoice is ₦4,750.\nStep 3: Cash discount 12% × ₦4,750 = ₦570: B.\nThe trap: D (₦850) adds 5% and 12% of ₦5,000, recording the trade discount and working cash discount on the gross price, two classic mistakes. C (₦600) is 12% of the gross price. Memory tip: Trade discount is Taken off before; Cash discount is Counted in the books.",
 "|A cash book had opening balance of ₦15,200, closing balance ₦18,400 and total cash received during the period ₦36,000. What was the amount of cash paid out during the same period?|₦17,800|₦19,600|₦29,200|₦32,800": "A cash book works like a bucket: what you start with plus what comes in, minus what goes out, equals what is left.\nStep 1: Opening balance + receipts = ₦15,200 + ₦36,000 = ₦51,200.\nStep 2: Payments = ₦51,200 − closing ₦18,400 = ₦32,800: D.\nCheck: ₦15,200 + ₦36,000 − ₦32,800 = ₦18,400.\nMemory tip: Opening + Receipts − Payments = Closing, so any one can be found from the other three.",
 "|In a petty cash book, the closing balance was ₦235, imprest ₦1,250, while the stationery expenses were ₦655. How much was paid for other general expenses?|₦360|₦355|₦350|₦305": "Under the imprest system the petty cashier starts each period with a fixed float (the imprest), and what has been spent is the imprest minus the cash left.\nStep 1: Total spent = ₦1,250 − ₦235 = ₦1,015.\nStep 2: Other general expenses = ₦1,015 − stationery ₦655 = ₦360: A.\nAt the end of the period the main cashier reimburses the ₦1,015 spent, restoring the float to ₦1,250. Memory tip: Imprest − Balance = Spent = what gets reimbursed.",
 "|When a bill is negotiated to a bank, it is said to be|surrendered|cashed|discounted|accepted": "A bill of exchange is a written order to pay a sum on a future date. A holder who needs cash before the bill matures can negotiate (sell) it to a bank, which pays the face value less a charge for waiting, called discount. The bill is therefore said to be discounted: C. \"Accepted\" (D) is what the drawee does when he signs the bill agreeing to pay; \"cashed\" and \"surrendered\" are not technical terms for this. If the bill is later dishonoured, the bank can claim from the holder who discounted it. Memory tip: Discounting = selling a bill early, at a discount.",
 "|Given that the balance as per cash book after necessary adjustments was ₦4,315, unpresented cheques were ₦1,688 and the bank statement balance was ₦4,791, what was the balance of the uncredited cheques?|₦1,223|₦1,212|₦1,202|₦1,115": "The bank statement differs from the adjusted cash book only by timing items. Unpresented cheques (paid out in the cash book, not yet by the bank) make the bank balance higher; uncredited cheques (paid in, not yet credited by the bank) make it lower.\nStep 1: Bank balance = cash book + unpresented − uncredited.\nStep 2: ₦4,791 = ₦4,315 + ₦1,688 − x, so x = ₦6,003 − ₦4,791 = ₦1,212: B.\nThe trap: A (₦1,223) and C (₦1,202) are a digit away from the answer. Memory tip: Unpresented Up the bank, Uncredited Under it.",
 "|If a property developing company sells a completed building to an interested third party, this sale can be treated in the books of the company as|capital receipt|revenue receipt|windfall receipt|miscellaneous receipt": "Whether a receipt is capital or revenue depends on the nature of the business. For a property development company, building and selling houses is its ordinary trade; the completed building is its stock-in-trade, just as cloth is stock for a cloth seller. The sale proceeds are therefore a revenue receipt, shown as sales in the trading account: B. The same building sold by a company that used it as its office would be a fixed asset, and its sale would be a capital receipt. The trap: A (capital receipt) catches students who think \"building\" always means fixed asset. Memory tip: ask \"is this the firm's normal trade?\" If yes, it is revenue.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPalaju Enterprises had fixed assets of ₦3,100,000 at the end of the year. Fixed assets costing ₦900,000 with provision for depreciation of ₦150,000 were disposed of during the period. Fixed assets of ₦100,000 were added during the year. Provision for depreciation was ₦350,000 at the beginning of the year and ₦300,000 at the end of the year.\n\nWhat was the value of fixed assets at the beginning of the year?|₦2,900,000|₦3,800,000|₦3,900,000|₦4,000,000": "Work the fixed asset (cost) account backwards.\nStep 1: Opening cost + additions − disposals = closing cost.\nStep 2: Opening = ₦3,100,000 − ₦100,000 + ₦900,000 = ₦3,900,000: C.\nCheck: ₦3,900,000 + ₦100,000 − ₦900,000 = ₦3,100,000.\nThe trap: B (₦3,800,000) comes from adding the ₦100,000 addition instead of deducting it. The ₦150,000 provision on the disposed assets affects the provision account, not the cost account. Memory tip: the asset account holds COST only; depreciation lives in its own account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPalaju Enterprises had fixed assets of ₦3,100,000 at the end of the year. Fixed assets costing ₦900,000 with provision for depreciation of ₦150,000 were disposed of during the period. Fixed assets of ₦100,000 were added during the year. Provision for depreciation was ₦350,000 at the beginning of the year and ₦300,000 at the end of the year.\n\nThe depreciation expense charged for the year is|₦450,000|₦250,000|₦150,000|₦100,000": "The year's depreciation charge is the figure that balances the provision for depreciation account.\nStep 1: Opening provision ₦350,000.\nStep 2: Remove the provision on the assets sold: ₦350,000 − ₦150,000 = ₦200,000.\nStep 3: Closing provision is ₦300,000, so the charge = ₦300,000 − ₦200,000 = ₦100,000: D.\nThe trap: B (₦250,000) adds the ₦150,000 transfer to the charge, and C (₦150,000) mistakes the disposal transfer for the charge. Memory tip: Opening − Disposal + Charge = Closing.",
 "|A reduction in the catalogue price of an article given by a wholesaler to a retailer is called a|bad debt|trade discount|premium|cash discount": "A trade discount is a reduction from the list or catalogue price given by a manufacturer or wholesaler to traders who will resell the goods, to leave them room for profit or to reward large orders. It is shown on the invoice but never entered in the ledger: B. A cash discount (D) is a reward for paying promptly and is recorded (discount allowed or received). A bad debt is a debt that cannot be collected, and a premium is an extra amount paid. Memory tip: Trade discount = for the Trade (catalogue price cut); Cash discount = for Cash paid quickly.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock Control Account [Extract] as at January, 2000\nJan. 1 ... Receipts 100 units @ ₦5.00 = ₦500.00 ... Balance ₦500.00\nJan. 5 ... Receipts 50 units @ ₦6.00 = ₦300.00 ... Balance ₦800.00\nJan. 7 ... Issues 40 units @ ₦6.00 = ₦240.00 ... Balance ₦560.00\nJan. 10 ... Receipts 50 units @ ₦4.00 = ₦200.00 ... Balance ₦760.00\nJan. 15 ... Issues 50 units @ ₦4.00 = ₦200.00 ... Balance ₦560.00\nJan. 20 ... Issues 10 units @ X = Y ... Balance Z\n\nWhat is the stock valuation method used?|Last In First Out|First In First Out|Average cost|Weighted average": "Look at the price used for each issue. On 7 January 40 units are issued at ₦6.00, the price of the latest receipt (5 January), although older units cost ₦5.00. On 15 January 50 units are issued at ₦4.00, again the latest receipt (10 January). Issuing the most recently received goods first is Last In First Out (LIFO): A. Under FIFO (B) the issues would have been priced at ₦5.00, the oldest stock. Average methods (C, D) would give a price between the purchase prices. LIFO is no longer allowed under IAS 2 (Inventories); FIFO and weighted average are used today. Memory tip: newest price out first = LIFO.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock Control Account [Extract] as at January, 2000\nJan. 1 ... Receipts 100 units @ ₦5.00 = ₦500.00 ... Balance ₦500.00\nJan. 5 ... Receipts 50 units @ ₦6.00 = ₦300.00 ... Balance ₦800.00\nJan. 7 ... Issues 40 units @ ₦6.00 = ₦240.00 ... Balance ₦560.00\nJan. 10 ... Receipts 50 units @ ₦4.00 = ₦200.00 ... Balance ₦760.00\nJan. 15 ... Issues 50 units @ ₦4.00 = ₦200.00 ... Balance ₦560.00\nJan. 20 ... Issues 10 units @ X = Y ... Balance Z\n\nThe value of X is|₦4.00|₦5.00|₦5.50|₦6.00": "Track the layers of stock under LIFO.\nStep 1: After 5 Jan: 100 @ ₦5.00 + 50 @ ₦6.00.\nStep 2: 7 Jan issue 40 @ ₦6.00 leaves 100 @ ₦5.00 + 10 @ ₦6.00 (₦560).\nStep 3: 10 Jan receipt 50 @ ₦4.00; 15 Jan issue those 50 @ ₦4.00, leaving 100 @ ₦5.00 + 10 @ ₦6.00 (₦560).\nStep 4: 20 Jan issue 10 units from the latest layer remaining, the ₦6.00 units, so X = ₦6.00, Y = ₦60 and Z = ₦500: D.\nThe trap: A (₦4.00) is the last purchase price, but those units were already issued on 15 January. Memory tip: in LIFO, use the latest layer still in stock.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPurchases ledger opening balances ... ₦4,000\nSales ledger opening balance ... ₦6,000\nCredit purchases during the year ... ₦25,000\nDiscounts allowed ... ₦1,000\nReturns inwards ... ₦2,000\nCredit sales during the year ... ₦10,000\nReturns outwards ... ₦6,000\n\nCalculate the sales ledger balance.|₦3,000|₦6,000|₦10,000|₦13,000": "The sales ledger (debtors) control account is debited with what customers owe and credited with what reduces it.\nStep 1: Opening balance ₦6,000 + credit sales ₦10,000 = ₦16,000.\nStep 2: Less discounts allowed ₦1,000 and returns inwards ₦2,000 = ₦3,000.\nStep 3: Closing balance = ₦16,000 − ₦3,000 = ₦13,000: D.\nNo cash received from debtors is given, so none is deducted. Purchases figures belong to the other control account. Memory tip: Debtors go Up with credit sales; Down with cash, discount allowed and returns inwards.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPurchases ledger opening balances ... ₦4,000\nSales ledger opening balance ... ₦6,000\nCredit purchases during the year ... ₦25,000\nDiscounts allowed ... ₦1,000\nReturns inwards ... ₦2,000\nCredit sales during the year ... ₦10,000\nReturns outwards ... ₦6,000\n\nWhat is the purchases ledger balance?|₦4,000|₦23,000|₦24,000|₦29,000": "The purchases ledger (creditors) control account is credited with amounts owed to suppliers and debited with what reduces them.\nStep 1: Opening balance ₦4,000 + credit purchases ₦25,000 = ₦29,000.\nStep 2: Less returns outwards ₦6,000 = ₦23,000: B.\nDiscounts allowed and returns inwards concern customers, not suppliers, so they are ignored here. The trap: D (₦29,000) forgets the returns outwards. Memory tip: returns OUTwards reduce what we OWE.",
 "|For an incomplete record to provide necessary information, it must be converted to|complete records|double entry records|statement of affairs|single entry": "Incomplete records (single entry) are kept by many small traders: usually only a cash book and lists of debtors and creditors. To produce full, reliable information such as a trading, profit and loss account and a balance sheet, the records must be converted to double entry, by building up the missing accounts (control accounts, total purchases and sales) from the available data: B. A statement of affairs (C) is only a list of assets and liabilities used to find capital. Single entry (D) is the incomplete system itself. Memory tip: Incomplete records become Complete by Converting to double entry.",
 "|Use the following information: \n(31/12/98 ... 31/12/99)\nAssets:\nPlant & Machinery ... ₦1,500 ... ₦1,200\nFixtures ... ₦700 ... ₦520\nStock ... ₦500 ... ₦600\nDebtors ... ₦900 ... ₦400\nCash ... ₦200 ... ₦300\nLiabilities:\nCreditors ... ₦500 ... ₦600\nLoan ... ₦600 ... ₦400\n\nDetermine the total fixed asset from the opening balance sheet.|₦2,200|₦2,020|₦1,720|₦1,270": "The opening balance sheet is the earlier date, 31/12/98. Fixed assets are the long-term assets used in the business: plant and machinery and fixtures.\nStep 1: Plant and machinery ₦1,500 + fixtures ₦700 = ₦2,200: A.\nStock, debtors and cash are current assets, and the 31/12/99 figures (₦1,200 + ₦520 = ₦1,720, option C) belong to the closing balance sheet.\nThe trap: C (₦1,720) uses the closing year's figures. Memory tip: opening = the earlier date column.",
 "|Use the following information: \n(31/12/98 ... 31/12/99)\nAssets:\nPlant & Machinery ... ₦1,500 ... ₦1,200\nFixtures ... ₦700 ... ₦520\nStock ... ₦500 ... ₦600\nDebtors ... ₦900 ... ₦400\nCash ... ₦200 ... ₦300\nLiabilities:\nCreditors ... ₦500 ... ₦600\nLoan ... ₦600 ... ₦400\n\nWhat is the capital from the opening balance sheet?|₦3,600|₦2,700|₦2,070|₦1,520": "Capital (opening) = assets − liabilities at 31/12/98.\nStep 1: Assets = ₦1,500 + ₦700 + ₦500 + ₦900 + ₦200 = ₦3,800.\nStep 2: Liabilities = creditors ₦500 + loan ₦600 = ₦1,100.\nStep 3: Capital = ₦3,800 − ₦1,100 = ₦2,700: B.\nThis is a statement of affairs, the first step in finding profit from incomplete records (closing capital − opening capital + drawings − capital introduced). Memory tip: Capital = what you own minus what you owe.",
 "|Given:\nDirect material ... ₦2,500\nDirect labour ... ₦5,000\nDirect expenses ... ₦1,000\nOverhead expenses ... ₦1,500\n\nFrom the data above, compute the prime cost.|₦8,500|₦7,500|₦6,500|₦6,000": "Prime cost is the total of all direct costs, those traced straight to the product.\nStep 1: Direct material ₦2,500 + direct labour ₦5,000 + direct expenses ₦1,000 = ₦8,500: A.\nStep 2: Overheads (₦1,500) are added after prime cost to give production (factory) cost of ₦10,000.\nThe trap: B (₦7,500) leaves out direct expenses, which are part of prime cost. Memory tip: Prime = all the Directs (materials, labour, expenses); Production = Prime + factory overheads.",
 "|Use the following information: \nBar opening stock ... ₦10,000\nAmount owed to bar suppliers at the beginning of the year ... ₦10,000\nBar sales ... ₦50,000\nBar credit purchases ... ₦40,000\nPayment to suppliers ... ₦30,000\nBar expenses ... ₦1,000\nBar closing stock ... ₦15,000\n\nWhat is the bar profit?|₦20,000|₦15,000|₦14,000|₦10,000": "A club's bar is run like a small shop, so it has a bar trading account.\nStep 1: Cost of sales = opening stock ₦10,000 + purchases ₦40,000 − closing stock ₦15,000 = ₦35,000.\nStep 2: Gross bar profit = sales ₦50,000 − ₦35,000 = ₦15,000.\nStep 3: Less the bar's own expenses ₦1,000 = bar profit ₦14,000: C, transferred to the income and expenditure account.\nSome readers stop at the gross figure, which is why this item is debatable. The trap: B (₦15,000) is the gross profit before bar expenses. Memory tip: bar profit = bar sales − bar costs − bar expenses.",
 "|Use the following information: \nBar opening stock ... ₦10,000\nAmount owed to bar suppliers at the beginning of the year ... ₦10,000\nBar sales ... ₦50,000\nBar credit purchases ... ₦40,000\nPayment to suppliers ... ₦30,000\nBar expenses ... ₦1,000\nBar closing stock ... ₦15,000\n\nHow much is owed to suppliers?|₦50,000|₦40,000|₦30,000|₦20,000": "Build the bar creditors (suppliers) account.\nStep 1: Owed at the start ₦10,000 + credit purchases ₦40,000 = ₦50,000 owed in total during the year.\nStep 2: Less payments ₦30,000 = ₦20,000 still owed: D.\nThis ₦20,000 is a current liability in the club's balance sheet. The trap: B (₦40,000) is the purchases figure alone. Memory tip: Creditors = Opening + Credit purchases − Payments.",
 "|Lubricating oil and spare parts of machinery are examples of|direct material|indirect materials|indirect expenses|direct expenses": "In cost accounting, materials are direct when they become part of the product and can be traced to each unit (wood in a table). Lubricating oil for machines and spare parts for repairs are used up in the factory but cannot be traced to particular units of output, so they are indirect materials: B. Indirect materials are one part of factory overheads, together with indirect labour (supervisors) and indirect expenses (factory rent, power). The trap: C (indirect expenses) catches students because these items are overheads, but the kind of overhead they are is materials. Memory tip: if it is a physical item consumed in the factory but not in the product, it is an indirect material.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscriptions received during the year ... ₦30,000\nSubscription owed last year ... ₦4,000\nSubscription received for next year ... ₦6,000\n\nThe ₦6,000 subscription received is|current asset|current liability|fixed asset|capital": "When members pay subscriptions for next year in advance, the club has received cash for a service it still owes them. Until the next year starts, that money is a liability, shown under current liabilities in the balance sheet as \"subscriptions in advance\": B. Next year it becomes income. The opposite case, subscriptions in arrears (owed by members), is a current asset (A). Memory tip: received in Advance = Accrued liability; owed in Arrears = Asset.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscriptions received during the year ... ₦30,000\nSubscription owed last year ... ₦4,000\nSubscription received for next year ... ₦6,000\n\nWhat is the subscription to be charged to income and expenditure account?|₦36,000|₦34,000|₦30,000|₦20,000": "Subscription income must match the year, not the cash.\nStep 1: Cash received ₦30,000.\nStep 2: Less ₦4,000 that was for last year (arrears now paid) = ₦26,000.\nStep 3: Less ₦6,000 that is for next year (in advance) = ₦20,000: D.\nThe trap: B (₦34,000) adds the ₦4,000 instead of deducting it. Memory tip: cash received − last year's − next year's = this year's.",
 "|A club received rent ₦10,000 and donation ₦30,000. It paid ₦6,000 for entertainment and is still owing ₦16,000. The balance of the receipts and payment account is|₦24,000|₦22,000|₦14,000|₦18,000": "A receipts and payments account is a summary of the club's cash book: it shows only cash actually received and paid, and its balance is cash in hand. Here receipts are rent ₦10,000 + donation ₦30,000 = ₦40,000 and the only payment is ₦6,000, so on a cash basis the balance is ₦34,000, which is not among the options. JAMB's answer, ₦18,000 (D), subtracts the ₦16,000 still owed as well, which would only be right if it had been paid. Amounts owing belong in the income and expenditure account and balance sheet, not in receipts and payments. Memory tip: Receipts and Payments = Real cash only.",
 "|Given:\nClub trading profit ... ₦12,000\nMembers' subscription ... ₦15,000\nProfit on sale of fixed assets ... ₦2,500\nLoss on sale of investment ... ₦1,000\nWages ... ₦16,000\nOther expenses ... ₦10,000\n\nDetermine the club's excess of income over expenditure.|₦500|₦1,500|₦2,000|₦2,500": "The income and expenditure account matches the club's income and expenses for the year.\nStep 1: Income = trading profit ₦12,000 + subscriptions ₦15,000 + profit on sale of fixed assets ₦2,500 = ₦29,500.\nStep 2: Expenditure = loss on sale of investment ₦1,000 + wages ₦16,000 + other expenses ₦10,000 = ₦27,000.\nStep 3: Excess of income over expenditure = ₦29,500 − ₦27,000 = ₦2,500: D.\nPut each item on the right side: profits on disposals are income, losses are expenditure. Memory tip: gains go up, losses go down.",
 "|The excess of income over expenditure is usually transferred to the|accumulated fund|profit and loss account|current assets in the balance sheet|current liabilities in the balance sheet": "A non-profit organisation such as a club has no owner's capital; its capital is called the accumulated fund. A surplus (excess of income over expenditure) is added to the accumulated fund, and a deficit is deducted from it, in the balance sheet: A. A profit and loss account (B) belongs to trading businesses. The accumulated fund itself equals the club's total assets minus its liabilities. Memory tip: Clubs Accumulate; traders Profit.",
 "|Partner's salaries and drawings are usually posted to the|trading account|current account|capital account|partners' account": "In a partnership with fixed capital accounts, the capital account holds only the capital introduced. Everything that changes from year to year, salaries, interest on capital, share of profit (credits) and drawings and interest on drawings (debits), goes to each partner's current account: B. Partners' salaries are an appropriation of profit, not an expense, so they never go to the trading account (A). The trap: C (capital account) is right only where capitals are fluctuating, but the usual arrangement uses current accounts. Memory tip: Capital stays Constant; Current account takes the Changes.",
 "|Marmuhu and Yusuf are in partnership sharing profits and losses in the ratio 2 : 1. On 31/3/2000, the partnership decided to admit Idris who is to take ¼ of future profits without changing the ratio of Marmuhu and Yusuf. What is the new profit-sharing ratio of Marmuhu and Yusuf?|50% : 25%|50% : 20%|25% : 50%|25% : 25%": "Idris takes ¼, leaving ¾ for the old partners, who keep their 2 : 1 ratio.\nStep 1: Marmuhu = ⅔ × ¾ = ½ = 50%.\nStep 2: Yusuf = ⅓ × ¾ = ¼ = 25%.\nStep 3: Check: 50% + 25% + Idris 25% = 100%.\nSo the new shares are 50% : 25%: A (overall ratio 2 : 1 : 1).\nMemory tip: new partner's share first, then split the rest in the old ratio.",
 "|If a partner pays a premium of ₦500 for 1/5 share of profit, then the total value of goodwill is|₦500|₦2,000|₦2,500|₦3,000": "A premium paid by an incoming partner is payment for his share of the goodwill the old partners built.\nStep 1: Premium ₦500 buys 1/5 of goodwill.\nStep 2: Total goodwill = ₦500 × 5 = ₦2,500: C.\nThe trap: B (₦2,000) is the old partners' 4/5 share (₦2,500 − ₦500), not the whole goodwill. Memory tip: premium ÷ share bought = whole goodwill.",
 "|On the dissolution of a partnership business, the net book value of the assets is transferred to|debit of realization account|credit of realization account|debit of bank account|credit of bank account": "On dissolution, a realisation account collects all the assets to be sold. Each asset account is credited (closed) and the realisation account is debited with the book value; sale proceeds are then credited to realisation, and the difference is the profit or loss shared by partners: A. The trap: B (credit of realisation) is where the sale proceeds go, not the book values. Memory tip: assets IN at book value on the debit; cash OUT from sales on the credit.",
 "|Bala Ltd acquired the business of Bello Ltd and caused the separate existence of the latter company to terminate. This situation is best described as|absorption|merger|conversion|dissolution": "Business combinations take different forms. In an absorption, an existing company takes over another existing company, and the company taken over goes into liquidation and ceases to exist: A. In an amalgamation or merger (B), two or more companies combine, often forming a new company. Conversion (C) means changing a business's form, e.g. a partnership into a company. Dissolution (D) is closing a business, not acquiring one. The trap: B (merger) is closely related, but a merger joins companies as equals rather than one swallowing the other. Memory tip: Absorb = one company soaks up the other, which disappears.",
 "|Where a company acquires controlling shares of another and the consideration is paid in cash, the entries in the books of the purchasers are debit|investment and credit cash|investment and credit shares|purchases and credit cash|purchases and credit shares": "When a company buys controlling shares in another company, it acquires an asset: an investment in a subsidiary. Paying cash reduces its bank balance. The entry is therefore Debit Investment account, Credit Cash (bank): A. If it paid with its own shares instead, it would credit share capital (B). \"Purchases\" (C, D) is used for goods bought for resale, not for shares; this item is debatable because some texts speak of a \"purchase of business\" account. Memory tip: buy shares with cash = Dr Investment, Cr Cash.",
 "|Given:\nCapital ... ₦200,000\nLiabilities ... ₦10,000\nTotal ... ₦210,000\nTotal assets ... ₦210,000\n\nIf the business is purchased at a price including a goodwill of ₦20,000, what must have been the purchase price?|₦190,000|₦210,000|₦220,000|₦230,000": "Net assets are what the business is worth on its books: total assets minus liabilities.\nStep 1: Net assets = ₦210,000 − ₦10,000 = ₦200,000 (equal to capital).\nStep 2: Purchase price = net assets + goodwill = ₦200,000 + ₦20,000 = ₦220,000: C.\nThe trap: D (₦230,000) adds goodwill to total assets, forgetting that the buyer takes over the liabilities too. Memory tip: price = net assets + goodwill.",
 "|The main difference between the ordinary and preference shareholders is that|the former receive dividends while the latter do not|the latter are not members of the company while the former are|in the case of winding up, the former are paid first before the latter|the former have voting rights while the latter do not": "Ordinary shareholders are the true owners: they bear most of the risk, receive whatever dividend the directors declare after preference dividends, and have voting rights at general meetings. Preference shareholders receive a fixed dividend before ordinary shareholders and are repaid first on winding up, but normally cannot vote except when their dividend is in arrears: D. A is false (both can receive dividends), B is false (both are members), and C reverses the order of repayment. Memory tip: Ordinary = Owners who vote; Preference = Priority but no voice.",
 "|Use the following information: \nAuthorized capital:\n100,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each\nIssued and fully paid:\n50,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each ... ₦50,000\n10,000 8% preference shares of ₦1 each ... ₦10,000\nReserves ... ₦25,000\nCreditors ... ₦15,000\nDebtors ... ₦13,000\nCash in hand ... ₦5,000\n\nDetermine the net current assets.|₦43,000|₦28,000|₦13,000|₦3,000": "Net current assets (working capital) = current assets − current liabilities.\nStep 1: Current assets = debtors ₦13,000 + cash ₦5,000 = ₦18,000.\nStep 2: Current liabilities = creditors ₦15,000.\nStep 3: Net current assets = ₦18,000 − ₦15,000 = ₦3,000: D.\nShare capital and reserves are part of equity, not current items. Memory tip: working capital = what turns into cash soon minus what must be paid soon.",
 "|Use the following information: \nAuthorized capital:\n100,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each\nIssued and fully paid:\n50,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each ... ₦50,000\n10,000 8% preference shares of ₦1 each ... ₦10,000\nReserves ... ₦25,000\nCreditors ... ₦15,000\nDebtors ... ₦13,000\nCash in hand ... ₦5,000\n\nCalculate the shareholder's fund.|₦60,000|₦75,000|₦85,000|₦185,000": "Shareholders' funds are all the capital belonging to the shareholders: issued share capital plus reserves.\nStep 1: Ordinary shares ₦50,000 + preference shares ₦10,000 = ₦60,000.\nStep 2: Add reserves ₦25,000 = ₦85,000: C.\nAuthorised capital is not included; only issued capital counts. Some books use \"shareholders' fund\" to mean only the ordinary shareholders' part (₦75,000), so this item is debatable. The trap: B (₦75,000) leaves out the preference shares, and A (₦60,000) leaves out reserves. Memory tip: shareholders' funds = issued capital + reserves.",
 "|The law that currently regulates the registration of companies in Nigeria is the|Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, 1999|Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree, 1972|Companies and Allied Matters Decree, 1990|Companies Decree, 1968": "When this paper was set (2001), the registration, management and winding up of companies in Nigeria was governed by the Companies and Allied Matters Decree (Act) of 1990, which also set up the Corporate Affairs Commission: C. It replaced the Companies Decree 1968 (D). The Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree 1972 (B) was the indigenisation law about foreign ownership. Today the law is the Companies and Allied Matters Act 2020, which repealed CAMA 1990. The trap: D (Companies Decree 1968) was the law before 1990. Memory tip: CAC registers companies under CAMA (1990, now 2020).",
 "|X and Y are two departments that are to share 50% of all joint costs equally and the balance in the ratio 2 : 1. If a sum of ₦150,000 is incurred jointly, what will be the portion attributable to X?|₦37,500|₦62,500|₦87,500|₦75,000": "Split the joint cost into its two parts.\nStep 1: 50% of ₦150,000 = ₦75,000, shared equally: X gets ₦37,500.\nStep 2: The balance ₦75,000 is shared 2 : 1: X gets ⅔ × ₦75,000 = ₦50,000.\nStep 3: X's total = ₦37,500 + ₦50,000 = ₦87,500: C.\nCheck: Y gets ₦37,500 + ₦25,000 = ₦62,500, and ₦87,500 + ₦62,500 = ₦150,000.\nThe trap: B (₦62,500) is Y's share, and A (₦37,500) is only X's equal part. Memory tip: do each part of the sharing rule separately, then add.",
 "|Department F transferred some goods to department G at a selling price. The goods were not sold at the end of the accounting period. Which account is affected at the time of preparing a combined balance sheet?|Creditors|Debtors|Stock|Suspense": "When one department transfers goods to another at selling price, the profit included is only realised when the goods are sold to outsiders. If the goods are still unsold at the year end, the receiving department's closing stock includes that unrealised profit. In the combined accounts, a provision for unrealised profit is deducted from stock, so stock is the account affected: C. Debtors and creditors (A, B) are not involved because the transfer is within the same business. Memory tip: unsold inter-department goods = cut the profit out of Stock.",
 "|The correct entry to reflect the receipt of cash sent by a branch to a head office is|debit cash and credit branch current account|debit branch current account and credit cash|credit branch debtors and debit cash|credit branch current account and debit branch debtors": "In head office books, the branch current account shows how much the branch owes head office. When the branch sends cash to head office, head office's cash rises, so Debit Cash, and the branch now owes less, so Credit Branch Current Account: A. Option B is the reverse entry, used when head office sends cash to the branch. The trap: B reverses the entry, a common slip. Memory tip: money arriving at head office: Cash up (Dr), branch debt down (Cr).",
 "|To account for expenses paid by head office on behalf of the branch, the branch should|debit head office account and credit cash|debit profit and loss account and credit head office account|credit cash and debit profit and loss account|credit profit and loss account and debit head office account": "In the branch's books, the head office account shows what the branch owes head office. If head office pays an expense for the branch, the expense belongs to the branch, so it is debited to the branch's profit and loss (expense) account, and because head office paid, the branch now owes it more: credit Head Office account: B. The branch paid no cash itself, so options involving cash (A, C) are wrong. Memory tip: someone paid for you = Dr expense, Cr the payer.",
 "|The officer responsible for ascertaining whether all public expenditure and appropriation are in line with approved guidelines is the|Accountant General|Auditor General|Finance Minister|Permanent Secretary": "In Nigerian public sector accounting, the Accountant-General (A) prepares and keeps the government's accounts and controls the treasury. The Auditor-General is the independent officer who audits those accounts and reports to the National Assembly on whether expenditure was properly authorised and spent in line with the Appropriation Act and financial regulations: B. Permanent Secretaries are accounting officers of their ministries, and the Finance Minister directs fiscal policy. The trap: A (Accountant General) is the officer most often confused with the Auditor-General. Memory tip: Accountant-General Accounts; Auditor-General Audits.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPTF Trial Balance [Extract] as at 31 December 1999 (Dr / Cr, ₦'000)\nCash ... Dr 2,000\nInvestments ... Dr 3,000\nAccounts receivable ... Dr 6,000\nFund balance ... Cr 11,000\nTotals ... 11,000 ... 11,000\n\nAssuming all the investments realized is ₦4m, what will be the ending fund balance?|₦8m|₦11m|₦12m|₦16m": "Figures are in ₦'000 (₦m). The fund balance changes only by gains and losses.\nStep 1: Investments in the books ₦3m are realised for ₦4m: gain ₦1m.\nStep 2: Ending fund balance = ₦11m + ₦1m = ₦12m: C.\nCheck: assets become cash ₦2m + ₦4m = ₦6m plus receivables ₦6m = ₦12m.\nThe trap: B (₦11m) ignores the gain on realisation. Memory tip: fund balance = assets at their new values.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPTF Trial Balance [Extract] as at 31 December 1999 (Dr / Cr, ₦'000)\nCash ... Dr 2,000\nInvestments ... Dr 3,000\nAccounts receivable ... Dr 6,000\nFund balance ... Cr 11,000\nTotals ... 11,000 ... 11,000\n\nIf only 1/3 of the investment is sold for ₦2m and ₦5m realized from the accounts receivable, what will be the balance of the fund?|₦9m|₦11m|₦13m|₦16m": "Step 1: One-third of investments = ⅓ × ₦3m = ₦1m book value, sold for ₦2m: gain ₦1m.\nStep 2: Receivables of ₦6m realise ₦5m: loss ₦1m.\nStep 3: Fund balance = ₦11m + ₦1m − ₦1m = ₦11m: B.\nCheck: cash ₦2m + ₦2m + ₦5m = ₦9m, plus remaining investments ₦2m = ₦11m.\nThe trap: A (₦9m) is the cash total alone, forgetting the ₦2m of investments still held. Memory tip: add every gain, subtract every loss, from the fund.",
 "|In preparing the final account, the bad debt account is closed by a transfer to the|Balance sheet|Provision for bad debt|Profit and loss account|Trading account": "Bad debts are amounts owed by customers that will never be collected. They are a loss, like any expense: they are collected in a bad debts account during the year, and at the year end that account is closed by transferring the total to the debit of the profit and loss account: C. The trading account only deals with sales and cost of goods sold, and the balance sheet shows debtors after the loss has been taken off. The trap: the provision for bad debt (B) sounds right, and some firms do write bad debts off against it, but the standard closing entry for the bad debts account is to profit and loss. Memory tip: losses and expenses go to P&L; only buying and selling goes to Trading.",
 "|Given: \nStock (1/4/94) ... ₦800\nPurchases of flour ... ₦2,450\nSales ... ₦4,745\nWages of bakery staff ... ₦675\nCarriage outwards ... ₦50\nSalary of administrative staff ... ₦225\nStock (31/3/95) ... ₦940\nCapital ... ₦1,540\n\nDetermine the cost of goods sold.|₦2,575|₦2,985|₦3,250|₦3,925": "This is a bakery, so the wages of the bakery staff are part of the cost of making the bread that was sold. Step 1: opening stock ₦800 + purchases of flour ₦2,450 = ₦3,250. Step 2: add bakery wages ₦675 = ₦3,925. Step 3: less closing stock ₦940 = ₦2,985: B. Carriage outwards and administrative salaries are selling and office expenses for the profit and loss account, and sales and capital are not costs at all. The trap: ₦3,925 (D) is what you get if you forget to deduct the closing stock. Memory tip: COGS = Opening stock + Purchases + Direct costs − Closing stock.",
 "|In a petty cash book the imprest is ₦1,380.\nExpenses:\nStationery ... ₦350\nCleaning material ... ₦335\nGeneral expenses ... ₦265\n\nHow much was received to maintain the imprest at the end of the month?|₦950|₦970|₦430|₦380": "Under the imprest system the petty cashier starts each period with a fixed float (here ₦1,380). At the end of the period the main cashier reimburses exactly what has been spent, which brings the float back to the imprest. Step 1: spending = stationery ₦350 + cleaning material ₦335 + general expenses ₦265 = ₦950. Step 2: cash left = ₦1,380 − ₦950 = ₦430. Step 3: amount received = ₦950, restoring ₦430 + ₦950 = ₦1,380: A. The trap: ₦430 (C) is the cash still in hand, not the amount needed to top up. Memory tip: imprest top-up = what was spent.",
 "|Income statement for the year ended June 30, 2001:\nOffice rent ... ₦20,000\nNEPA (light) ... ₦15,000\nSalary of staff ... ₦120,000\nMiscellaneous expenses ... ?\nNet income ... ?\nRevenue ... ₦820,000\nMiscellaneous expenses is 10% of revenue.\n\nCalculate the net income.|₦583,000|₦563,000|₦483,000|₦683,000": "Net income is revenue less all expenses. Step 1: miscellaneous expenses = 10% × ₦820,000 = ₦82,000. Step 2: total expenses = office rent ₦20,000 + NEPA (light) ₦15,000 + salary ₦120,000 + miscellaneous ₦82,000 = ₦237,000. Step 3: net income = ₦820,000 − ₦237,000 = ₦583,000: A. NEPA, the old electricity authority, was later renamed PHCN and its business passed to private distribution companies in 2013, but electricity is still an ordinary expense. Memory tip: find the missing expense first, then Revenue − Expenses = Net income.",
 "|An entry in a subsidiary book which does not form part of the double entry system is a|contra entry|journal entry|single entry|memorandum entry": "A memorandum entry is a note made in a book for information only; it is not posted to any ledger account, so it does not form part of the double entry. Examples are trade discounts shown on an invoice, or the memorandum columns for discount in the cash book before their totals are posted. A contra entry and a journal entry both lead to debits and credits in the ledger, and \"single entry\" is a whole system of incomplete records, not one entry: D. Memory tip: memorandum = memory note, never posted.",
 "|In an incomplete record, the preparation of the bank reconciliation ensures that|all cash takings are banked|the cash book is correct|there are no overdrafts|there are no hidden loans": "In incomplete records the trader usually keeps no proper cash book; much of the cash and bank picture has to be rebuilt from the bank statement, paying-in slips and a record of takings. Comparing the bank statement with the record of cash takings and payments (a bank reconciliation) shows whether all the takings were actually banked, and brings to light takings used for drawings or expenses before banking: A. In full double-entry books a reconciliation mainly checks that the cash book is correct (B), but with incomplete records there is often no proper cash book to check; the point is to account for every naira of takings. Memory tip: incomplete records: reconcile takings with bankings.",
 "|I. Fixtures account; II. Machinery account; III. Wages account; IV. Rent account. Which of the above are nominal accounts?|III and IV|I and II|I and III|II and III": "Accounts are classified as personal (people and firms), real (assets such as fixtures, machinery, buildings) and nominal (expenses, losses, incomes and gains). Wages and rent are expenses, so they are nominal accounts, and their balances are closed to the profit and loss account each year: A. Fixtures and machinery are fixed assets, so they are real accounts that are carried forward in the balance sheet. Memory tip: Nominal = Name of an expense or income; Real = things you can touch.",
 "|NAFARA & SONS balance sheet items as at 31st December, 1987:\nCapital ... ₦74,480\nCreditors ... ₦15,875\nPrepaid expenses ... ₦580\nMotor vehicles ... ₦34,750\nFurniture ... ₦35,850\nAccrued expenses ... ₦645\nStock ... ₦8,100\nCash balances ... ₦9,911\nDebtors ... ₦1,809\n\nCompute the value of current assets.|₦20,400|₦20,465|₦35,695|₦19,820": "Current assets are assets that will turn into cash within a year. Step 1: pick them out: prepaid expenses ₦580, stock ₦8,100, cash balances ₦9,911, debtors ₦1,809. Step 2: add them: ₦580 + ₦8,100 = ₦8,680; + ₦9,911 = ₦18,591; + ₦1,809 = ₦20,400: A. Motor vehicles and furniture are fixed assets; creditors and accrued expenses are current liabilities; capital is the owner's interest. The trap: ₦20,465 (B) is what you get by adding accrued expenses (₦645) instead of prepaid expenses (₦580). Today the balance sheet is called the statement of financial position and stock is called inventory. Memory tip: prepaid = asset; accrued = liability.",
 "|Use the following information: \nThe assets and liabilities of Udo Co. Ltd as at 31st December, 2000:\nCreditors ... ₦50,000\nDebtors ... ₦46,000\nLoan ... ₦100,000\nStock ... ₦20,000\nMotor vehicle ... ₦120,000\nBuilding ... ₦70,000\nAccrued wages ... ₦30,000\nBank ... ₦25,000\n\nThe value of capital invested by the owners is|₦110,000|₦105,000|₦101,000|₦100,000": "The accounting equation is Capital = Assets − Liabilities. Step 1: assets = debtors ₦46,000 + stock ₦20,000 + motor vehicle ₦120,000 + building ₦70,000 + bank ₦25,000 = ₦281,000. Step 2: liabilities = creditors ₦50,000 + loan ₦100,000 + accrued wages ₦30,000 = ₦180,000. Step 3: capital = ₦281,000 − ₦180,000 = ₦101,000: C. Memory tip: A = C + L, so C = A − L.",
 "|Use the following information: \nThe assets and liabilities of Udo Co. Ltd as at 31st December, 2000:\nCreditors ... ₦50,000\nDebtors ... ₦46,000\nLoan ... ₦100,000\nStock ... ₦20,000\nMotor vehicle ... ₦120,000\nBuilding ... ₦70,000\nAccrued wages ... ₦30,000\nBank ... ₦25,000\n\nThe liabilities of Udo Co. Ltd is|₦180,000|₦181,000|₦177,000|₦110,000": "Liabilities are amounts the business owes to outsiders. Udo Co. Ltd owes its creditors ₦50,000, the lender ₦100,000 and its workers ₦30,000 of wages earned but unpaid. Step 1: ₦50,000 + ₦100,000 = ₦150,000. Step 2: ₦150,000 + ₦30,000 = ₦180,000: A. Debtors, stock, motor vehicle, building and bank are assets. Check: assets ₦281,000 = liabilities ₦180,000 + capital ₦101,000. Memory tip: owe = liability; own = asset.",
 "|The normal accounting entry to record the dishonour of a cheque by a businessman is to|debit cash book and credit suspense account|debit cash book and credit drawer|credit cash book and debit suspense account|credit cash book and debit drawer": "When a customer pays by cheque, the business debits the bank column of the cash book and credits the customer (the drawer). If the bank later returns the cheque as dishonoured, no money was received, so that entry is reversed: credit the cash book (bank column) and debit the drawer's account, showing that he owes the money again: D. A suspense account is only for unexplained differences in the trial balance. The trap: B (debit cash book, credit drawer) is the entry for receiving the cheque in the first place, not for its dishonour. Memory tip: dishonoured cheque = reverse the receipt.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStatement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31st December, 2001 (₦m):\nShareholders' interest ... 240\nCurrent liabilities ... 20\nCurrent assets ... 110\nFixed assets ... 140\n\nWhat is the net working capital?|₦90m|₦70m|₦100m|₦80m": "Net working capital (net current assets) measures how far current assets cover short-term debts. Net working capital = current assets − current liabilities = ₦110m − ₦20m = ₦90m: A. Shareholders' interest and fixed assets are long-term items and do not enter the calculation. A positive figure means the company can pay its short-term debts from its current assets and still have something left to trade with. Memory tip: working capital = CA − CL.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStatement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31st December, 2001 (₦m):\nShareholders' interest ... 240\nCurrent liabilities ... 20\nCurrent assets ... 110\nFixed assets ... 140\n\nDetermine the value of the trade investment.|₦8m|₦10m|₦15m|₦20m": "The statement must balance: what the company uses (assets) equals where the money came from (shareholders' interest plus liabilities). Step 1: sources = shareholders' interest ₦240m + current liabilities ₦20m = ₦260m. Step 2: assets listed = fixed assets ₦140m + current assets ₦110m = ₦250m. Step 3: the missing asset, the trade investment, = ₦260m − ₦250m = ₦10m: B. Memory tip: when one item is missing from a balance sheet, it is the balancing figure.",
 "|The concise statement used to explain entries in the general journal is known as|narration|summary|information|commentary": "Every entry in the general journal shows the date, the account debited, the account credited, the amounts and a short explanation of why the entry was made. That brief explanation, written under the entry, is called the narration: A. It lets anyone reading the journal later understand the transaction without hunting for the source document. Memory tip: journal = Date, Debit, Credit, Narration.",
 "|Use the following information: \nErero's Trading Account for the month ended 31/05/2001:\nOpening stock ... ₦45,000\nPurchases ₦110,000 less returns ₦6,000 ... ₦104,000\n(Opening stock + net purchases ... ₦149,000)\nLess closing stock ... ???\nCost of sales ... ???\nGross profit ... ???\nTotal ... ₦157,000\nSales ₦161,000 less returns ₦4,000 ... ₦157,000\nThe gross profit ratio for the company is 25%.\n\nIf the total expenses is ₦20,845, what will be the net profit for the company during the month?|₦19,149|₦16,168|₦21,655|₦18,405": "Gross profit ratio = gross profit ÷ net sales. Step 1: net sales = ₦161,000 − ₦4,000 = ₦157,000. Step 2: gross profit = 25% × ₦157,000 = ₦39,250. Step 3: net profit = gross profit − total expenses = ₦39,250 − ₦20,845 = ₦18,405: D. Memory tip: GP from the ratio first, then GP − Expenses = NP.",
 "|Use the following information: \nErero's Trading Account for the month ended 31/05/2001:\nOpening stock ... ₦45,000\nPurchases ₦110,000 less returns ₦6,000 ... ₦104,000\n(Opening stock + net purchases ... ₦149,000)\nLess closing stock ... ???\nCost of sales ... ???\nGross profit ... ???\nTotal ... ₦157,000\nSales ₦161,000 less returns ₦4,000 ... ₦157,000\nThe gross profit ratio for the company is 25%.\n\nThe closing stock for this company is|₦72,000|₦45,000|₦42,500|₦31,250": "Step 1: net sales = ₦157,000, so gross profit at 25% = ₦39,250. Step 2: cost of sales = ₦157,000 − ₦39,250 = ₦117,750. Step 3: goods available = opening stock ₦45,000 + net purchases (₦110,000 − ₦6,000 = ₦104,000) = ₦149,000. Step 4: closing stock = ₦149,000 − ₦117,750 = ₦31,250: D. Check: ₦117,750 + ₦39,250 = ₦157,000, which matches the total on the account. Memory tip: closing stock = goods available − cost of sales.",
 "|In a departmental accounting system, which of the following expenses will most likely be apportioned on the basis of turnover?|Carriage outwards|Carriage inwards|Discounts received|Returns outwards": "In departmental accounts each expense is shared on the basis that best reflects what causes it. Carriage outwards is the cost of delivering goods to customers, so it varies with sales and is apportioned in the ratio of departmental turnover: A. Carriage inwards is linked to buying, so it is shared on purchases; discounts received and returns outwards also relate to purchases. The trap: carriage inwards (B) looks almost the same, but \"inwards\" goes with purchases, not sales. Memory tip: OUTwards follows goods going OUT (sales); INwards follows goods coming IN (purchases).",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock of raw materials (1st January) ... ₦3,000\nDirect wages ... ₦2,500\nDirect expenses ... ₦1,000\nFactory overheads ... ₦2,000\nCost of raw materials used ... ₦5,500\nStock of finished goods (31st December) ... ₦2,000\n\nDetermine the prime cost.|₦7,500|₦6,500|₦9,000|₦8,000": "Prime cost is the total of all direct costs of production. Step 1: direct materials used = ₦5,500 (the opening stock of ₦3,000 is already inside this figure). Step 2: add direct wages ₦2,500 = ₦8,000. Step 3: add direct expenses ₦1,000 = ₦9,000: C. Factory overheads come after prime cost, and finished goods stock belongs to the trading account. The trap: ₦8,000 (D) is the answer if you forget direct expenses. Memory tip: Prime cost = direct Materials + direct Labour + direct Expenses.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock of raw materials (1st January) ... ₦3,000\nDirect wages ... ₦2,500\nDirect expenses ... ₦1,000\nFactory overheads ... ₦2,000\nCost of raw materials used ... ₦5,500\nStock of finished goods (31st December) ... ₦2,000\n\nThe cost of goods manufactured is|₦11,000|₦12,000|₦14,000|₦9,000": "The cost of goods manufactured (production cost) is prime cost plus factory overheads. Step 1: prime cost = ₦5,500 + ₦2,500 + ₦1,000 = ₦9,000. Step 2: add factory overheads ₦2,000 = ₦11,000: A. No work-in-progress is given, and the stock of finished goods is used in the trading account, not the manufacturing account. The trap: ₦9,000 (D) is only the prime cost; the question asks for the full factory cost. Memory tip: Prime cost + Factory overheads = Cost of production.",
 "|I. Orientation; II. Entity; III. Legal status; IV. Finance. Which of the characteristics above distinguishes a profit-making from a not-for-profit-making organization?|I, II, III and IV|I, II and III|II and III|I and II": "A profit-making business (sole trader, partnership or company) is oriented to making profit for its owners, while a club, society or charity aims to serve its members or the public. They are also different kinds of entity and have different legal status: a business is a registered firm or a company under company law, while a club is an unincorporated association or a registered trust. These three features set them apart: B. Both kinds of organisation need finance, although they raise it differently, so finance is not treated as the distinguishing feature here. Memory tip: profit-making vs not-for-profit: aim, entity, legal form.",
 "|Use the following information: \nAdex Ltd issues stock to its retail branch at cost price. The following particulars relate to Ede branch:\nStock at branch 1st January at cost ... ₦400\nGoods sent to branch at cost ... ₦8,000\nReturns to head office ... ₦340\nCash sales ... ₦9,160\nStock at branch 31st December at cost ... ₦720\n\nWhat is the gross profit carried to the profit and loss account?|₦1,820|₦1,640|₦1,530|₦1,870": "Goods go to the Ede branch at cost, so the branch trading account works like an ordinary one. Step 1: net goods received = ₦8,000 − returns ₦340 = ₦7,660. Step 2: add opening stock ₦400 = ₦8,060. Step 3: less closing stock ₦720 = cost of goods sold ₦7,340. Step 4: gross profit = cash sales ₦9,160 − ₦7,340 = ₦1,820: A. Memory tip: branch GP = Sales − (Opening + Goods sent − Returns − Closing).",
 "|Use the following information: \nAdex Ltd issues stock to its retail branch at cost price. The following particulars relate to Ede branch:\nStock at branch 1st January at cost ... ₦400\nGoods sent to branch at cost ... ₦8,000\nReturns to head office ... ₦340\nCash sales ... ₦9,160\nStock at branch 31st December at cost ... ₦720\n\nCalculate the cost of goods credited to the head office trading account.|₦7,660|₦7,500|₦7,460|₦7,200": "Goods sent to a branch are taken out of head office's own trading account, usually by crediting them to that account (or deducting them from purchases), so that head office is not charged for goods it sent away. Only the net amount the branch kept counts. Step 1: goods sent ₦8,000. Step 2: less returns to head office ₦340 = ₦7,660: A. Memory tip: net goods to branch = sent − returned.",
 "|Nasara Manufacturing Plc has three direct labour employees that work 40 hours each a week for 50 weeks a year. Factory overhead costs of ₦60,000 is distributed on the basis of direct labour hours. Compute the overhead rate.|₦12 per hour|₦16 per hour|₦10 per hour|₦15 per hour": "An overhead absorption rate shares factory overheads over the hours worked. Step 1: direct labour hours = 3 employees × 40 hours × 50 weeks = 6,000 hours. Step 2: rate = ₦60,000 ÷ 6,000 hours = ₦10 per hour: C. Each product is then charged ₦10 of overhead for every direct labour hour spent on it, so a job taking 5 hours absorbs ₦50 of overheads. Memory tip: absorption rate = total overheads ÷ total hours.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts and Payments Account [Extract]\nReceipts: Balance b/d ₦4,000; Subscriptions received ?; Donations received ₦6,000; Refreshment sales ₦10,000\nPayments: Motor vehicle expenses ₦12,000; Insurance ₦3,000; Ambulance expenses ₦12,500; Electricity ₦1,000; Balance c/d ?\nSubscriptions received are always put at 125% of the total donations received and refreshment sales.\n\nWhat is the closing cash balance?|₦11,500|₦12,000|₦13,000|₦13,500": "A receipts and payments account is a summary of the cash book of a club. Step 1: subscriptions = 125% × (₦6,000 + ₦10,000) = ₦20,000. Step 2: total receipts = ₦4,000 + ₦20,000 + ₦6,000 + ₦10,000 = ₦40,000. Step 3: total payments = motor vehicle expenses ₦12,000 + insurance ₦3,000 + ambulance expenses ₦12,500 + electricity ₦1,000 = ₦28,500. Step 4: closing balance = ₦40,000 − ₦28,500 = ₦11,500: A. Memory tip: closing cash = opening cash + receipts − payments.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts and Payments Account [Extract]\nReceipts: Balance b/d ₦4,000; Subscriptions received ?; Donations received ₦6,000; Refreshment sales ₦10,000\nPayments: Motor vehicle expenses ₦12,000; Insurance ₦3,000; Ambulance expenses ₦12,500; Electricity ₦1,000; Balance c/d ?\nSubscriptions received are always put at 125% of the total donations received and refreshment sales.\n\nCompute the subscriptions received.|₦30,000|₦28,000|₦24,000|₦20,000": "The note says subscriptions are 125% of donations plus refreshment sales. Step 1: donations ₦6,000 + refreshment sales ₦10,000 = ₦16,000. Step 2: 125% × ₦16,000 = 1.25 × ₦16,000 = ₦20,000: D. Check: ₦20,000 is ₦4,000 more than ₦16,000, and ₦4,000 is exactly a quarter of ₦16,000. Memory tip: 125% means the base plus a quarter of it: ₦16,000 + ₦4,000.",
 "|Use the following information: \n(Total / Dept. P / Dept. Q)\nSales ... ₦10,000 / ₦6,000 / ₦4,000\nPurchases ... ₦4,000 / ₦1,000 / ₦3,000\nDiscount received ... ₦1,000 / ? / -\nDiscounts allowed ... ₦2,000 / - / ?\nDiscounts (allowed and received) are apportioned to the two departments on the basis of departmental sales and purchases.\n\nDepartment P's share of discount received is|₦750|₦1,000|₦250|₦500": "Discount received comes from paying suppliers, so it is shared in the ratio of departmental purchases. Step 1: total purchases ₦4,000; Department P bought ₦1,000, which is 1/4. Step 2: P's share = 1/4 × ₦1,000 = ₦250: C. Department Q gets the other ₦750. The trap: ₦750 (A) is Department Q's share; it is easy to take the bigger share by mistake. Memory tip: discount received follows purchases; discount allowed follows sales.",
 "|Use the following information: \n(Total / Dept. P / Dept. Q)\nSales ... ₦10,000 / ₦6,000 / ₦4,000\nPurchases ... ₦4,000 / ₦1,000 / ₦3,000\nDiscount received ... ₦1,000 / ? / -\nDiscounts allowed ... ₦2,000 / - / ?\nDiscounts (allowed and received) are apportioned to the two departments on the basis of departmental sales and purchases.\n\nWhat is Department Q's share of discount allowed?|₦2,000|₦1,500|₦800|₦1,200": "Discount allowed is given to customers, so it is shared in the ratio of departmental sales. Step 1: total sales ₦10,000; Department Q sold ₦4,000, which is 4/10. Step 2: Q's share = 4/10 × ₦2,000 = ₦800: C. Department P bears the other ₦1,200. The trap: ₦1,200 (D) is Department P's share, not Q's. Memory tip: allowed to customers, so it is shared like sales.",
 "|Use the following information: \nIncome and Expenditure Account\nExpenditure: Wages ₦12,000; Electricity ₦5,000; Depreciation: Furniture ₦1,000; Other expenses ?; Excess of income over expenditure ?; Total ₦35,000\nIncome: Subscriptions received ₦20,000; Donations received ₦10,000; Profit on sale of investment ₦5,000; Total ₦35,000\nIt is the tradition of the club to write off an amount equal to 25% of the subscriptions received as other expenses.\n\nWhat is the amount to be written off as other expenses?|₦4,500|₦6,000|₦4,000|₦5,000": "The club's custom is to write off 25% of subscriptions received as other expenses. Step 1: subscriptions received = ₦20,000. Step 2: 25% × ₦20,000 = ₦5,000: D. This ₦5,000 is the missing \"other expenses\" figure in the income and expenditure account, and it is needed before the club's surplus can be found. Note that the base is subscriptions only, not total income. Memory tip: 25% is one quarter: ₦20,000 ÷ 4.",
 "|Use the following information: \nIncome and Expenditure Account\nExpenditure: Wages ₦12,000; Electricity ₦5,000; Depreciation: Furniture ₦1,000; Other expenses ?; Excess of income over expenditure ?; Total ₦35,000\nIncome: Subscriptions received ₦20,000; Donations received ₦10,000; Profit on sale of investment ₦5,000; Total ₦35,000\nIt is the tradition of the club to write off an amount equal to 25% of the subscriptions received as other expenses.\n\nDetermine the club's excess of income over expenditure.|₦12,000|₦15,000|₦10,000|₦14,500": "An income and expenditure account is a club's profit and loss account; its \"profit\" is called the excess of income over expenditure (surplus). Step 1: income = ₦20,000 + ₦10,000 + ₦5,000 = ₦35,000. Step 2: expenditure = wages ₦12,000 + electricity ₦5,000 + depreciation ₦1,000 + other expenses ₦5,000 = ₦23,000. Step 3: surplus = ₦35,000 − ₦23,000 = ₦12,000: A. Memory tip: surplus = income − expenditure; the balancing figure on the debit side.",
 "|The gross profit on manufactured goods is the difference between the cost of goods manufactured and the|market value of goods produced|prime cost of production|indirect cost of production|goods produced": "Some manufacturers transfer finished goods from the manufacturing account to the trading account at market value (what it would cost to buy them from outside) instead of at cost. The difference between that market value of the goods produced and their cost of manufacture is the manufacturing (gross) profit, which shows whether making the goods is cheaper than buying them: A. Prime cost and indirect cost are only parts of the production cost. Memory tip: manufacturing profit = market value − cost of production.",
 "|Given that 1/3 of the ₦6,000 stock held by a branch is purchased from outsiders. If goods are invoiced to the branch at 25% on cost, the provision for unrealised profit is|₦1,000.00|₦333.33|₦666.66|₦800.00": "A provision for unrealised profit removes head office's profit from goods still unsold at the branch. Step 1: goods from outsiders contain no head office profit, so only 2/3 × ₦6,000 = ₦4,000 is from head office, at invoice price. Step 2: with a 25% mark-up on cost, cost ₦100 becomes invoice ₦125, so profit is 25/125 of the invoice price. Step 3: ₦4,000 × 25/125 = ₦800: D. The trap: ₦1,000 (A) takes 25% of the invoice price, but the 25% is on cost, not on the invoice price. Memory tip: mark-up on cost x% → profit = x/(100 + x) of selling price.",
 "|A.S.D Club Balance Sheet [Extract]\nAccumulated fund ₦6,000 add surplus income ₦1,000 ... ₦7,000\nCreditors for supplies ... ₦1,000\nSubscriptions in advance ... ₦2,000\nTotal ... ₦10,000\nMotor van ... ₦4,000\nStock ... ₦3,500\nDebtors for subscriptions ... ₦1,000\nBank ... ₦1,500\nTotal ... ₦10,000\n\nThe working capital of the club is|₦5,000|₦4,000|₦3,000|₦7,000": "Working capital = current assets − current liabilities. Step 1: current assets = stock ₦3,500 + debtors for subscriptions ₦1,000 + bank ₦1,500 = ₦6,000. Step 2: current liabilities = creditors for supplies ₦1,000 + subscriptions in advance ₦2,000 = ₦3,000. Step 3: working capital = ₦6,000 − ₦3,000 = ₦3,000: C. The motor van is a fixed asset and the accumulated fund is the club's capital. Memory tip: subscriptions in advance = liability; subscriptions owing = asset.",
 "|Costs that vary in proportion to the level of production in a manufacturing environment are known as|control costs|overheads|direct costs|indirect costs": "Direct costs are costs that can be traced straight to each unit produced: direct materials, direct labour and direct expenses. Making twice as many units needs about twice as much material and labour, so these costs vary in proportion to the level of production: C. Overheads and indirect costs (factory rent, supervisors' salaries) are mostly fixed over a range of output. Memory tip: Direct = varies Directly with output.",
 "|A company operating a chain of retail provision stores invoices goods to the branches at cost plus a mark-up of 25%. What is the mark-up percentage on selling price?|35%|30%|20%|15%": "Mark-up is profit as a percentage of cost; margin is profit as a percentage of selling price. Step 1: let cost = ₦100; a 25% mark-up gives profit ₦25 and selling price ₦125. Step 2: margin = ₦25 ÷ ₦125 × 100 = 20%: C. Memory tip: mark-up of 1/4 on cost = margin of 1/5 on selling price (x/(100 + x)).",
 "|Given: \nCash purchases ... ₦25,000\nTrade creditors ... ₦45,000\nOpening balance of trade creditors ... ₦35,000\n\nCalculate the purchases for the period.|₦80,000|₦35,000|₦105,000|₦55,000": "Purchases = cash purchases + credit purchases. Credit purchases come from the creditors account: payments + closing creditors − opening creditors. Step 1: no payments to creditors are given, so credit purchases = closing ₦45,000 − opening ₦35,000 = ₦10,000. Step 2: total purchases = ₦25,000 + ₦10,000 = ₦35,000: B. The trap: ₦105,000 (C) comes from adding the opening creditors instead of deducting them; opening creditors are debts for purchases of the previous period. Memory tip: credit purchases = paid + closing − opening creditors.",
 "|Which method of pricing can be used satisfactorily in either a rising or falling price situation?|Standard price|Market price|FIFO|Average method": "When prices rise, FIFO gives high profits and stock values, while LIFO gives low ones; when prices fall the effects reverse. The average cost method blends old and new prices, so issues are priced at a middle figure and stock is valued sensibly whichever way prices move: D. Standard price needs frequent revision, and market price is not a cost-based method. Under IAS 2 (Inventories) today, only FIFO and weighted average cost (or specific identification) are allowed. Memory tip: Average = the middle road for up or down prices.",
 "|Given: \nCapital at start ... ₦3,250\nCapital at close ... ₦6,250\nAdditional capital during the period ... ₦1,000\n\nCalculate the profit or loss.|₦8,500 loss|₦2,000 loss|₦2,000 profit|₦8,500 profit": "Under the net worth method, profit = closing capital − opening capital + drawings − additional capital. Step 1: increase in capital = ₦6,250 − ₦3,250 = ₦3,000. Step 2: part of that increase came from ₦1,000 of new capital put in, not from trading, so deduct it: ₦3,000 − ₦1,000 = ₦2,000. No drawings are given. Profit = ₦2,000: C. Memory tip: Profit = Closing − Opening + Drawings − New capital.",
 "|In a control account, discount received is found on the|debit side of the purchases ledger control account|debit side of the sales ledger control account|credit side of the purchases ledger control account|credit side of the sales ledger control account": "The purchases ledger control account mirrors the suppliers' accounts: credit purchases go on the credit side, and anything that reduces what we owe (payments, returns outwards, discounts received) goes on the debit side. Discount received reduces the amount owed to suppliers, so it is debited there: A. Discount allowed belongs on the credit side of the sales ledger control account. The trap: C (credit side of the purchases ledger control) catches students who think \"received\" means credit; it would wrongly increase what we owe. Memory tip: received from suppliers = Debit Purchases ledger control.",
 "|In an incomplete record system, a trading account cannot be prepared until the|day book has been balanced|amount of personal drawings has been established|cash book has been balanced|amount of sales and purchases has been established": "A trading account compares sales with the cost of goods sold (opening stock + purchases − closing stock). In incomplete records these figures are not in any ledger, so they must first be worked out, for example from total debtors and total creditors accounts and cash records: D. Drawings matter for the capital account, not the trading account, and there may be no day book or cash book to balance. Memory tip: no sales and purchases, no trading account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts: January 2nd, 500 units at ₦25 = ₦12,500; March 7th, 250 units at ₦28 = ₦7,000\nIssues were made as follows: January 9th, 200 units; February 14th, 200 units; March 11th, 200 units\n\nThe closing stock on March 11th by LIFO valuation is|₦4,200|₦2,700|₦4,500|₦3,900": "Perpetual LIFO issues the latest goods first. Step 1: 2 Jan: 500 units at ₦25. Step 2: 9 Jan issue 200, then 14 Feb issue 200: 100 units at ₦25 left. Step 3: 7 Mar: receive 250 at ₦28. Step 4: 11 Mar issue 200, all from the latest batch at ₦28, leaving 50 at ₦28. Step 5: closing stock = 50 × ₦28 + 100 × ₦25 = ₦1,400 + ₦2,500 = ₦3,900: D. The trap: ₦4,200 (A) is the FIFO figure (150 × ₦28). Under IAS 2 (Inventories), LIFO is no longer allowed. Memory tip: LIFO leaves the OLD stock behind.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts: January 2nd, 500 units at ₦25 = ₦12,500; March 7th, 250 units at ₦28 = ₦7,000\nIssues were made as follows: January 9th, 200 units; February 14th, 200 units; March 11th, 200 units\n\nThe value of closing stock as at February 14th by simple average method is|₦3,900|₦2,500|₦4,100|₦2,700": "Under the simple average method each issue is priced at the plain average of the purchase prices of the batches in stock. Step 1: by 14 February only the 2 January batch (₦25 per unit) has been received, so the average price is ₦25. Step 2: units left = 500 − 200 − 200 = 100. Step 3: closing stock = 100 × ₦25 = ₦2,500: B. The March batch at ₦28 arrives later and cannot affect the February value. Memory tip: value stock at a date using only the batches received by that date.",
 "|Given an incomplete record without sufficient information to determine profit, the necessary thing to do is to|draw up the statement of affairs|draw up a T-account to establish the amount|compare the journal entries with the cash book|cross-check the cash book for further information": "When records are so incomplete that a trading and profit and loss account cannot be drawn up, profit is found by comparing net worth. Draw up a statement of affairs (assets less liabilities) at the start and at the end of the period; the increase in capital, adjusted for drawings and new capital, is the profit: A. T-accounts and cash-book checks help only when there is enough information to rebuild the figures. Memory tip: not enough data = statement of affairs.",
 "|Control accounts help to verify the arithmetic accuracy of the postings from the|subsidiary books into the trial balance|ledgers into the trial balance|journals into the ledgers|subsidiary books into the ledgers": "A control account (total account) is built from the totals in the subsidiary books: sales day book, returns books, cash book and journal. Its balance should equal the total of the individual balances in the sales or purchases ledger. If they agree, the postings from the subsidiary books into the ledgers are arithmetically correct; if not, the error is located in that ledger: D. Memory tip: control account = subsidiary book totals checked against ledger balances.",
 "|Keeping records under the single entry system has the advantage of|duality in terms of records|completeness in terms of records|accuracy in terms of operation|simplicity in terms of operation": "Single entry (incomplete records) means recording only one side of transactions, often just cash and personal accounts. It is cheap and simple, needs little bookkeeping skill, and suits small traders: D. It lacks duality, it is not complete, and it gives no arithmetic check such as a trial balance, so A, B and C are actually the advantages of double entry, not of single entry. Memory tip: Single entry = Simple; Double entry = Dependable.",
 "|Given: \nSales ... ₦195,200\nStock 1st January ... ₦34,000\nPurchases ... ₦126,000\nSales returns ... ₦1,200\nPurchases returns ... ₦2,000\n\nIf the gross profit is ₦66,000, what is the value of stock at 31st December?|₦30,000|₦40,000|₦50,000|₦20,000": "Step 1: net sales = ₦195,200 − ₦1,200 = ₦194,000. Step 2: cost of sales = net sales − gross profit = ₦194,000 − ₦66,000 = ₦128,000. Step 3: net purchases = ₦126,000 − ₦2,000 = ₦124,000. Step 4: goods available = ₦34,000 + ₦124,000 = ₦158,000. Step 5: closing stock = ₦158,000 − ₦128,000 = ₦30,000: A. Memory tip: work back from GP: Sales − GP = Cost of sales; then Available − Cost of sales = Closing stock.",
 "|Given (₦m): \nBalance at 31st December ... 14,744\nTreasury Bills issued Jan – Dec ... 7,124\nRevenue for the year ... 6,387\nExpenditure ... 8,767\n\nWhat is the opening balance on the consolidated revenue fund account?|₦10,000m|₦12,000m|₦9,000m|₦11,000m": "The Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF) is the main government fund into which revenues are paid and from which expenditure is met. Closing balance = opening balance + receipts − payments. Here receipts are treasury bills issued (₦7,124m) and revenue (₦6,387m). Step 1: opening = closing − receipts + expenditure. Step 2: ₦14,744m − ₦7,124m = ₦7,620m. Step 3: ₦7,620m − ₦6,387m = ₦1,233m. Step 4: ₦1,233m + ₦8,767m = ₦10,000m: A. Memory tip: work the fund backwards: closing − ins + outs = opening.",
 "|Use the following information: \nThe partnership agreement between Abba, Baba and Kaka contains the following provisions: (i) 5% interest to be paid on capital and no interest to be charged on drawings; (ii) profits and losses to be shared in the ratio 3:2:1 respectively; (iii) net profit for the year to 31/12/95 ₦2,250.\n(Abba / Baba / Kaka)\nCapital ... ₦5,000 / ₦4,000 / ₦3,000\nCurrent account ... ₦250 / ₦100 / ₦175\nSalary ... ₦300 / ₦300 / -\nDrawings ... ₦600 / ₦500 / ₦250\n\nAbba's capital balance at the end of the year will be|₦5,475|₦5,725|₦4,400|₦5,000": "When partners keep both capital accounts and current accounts, the capital accounts are fixed: they change only when a partner brings in or withdraws capital permanently. Interest on capital, salaries, shares of profit and drawings all go through the current accounts. Nothing has been added to or withdrawn from Abba's capital, so it stays at ₦5,000: D. The trap: ₦5,725 (B) is what you get if you add Abba's current account items (₦250 + ₦250 + ₦300 + ₦525 − ₦600 = ₦725) to his capital. Memory tip: fixed capitals: everything else goes to current.",
 "|Use the following information: \nThe partnership agreement between Abba, Baba and Kaka contains the following provisions: (i) 5% interest to be paid on capital and no interest to be charged on drawings; (ii) profits and losses to be shared in the ratio 3:2:1 respectively; (iii) net profit for the year to 31/12/95 ₦2,250.\n(Abba / Baba / Kaka)\nCapital ... ₦5,000 / ₦4,000 / ₦3,000\nCurrent account ... ₦250 / ₦100 / ₦175\nSalary ... ₦300 / ₦300 / -\nDrawings ... ₦600 / ₦500 / ₦250\n\nCurrent account balance of Kaka at the end of the year will be|₦250|₦350|₦175|₦325": "Step 1: interest at 5%: Abba ₦250, Baba ₦200, Kaka ₦150 (total ₦600). Step 2: salaries ₦300 + ₦300 = ₦600. Step 3: residual profit = ₦2,250 − ₦600 − ₦600 = ₦1,050; Kaka's 1/6 = ₦175. Step 4: Kaka's current account = opening ₦175 + interest ₦150 + profit ₦175 − drawings ₦250 = ₦250: A. The trap: ₦175 (C) is both his opening balance and his profit share, but not the closing balance. Memory tip: current account = opening + interest + salary + share − drawings.",
 "|Upon the dissolution of a partnership, the Partnership Act provides that the amount realised should be|used to pay all taxes due to government|used to start a new partnership business by members who are willing|shared equally by the existing partners|used in paying the debts and liabilities of the firm to persons who are not partners": "On dissolution the firm's assets are sold, and the Partnership Act 1890 (section 44), which applies in Nigeria through the state partnership laws, sets the order of payment: first the debts and liabilities of the firm to outsiders (non-partners), then loans made by partners, then partners' capital, and any surplus is shared in the profit-sharing ratio: D. The money is not shared equally first, nor used to start a new firm. Memory tip: outsiders first, partners last.",
 "|In the public sector, the method of accounting that reports revenues and expenditures in the period in which they are received and paid is called|fund accounting|commitment accounting|cash accounting|accrual accounting": "Under cash accounting, revenue is recorded when cash is received and expenditure when cash is paid, regardless of the period the service relates to. This was the traditional basis of Nigerian government accounts: C. Accrual accounting records items when earned or incurred; commitment accounting records orders when they are placed; fund accounting keeps separate accounts for separate funds. Nigeria adopted IPSAS (International Public Sector Accounting Standards) and moved to accrual accounting from 2016. Memory tip: Cash basis = Cash in, Cash out.",
 "|Which of the following is used to update the cash book in bank reconciliation?|Interest received and unpresented cheques|Commission and debit note|Unpresented cheques and direct credit|Interest received and direct credit": "A bank reconciliation compares the cash book with the bank statement. Items that appear on the bank statement but not yet in the cash book are entered in the cash book to update it: interest received, direct credits (e.g. customers paying straight into the account), dividends collected, bank charges, commission and standing orders. Items that are already in the cash book but not yet on the statement, such as unpresented cheques and uncredited lodgements, are timing differences that go only in the reconciliation statement. Interest received and direct credit are both bank-statement items, so they update the cash book: D. Options A and C include unpresented cheques, which never update the cash book. Memory tip: the bank knew first, so the cash book must catch up.",
 "|The two legally recognized professional accounting bodies in Nigeria are the|Institute of Certified Public Accountants of Nigeria and the Institute of Cost and Management Accountants of Nigeria|Association of Accountants of Nigeria and the Institute of Management Accountants of Nigeria|Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria and the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria|Nigeria Accounting Association and the Executive Cost and Management Accountants of Nigeria": "Two professional accountancy bodies in Nigeria were set up by law. The Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria (ICAN) was established by Act No. 15 of 1965, and the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria (ANAN) by Decree No. 76 of 1993 (now an Act). Both can admit members and regulate their professional conduct: C. This is still true today, while the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria (FRC, which replaced the NASB in 2011) sets accounting standards. None of the other bodies named was created by a Nigerian law as an accountancy body. Memory tip: ICAN 1965, ANAN 1993: the two legal accountancy bodies.",
 "|Verifiability in accounting is only possible when there are|minutes of meetings|payment vouchers|source documents|audit certificates": "Verifiability (also called objectivity) means that another person, such as an auditor, can check a figure and reach the same result. That is only possible if every entry is supported by documentary evidence: invoices, receipts, credit notes, cheque counterfoils and vouchers, together known as source documents: C. The trap: payment vouchers (B) are one kind of source document, but they only support payments, not all transactions. Minutes of meetings record decisions, and an audit certificate is the auditor's report at the end, which itself relies on source documents. Memory tip: no document, no entry; no entry can be verified without a source document.",
 "|Use the following information:\nCapital ... ₦2,000\nBank ... ₦1,200\nPurchases ... ₦2,500\nSales ... ₦6,700\nStock ... ₦1,300\nCreditors ... ₦1,000\nFixed assets ... ₦3,700\nDrawings ... ?\nDrawings are always estimated at 50% of capital.\n\nThe trial balance total is|₦9,700|₦8,700|₦7,900|₦7,800": "Step 1: Drawings are 50% of capital: 50% × ₦2,000 = ₦1,000.\nStep 2: Debit balances (assets, expenses, drawings): bank 1,200 + purchases 2,500 + stock 1,300 + fixed assets 3,700 + drawings 1,000 = ₦9,700.\nStep 3: Credit balances (capital, liabilities, income): capital 2,000 + sales 6,700 + creditors 1,000 = ₦9,700.\nThe two sides agree, so the trial balance total is ₦9,700: A. The trap: ₦8,700 (B) is the debit total if you forget to put in the drawings. Memory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits are Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits are Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|Use the following information:\nCapital ... ₦2,000\nBank ... ₦1,200\nPurchases ... ₦2,500\nSales ... ₦6,700\nStock ... ₦1,300\nCreditors ... ₦1,000\nFixed assets ... ₦3,700\nDrawings ... ?\nDrawings are always estimated at 50% of capital.\n\nCompute the amount withdrawn|₦2,000|₦1,500|₦1,200|₦1,000": "Drawings are the value a sole trader takes out of the business for personal use. The note says drawings are always estimated at 50% of capital. Capital in the trial balance is ₦2,000, so drawings = 50% × ₦2,000 = ₦1,000: D. Drawings are a debit balance, which is why adding ₦1,000 to the debit side made the trial balance agree at ₦9,700 in the previous question. Memory tip: read the note under a table first; it usually holds the missing figure.",
 "|Use the following information:\nEzekiel Malgwi is a retailer. He recorded sales representing 95% cash and 5% credit. He took the total cash sales of ₦19,000 from the business tray and banked it.\n\nThe credit sales for the period would be|₦5,000|₦1,900|₦1,000|₦950": "Step 1: The cash sales of ₦19,000 are 95% of total sales.\nStep 2: Total sales = 19,000 ÷ 95% = 19,000 × 100/95 = ₦20,000.\nStep 3: Credit sales = 5% × 20,000 = ₦1,000 (check: 20,000 − 19,000 = ₦1,000): C.\nThe trap: ₦950 (D) is 5% of ₦19,000; it takes 5% of the cash sales instead of 5% of total sales. Memory tip: when a figure is a percentage of the whole, find the whole first (divide by the percentage), then take the other part.",
 "|Use the following information:\nEzekiel Malgwi is a retailer. He recorded sales representing 95% cash and 5% credit. He took the total cash sales of ₦19,000 from the business tray and banked it.\n\nThe transaction would be recorded in the cash book as debit|cash and credit bank|bank and credit cash|cash and credit cash|bank and credit bank": "Taking the cash sales from the business till and paying them into the bank is a contra entry: money moves from one part of the business to another, and both accounts are in the cash book. The bank account receives the money, so the bank column is debited; the cash account gives it out, so the cash column is credited. In the cash book each side is marked \"C\" for contra: B. The trap: A (debit cash, credit bank) is the reverse entry, used when cash is withdrawn from the bank for office use. Memory tip: debit the receiver, credit the giver; the bank receives the cash.",
 "|Which accounting concept supports the assertion that economic reality takes precedence over legal issues?|Realization concept|Substance over form|Conservatism|Measurement concept": "The concept of substance over form says that transactions should be accounted for according to their economic reality, not just their legal form. For example, goods on hire purchase are shown as the buyer's fixed asset even though legal title passes only after the last instalment; a finance lease is treated the same way: B. Realisation deals with when revenue is recognised, conservatism (prudence) with not overstating profits and assets, and measurement with how items are valued. Under today's IFRS Conceptual Framework, substance over form is part of faithful representation. Memory tip: substance over form = reality beats the paperwork.",
 "|The transaction that completes its double entry in the same ledger account appears in|cash account and personal account|bank account and general ledger|discount received and discount allowed|cash account and bank account": "A contra entry is the only kind of transaction whose debit and credit are both made in the same book: the two-column cash book holds the cash account and the bank account side by side. When cash is paid into the bank, the bank column is debited and the cash column credited; when cash is withdrawn for office use, the reverse: D. Discounts received and allowed are memorandum columns whose totals are posted to separate ledger accounts, and a personal account is in the sales or purchases ledger. Memory tip: contra = cash and bank swapping money inside the cash book.",
 "|Given:\nCapital ... ₦1,000\nLiabilities ... ₦500\nAssets ... ₦1,500\n\nThe accounting equation can be expressed as|₦1,000 + ₦500 + ₦1,500 = ₦3,000|₦1,000 − ₦500 + ₦1,500 = ₦2,000|₦1,000 + ₦500 = ₦1,500|₦1,500 + ₦500 = ₦2,000": "The accounting equation says that everything a business owns (assets) is financed either by the owner (capital) or by outsiders (liabilities): Capital + Liabilities = Assets. Here ₦1,000 + ₦500 = ₦1,500, which matches the assets figure given: C. It can also be written Assets − Liabilities = Capital (1,500 − 500 = 1,000). Option A adds all three figures together, which double counts; B and D mix the signs and give totals that do not match anything. Memory tip: A = C + L; the two sides of the balance sheet always agree.",
 "|The recording phase of accounting is known as|bookkeeping|trial balancing|ledger accounting|final accounting": "Accounting has stages: recording, classifying, summarising, and then analysing and interpreting. Bookkeeping is the recording phase: entering every transaction in the books of original entry (day books, journal, cash book) and posting to the ledger. Accounting builds on bookkeeping to prepare the trial balance and final accounts and to interpret them for users: A. The trap: ledger accounting (C) sounds like recording, but the ledger is only part of the records, and posting to it is a step within bookkeeping. Trial balancing and final accounts come after recording. Memory tip: bookkeeping keeps the books; accounting explains them.",
 "|Use the following information:\nMotor Vehicle Account as at 31st December, 2001\nDebit:\nJan. 1, Cost ... ₦1,950,000\nDec. 31, Addition ... ₦400,000\nCredit:\nJan. 1, Depreciation ... ₦1,360,000\nJune 30, Sales proceeds ... ₦700,000\nThe vehicle sold was purchased on January 1, 1998 at a cost of ₦1,000,000 and had depreciated at 25% on cost.\n\nAssuming that depreciation is charged on the addition of the year at the rate of 15% on reducing balance, what should be the net book value of the vehicle as at 31st December, 2003?|₦111,000|₦289,000|₦250,000|₦340,000": "The addition of ₦400,000 was made on 31 December 2001, the last day of the year, so there is no depreciation for 2001; the first charge is for 2002.\nStep 1: 2002: 15% × 400,000 = ₦60,000; NBV = ₦340,000.\nStep 2: 2003: 15% × 340,000 = ₦51,000; NBV = ₦289,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦340,000 (D) stops after one year, and ₦111,000 (A) is the accumulated depreciation (60,000 + 51,000), not the net book value. Under reducing balance the rate is applied to the falling book value each year, not to cost. Memory tip: reducing balance: rate × last year's NBV, year by year.",
 "|Use the following information:\nMotor Vehicle Account as at 31st December, 2001\nDebit:\nJan. 1, Cost ... ₦1,950,000\nDec. 31, Addition ... ₦400,000\nCredit:\nJan. 1, Depreciation ... ₦1,360,000\nJune 30, Sales proceeds ... ₦700,000\nThe vehicle sold was purchased on January 1, 1998 at a cost of ₦1,000,000 and had depreciated at 25% on cost.\n\nWhat is the actual profit or loss arising from the vehicle disposed of?|₦250,000 loss|₦50,000 loss|₦450,000 profit|₦575,000 profit": "Step 1: Depreciation at 25% on cost = 25% × ₦1,000,000 = ₦250,000 a year.\nStep 2: From 1 January 1998 to 30 June 2001 is 3½ years: 3.5 × 250,000 = ₦875,000.\nStep 3: NBV at disposal = 1,000,000 − 875,000 = ₦125,000.\nStep 4: Profit = proceeds 700,000 − NBV 125,000 = ₦575,000: D.\nThe trap: ₦450,000 profit (C) charges only the three full years 1998–2000 and ignores the six months of 2001; that would be right only if the business charged no depreciation in the year of sale. Memory tip: profit on disposal = proceeds − NBV; count the months up to the date of sale.",
 "|Given: I. Order of permanence; II. Order of liquidity; III. Vertical order; IV. Horizontal order. The orders in which balance sheet assets are arranged are|I and II|I and IV|II and III|III and IV": "Assets in a balance sheet are listed in one of two orders. In order of permanence, the most permanent assets come first (land and buildings, machinery, then stock, debtors, bank and cash), as is usual in Nigeria and Britain. In order of liquidity, the most liquid come first (cash, bank, debtors, stock, then fixed assets), as banks often do: A. Vertical and horizontal describe the layout of the whole statement, not the order of the assets. Today the balance sheet is called the statement of financial position. Memory tip: Permanence puts the Premises first; Liquidity puts the Loose cash first.",
 "|Use the following information:\nCapital ... Cr ₦24,000\nLand and building ... Dr ₦18,470\nMortgage on premises ... Cr ₦11,090\nDrawings ... Dr ₦3,000\nProfit and loss ... Cr ₦3,600\nFurniture and fittings ... Dr ₦5,120\nMotor vehicles ... Dr ₦3,462\nClosing stock ... Dr ₦3,000\nDebtors ... Dr ₦11,474\nCreditors ... Cr ₦7,354\nCash ... Dr ₦1,518\nTotals ... Dr ₦46,044 ... Cr ₦46,044\n\nWhat is the capital employed?|₦44,600|₦43,052|₦43,044|₦38,600": "Capital employed can be defined in more than one way; here it is taken as the total assets used in the business (gross capital employed).\nStep 1: Fixed assets: 18,470 + 5,120 + 3,462 = ₦27,052.\nStep 2: Current assets: stock 3,000 + debtors 11,474 + cash 1,518 = ₦15,992.\nStep 3: Total = 27,052 + 15,992 = ₦43,044: C.\nMemory tip: capital employed (gross) = fixed assets + current assets.",
 "|Use the following information:\nCapital ... Cr ₦24,000\nLand and building ... Dr ₦18,470\nMortgage on premises ... Cr ₦11,090\nDrawings ... Dr ₦3,000\nProfit and loss ... Cr ₦3,600\nFurniture and fittings ... Dr ₦5,120\nMotor vehicles ... Dr ₦3,462\nClosing stock ... Dr ₦3,000\nDebtors ... Dr ₦11,474\nCreditors ... Cr ₦7,354\nCash ... Dr ₦1,518\nTotals ... Dr ₦46,044 ... Cr ₦46,044\n\nCalculate the value of fixed assets|₦15,992|₦18,470|₦27,000|₦27,052": "Fixed assets are those held for long-term use in the business, not for resale. In this trial balance they are land and building ₦18,470, furniture and fittings ₦5,120 and motor vehicles ₦3,462.\nStep 1: 18,470 + 5,120 = 23,590.\nStep 2: 23,590 + 3,462 = ₦27,052: D.\nStock, debtors and cash are current assets (they total ₦15,992, option A). Option B, ₦18,470, is only the land and building. Memory tip: fixed assets are the things you keep (land, furniture, vehicles); current assets are the things that turn into cash.",
 "|Provision for discount allowed can be recorded as a debit to|the profit and loss account and a credit to provision for discount allowed|provision for discount allowed and a credit to the profit and loss account|discount allowed and a credit to the profit and loss account|expenses and a credit to customers' account": "A provision for discount allowed recognises that some debtors at the year end will take cash discounts when they pay next year. Under the accruals and prudence concepts that expected cost belongs to this year. The entry is debit profit and loss account (an expense) and credit provision for discount allowed; the provision is then shown as a deduction from debtors in the balance sheet: A. The trap: option B is exactly the reverse entry, which is used only when an existing provision is reduced. Memory tip: creating any provision: debit P&L, credit provision.",
 "|Use the following information:\nFeb. 1 ... Purchased 400 units at ₦1.00 each\nFeb. 5 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦2.00 each\nFeb. 10 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦3.00 each\nFeb. 15 ... Issued 320 units\nFeb. 20 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦3.00 each\nFeb. 25 ... Issued 120 units\n\nCalculate the price per unit of closing stock using the periodic weighted average method.|₦3.20|₦3.00|₦2.20|₦2.00": "The periodic weighted average method takes the total cost of all goods bought in the period and divides by the total units bought.\nStep 1: Total cost: 400 × 1 + 200 × 2 + 200 × 3 + 200 × 3 = 400 + 400 + 600 + 600 = ₦2,000.\nStep 2: Total units: 400 + 200 + 200 + 200 = 1,000.\nStep 3: Weighted average = 2,000 ÷ 1,000 = ₦2.00 per unit: D.\nClosing stock (1,000 − 440 = 560 units) is valued at this price. Stock is now called inventory. Memory tip: weighted average weighs each price by its quantity; total cost ÷ total units.",
 "|Use the following information:\nFeb. 1 ... Purchased 400 units at ₦1.00 each\nFeb. 5 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦2.00 each\nFeb. 10 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦3.00 each\nFeb. 15 ... Issued 320 units\nFeb. 20 ... Purchased 200 units at ₦3.00 each\nFeb. 25 ... Issued 120 units\n\nUsing the periodic simple average method, the cost per unit is|₦4.25|₦3.25|₦2.25|₦1.25": "The periodic simple average method ignores quantities and simply averages the different purchase prices.\nStep 1: Prices: ₦1.00, ₦2.00, ₦3.00, ₦3.00.\nStep 2: Sum = 1 + 2 + 3 + 3 = ₦9.00.\nStep 3: Simple average = 9 ÷ 4 = ₦2.25 per unit: C.\nCompare with the weighted average of ₦2.00: the simple average is higher because the large cheap batch of 400 units at ₦1 counts only once. Memory tip: simple average = add the prices, divide by how many prices; weighted average = total cost ÷ total units.",
 "|Use the following information:\nPurchases ledger control account\nDebit side:\nDiscount received ... ₦1,000\nBills payable ... ₦1,500\nCash paid to suppliers ... ₦8,000\nReturns outwards ... ₦3,000\nBalance c/d ... ?\nCredit side:\nBalance b/d ... ₦2,900\nCredit purchases ... ?\nBills payable dishonoured ... ₦1,600\nDishonoured cheques ... ₦2,000\nCredit purchases are always put at 150% of the total cash paid to suppliers.\n\nCalculate the closing balance of the ledger account|₦6,900|₦6,400|₦5,000|₦4,000": "Step 1: Credit purchases = 150% × cash paid ₦8,000 = ₦12,000.\nStep 2: Credit side: balance b/d 2,900 + credit purchases 12,000 + bills payable dishonoured 1,600 + dishonoured cheques 2,000 = ₦18,500.\nStep 3: Debit side: discount received 1,000 + bills payable 1,500 + cash paid 8,000 + returns outwards 3,000 = ₦13,500.\nStep 4: Balance c/d = 18,500 − 13,500 = ₦5,000: C.\nDishonoured cheques and bills go on the credit side because they restore what we owe suppliers. Memory tip: in the purchases ledger control account, what increases creditors is a credit; what reduces them is a debit.",
 "|Use the following information:\nPurchases ledger control account\nDebit side:\nDiscount received ... ₦1,000\nBills payable ... ₦1,500\nCash paid to suppliers ... ₦8,000\nReturns outwards ... ₦3,000\nBalance c/d ... ?\nCredit side:\nBalance b/d ... ₦2,900\nCredit purchases ... ?\nBills payable dishonoured ... ₦1,600\nDishonoured cheques ... ₦2,000\nCredit purchases are always put at 150% of the total cash paid to suppliers.\n\nDetermine the credit purchases|₦15,500|₦13,500|₦13,400|₦12,000": "The note says credit purchases are always 150% of the total cash paid to suppliers. Cash paid to suppliers is ₦8,000, so credit purchases = 150% × 8,000 = 1.5 × 8,000 = ₦12,000: D. This figure is then entered on the credit side of the purchases ledger control account, because buying on credit increases what the business owes its suppliers. ₦13,500 (B) is the total of the debit side before the balance, not the credit purchases. Memory tip: 150% means multiply by 1.5.",
 "|In a control account, provision for bad debts is found on the|debit side of the purchases ledger control account|debit side of the sales ledger control account|credit side of the purchases ledger control account|credit side of the sales ledger control account": "The provision for bad (doubtful) debts is linked to debtors, so it belongs with the sales ledger, not the purchases ledger. It has a credit balance and reduces the debtors figure, so when it is shown in a control account it is on the credit side of the sales ledger control account: D. In practice most businesses keep the provision in its own separate account and deduct it from debtors in the balance sheet, because it is an estimate, not an amount any one customer owes. Memory tip: bad debts and their provision always follow the debtors (sales ledger), on the credit side.",
 "|The stock valuation data is important because it enables management to|separate debtors from creditors|determine the cost of stock at the end of business|determine the cost of stock at the beginning of business|determine the total cost of goods purchased": "Stock valuation (by FIFO, LIFO, weighted or simple average) is used to put a cost on the goods left unsold at the end of the trading period. That closing stock figure is then used to find cost of sales and gross profit, and it appears as a current asset in the balance sheet: B. Stock at the beginning comes from last year's closing figure, and the cost of goods purchased comes from purchase invoices, not from valuation methods. Debtors and creditors have nothing to do with stock. Stock is now called inventory, valued under IAS 2 at the lower of cost and net realisable value. Memory tip: value the stock at the end to find the profit.",
 "|During the year ended 31st December 2001, Nuju Enterprises paid rent as follows:\nJan. 20 ₦24,000 for a period of 4 months\nMay 16 ₦30,000 for a period of 5 months\nOct. 15 ₦36,000 for a period of 6 months\nWhat is the overpaid portion of the rent?|₦36,000|₦18,000|₦12,000|₦6,000": "Step 1: The three payments cover 4 + 5 + 6 = 15 months (January to April, May to September, October to March 2002).\nStep 2: Only 12 months fall in the year to 31 December 2001, so 3 months (January to March 2002) are paid in advance.\nStep 3: Monthly rent on the last payment = 36,000 ÷ 6 = ₦6,000.\nStep 4: Prepaid rent = 3 × 6,000 = ₦18,000: B.\nThe prepayment is shown as a current asset and deducted from rent in the profit and loss account. Memory tip: months paid − months used = months prepaid.",
 "|Advertising expenses incurred on a product in a business organization should be charged to|production department|sales department|administration department|purchases department": "In departmental accounts, each expense is charged to the department that causes or benefits from it. Advertising a product is done to increase its sales, so it is a selling and distribution cost and is charged to the sales department: B. Production overheads are factory costs such as factory power and machine depreciation; administration covers office costs such as the manager's salary and office rent; the purchases department deals with buying goods. Memory tip: if it helps to sell, charge it to sales.",
 "|Four broad classifications of overheads are|production, selling, distribution and material|production, selling, distribution and administration|selling, distribution, production and wages|distribution, selling, administration and material": "Overheads are indirect costs: costs that cannot be traced to one unit of product. They are grouped by function into production (factory) overheads, selling overheads, distribution overheads and administration overheads: B. Materials and wages, as in options A, C and D, are mainly direct costs that form prime cost, so they are not classes of overheads. The trap: D lists three correct classes but adds material, which is a direct cost. Memory tip: overheads go PSDA: Production, Selling, Distribution, Administration.",
 "|Given:\nCost of raw material issued to production ... ₦37,000\nOpening stock of raw material ... ₦2,000\nPurchases of raw material ... ₦38,000\nWages ... ₦5,000\n\nWhat is the closing stock of raw material?|₦4,000|₦3,000|₦2,000|₦1,000": "Closing stock of raw materials is what is left after issues to production.\nStep 1: Materials available = opening stock 2,000 + purchases 38,000 = ₦40,000.\nStep 2: Less issued to production ₦37,000.\nStep 3: Closing stock = 40,000 − 37,000 = ₦3,000: B.\nWages of ₦5,000 are a labour cost and play no part in the raw materials stock. Memory tip: opening + purchases − used = closing, for any stock.",
 "|Use the following information:\nPQ Manufacturing Company\nRaw materials (1/1/2001) ... ₦3,500\nRaw materials (31/12/2001) ... ₦4,900\nPurchase of raw materials ... ₦56,000\nSalary ... ₦63,000\nWages ... ₦148,050\nFactory lighting ... ₦3,080\nPlant depreciation ... ₦2,800\nFactory insurance ... ₦2,170\n\nThe overhead cost is|₦71,050|₦68,250|₦67,970|₦66,080": "Prime cost is direct materials and direct labour; overheads are the indirect costs. Wages (₦148,050) are direct labour, so they are not overheads.\nStep 1: Factory overheads: lighting 3,080 + plant depreciation 2,800 + insurance 2,170 = ₦8,050.\nStep 2: Add salary ₦63,000 (indirect).\nStep 3: Overheads = 63,000 + 8,050 = ₦71,050: A.\nThe other options leave out one of the items: ₦68,250 omits the plant depreciation and ₦67,970 omits factory lighting. Memory tip: wages make the product (prime cost); salaries and factory expenses support it (overheads).",
 "|Use the following information:\nPQ Manufacturing Company\nRaw materials (1/1/2001) ... ₦3,500\nRaw materials (31/12/2001) ... ₦4,900\nPurchase of raw materials ... ₦56,000\nSalary ... ₦63,000\nWages ... ₦148,050\nFactory lighting ... ₦3,080\nPlant depreciation ... ₦2,800\nFactory insurance ... ₦2,170\n\nThe company consumed raw materials worth|₦54,600|₦56,000|₦57,400|₦59,500": "Raw materials consumed (used) is the first figure in a manufacturing account.\nStep 1: Opening stock of raw materials ₦3,500.\nStep 2: Add purchases ₦56,000 = ₦59,500 available.\nStep 3: Less closing stock ₦4,900.\nStep 4: Raw materials consumed = 59,500 − 4,900 = ₦54,600: A.\nThe trap: ₦57,400 (C) comes from adding the closing stock and taking away the opening stock instead. ₦59,500 (D) forgets to deduct closing stock. Memory tip: consumed = opening + purchases − closing.",
 "|Income and expenditure account is more informative than receipts and payments account because it discloses|cash|cheques|debtors|stocks": "A receipts and payments account records only cash actually received and paid. An income and expenditure account follows the accruals concept, so it includes income earned and expenses incurred whether paid or not. That is why it can show subscriptions in arrears and other amounts owed to the club, i.e. debtors, which the cash account cannot show: C. Cash and cheques are already in the receipts and payments account. Memory tip: R&P = cash only; I&E = accruals, so it sees who owes the club.",
 "|A company has two departments X and Y. Y transferred goods to X at a profit margin of 25%. ₦10,000 worth of goods was not sold at the end of the accounting period. How much is the unrealized profit?|₦2,000|₦2,500|₦12,000|₦12,500": "A profit margin is profit expressed as a percentage of the selling price; a mark-up is profit as a percentage of cost. The ₦10,000 of unsold goods is at the transfer price from Y, so it already contains Y's profit.\nStep 1: Margin of 25% on selling price: profit = 25% × 10,000 = ₦2,500: B.\nJAMB's answer, ₦2,000, treats the 25% as a mark-up on cost (10,000 × 25/125); the wording \"margin\" points to ₦2,500. The unrealised profit is removed from stock with a provision. Memory tip: margin on selling price, mark-up on cost.",
 "|Given:\nRich Social Club\nCash ... ₦100,000\nLoan ... ₦30,000\nSubscription in arrears ... ₦5,000\nSubscription in advance ... ₦15,000\n\nWhat is the accumulated fund?|₦60,000|₦80,000|₦90,000|₦110,000": "The accumulated fund is a club's equivalent of capital: total assets minus total liabilities.\nStep 1: Assets: cash 100,000 + subscriptions in arrears (owed by members) 5,000 = ₦105,000.\nStep 2: Liabilities: loan 30,000 + subscriptions in advance (owed to members as future service) 15,000 = ₦45,000.\nStep 3: Accumulated fund = 105,000 − 45,000 = ₦60,000: A.\nThe trap: ₦80,000 (B) swaps the two, treating arrears as a liability and advance as an asset. Memory tip: arrears = asset, advance = liability.",
 "|A company has departments M and N. The expense incurred wholly in M is ₦5,000. General expenses incurred amount to ₦25,000. Expenses are apportioned in the ratio 2:3 for departments M and N respectively. What is N's share of the expenses?|₦10,000|₦15,000|₦18,000|₦20,000": "In departmental accounts, an expense incurred wholly in one department is charged only to that department; general expenses are shared in the agreed ratio.\nStep 1: Ratio M : N = 2 : 3, so total parts = 5.\nStep 2: N's share of general expenses = 3/5 × 25,000 = ₦15,000: B.\nStep 3: The ₦5,000 incurred wholly in M goes only to M (M's total = 10,000 + 5,000 = ₦15,000).\nThe trap: ₦18,000 (C) wrongly shares the whole ₦30,000 between the departments (3/5 × 30,000). Memory tip: direct to the department, general by the ratio.",
 "|Use the following information:\nTotal sales of tickets ... ₦300,000\nAmount received from the sales ... ₦260,000\nCash purchase of the club house ... ₦100,000\nCredit purchase of equipment ... ₦60,000\nElectricity bill paid ... ₦5,000\nSalaries owed ... ₦2,000\n\nWhat is the receipts and payments account balance?|₦260,000|₦233,000|₦155,000|₦100,000": "A receipts and payments account is a cash summary, so only money actually received or paid counts.\nStep 1: Receipts: amount received from ticket sales ₦260,000.\nStep 2: Payments: cash purchase of club house 100,000 + electricity 5,000 = ₦105,000.\nStep 3: Balance = 260,000 − 105,000 = ₦155,000: C.\nThe credit purchase of equipment and the salaries owed have not been paid, and the ₦40,000 of unpaid ticket sales has not been received, so all of these are left out. Memory tip: R&P asks only, did cash move?",
 "|Use the following information:\nTotal sales of tickets ... ₦300,000\nAmount received from the sales ... ₦260,000\nCash purchase of the club house ... ₦100,000\nCredit purchase of equipment ... ₦60,000\nElectricity bill paid ... ₦5,000\nSalaries owed ... ₦2,000\n\nWhat is the balance of the income and expenditure account?|₦300,000|₦293,000|₦193,000|₦133,000": "The income and expenditure account uses the accruals concept and includes only revenue items.\nStep 1: Income: total ticket sales earned ₦300,000 (including the ₦40,000 not yet received).\nStep 2: Expenditure: electricity 5,000 + salaries owed 2,000 = ₦7,000.\nStep 3: Surplus = 300,000 − 7,000 = ₦293,000: B.\nThe club house and equipment are capital expenditure, shown in the balance sheet, not in the I&E account. The trap: ₦193,000 (C) wrongly charges the club house as an expense. Memory tip: I&E = earned income − revenue expenses; capital items go to the balance sheet.",
 "|The first step in converting a single entry to a double entry system of bookkeeping is to prepare a|suspense account|statement of affairs|realization account|trial balance": "Under single entry (incomplete records) a trader does not keep full double-entry books. To convert to double entry, you must first know the opening position, so you prepare a statement of affairs at the start date, listing all assets and liabilities; the difference is opening capital. The ledger accounts are then opened with these balances and the year's transactions posted: B. A suspense account is for trial balance differences, a realisation account is used when a partnership is dissolved, and a trial balance comes after the ledger is written up. Memory tip: statement of affairs first, then the double entry.",
 "|Use the following information:\nEmeka Enterprises: Statement of Affairs at start\nAssets:\nCash ... ₦70,000\nInventory ... ₦58,000\nMachines ... ₦100,000\nLiabilities:\nCreditors ... ₦85,000\nBank loan ... ₦60,000\n\nWhat is the capital at start?|₦53,000|₦63,000|₦73,000|₦83,000": "A statement of affairs is like a balance sheet for a business with incomplete records; the capital is the difference between assets and liabilities.\nStep 1: Assets: cash 70,000 + inventory 58,000 + machines 100,000 = ₦228,000.\nStep 2: Liabilities: creditors 85,000 + bank loan 60,000 = ₦145,000.\nStep 3: Capital at start = 228,000 − 145,000 = ₦83,000: D.\nMemory tip: capital = what you own − what you owe.",
 "|Receipts and payments account is the summary of|income and expenditure|cash book|balance sheet|profit and loss": "A receipts and payments account is prepared by clubs and societies. It starts with the opening cash and bank balances, lists all money received and paid during the year (capital and revenue items alike), and ends with the closing balance. It is therefore simply a summary of the cash book: B. The income and expenditure account is a different statement, prepared on the accruals basis like a profit and loss account, and the balance sheet lists assets and liabilities. Memory tip: R&P = cash book in summary.",
 "|Given:\nTotal debtors b/d 31/12/99 ... ₦25,000\nCheques received from debtors ... ₦255,000\nTotal debtors c/d 31/12/00 ... ₦15,000\n\nDetermine the sales figure.|₦295,000|₦265,000|₦245,000|₦215,000": "Total debtors account: debtors at the start plus credit sales, less cash received, gives debtors at the end. Work backwards for sales.\nStep 1: Cash received from debtors ₦255,000.\nStep 2: Add closing debtors ₦15,000 = ₦270,000.\nStep 3: Less opening debtors ₦25,000.\nStep 4: Credit sales = 270,000 − 25,000 = ₦245,000: C.\nThe trap: ₦265,000 (B) adds the opening debtors and takes away the closing ones, the reverse of the right adjustment. Memory tip: sales = cash received + closing debtors − opening debtors.",
 "|Which of these is a capital expenditure warrant?|Provisional general warrant|Development fund general warrant|Reserved expenditure general warrant|Supplementary warrant": "In Nigerian public sector accounting, a warrant is the authority from the Minister of Finance (or state Commissioner) that releases money for spending. Recurrent spending is authorised by the general warrant, supplementary warrant, provisional general warrant and reserved expenditure warrant. Capital spending comes from the Development Fund and is released by a development fund general warrant (or a development fund supplementary warrant): B. A provisional warrant lets spending continue before the budget is passed, and reserved expenditure covers statutory items such as judges' salaries. Memory tip: Development = capital projects.",
 "|For the purpose of conversion of a partnership to a company, a total purchase consideration of ₦3.6m was paid. If 30% of the purchase consideration was in cash and the rest by 50k shares, how many shares were issued?|1,080,000|2,160,000|3,600,000|5,040,000": "Step 1: Cash part = 30% × ₦3,600,000 = ₦1,080,000.\nStep 2: Shares part = 3,600,000 − 1,080,000 = ₦2,520,000 (70%).\nStep 3: Each share is 50 kobo (₦0.50), so number of shares = 2,520,000 ÷ 0.50 = 5,040,000: D.\nThe trap: 1,080,000 (A) is the cash part in naira, and 2,160,000 (B) divides the cash part, not the shares part, by 50k. Memory tip: number of shares = value paid in shares ÷ price per share.",
 "|Given: I. The consolidated revenue fund balance b/d ₦650,000; II. Issues from contingency fund amounted to ₦240,000; III. Amount transferred to contingency fund is ₦210,000. What is the consolidated revenue fund balance?|₦1,100,000|₦680,000|₦440,000|₦410,000": "The Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF) receives all government revenues. The Contingencies Fund is set up from the CRF to meet urgent, unforeseen spending.\nStep 1: Start with the CRF balance b/d ₦650,000.\nStep 2: Transfer to the Contingencies Fund leaves the CRF: 650,000 − 210,000 = ₦440,000: C.\nIssues from the Contingencies Fund (₦240,000) are spent on urgent needs and are later replaced by supplementary appropriation; they are not paid into the CRF. JAMB's answer, ₦680,000, adds them to the CRF. Memory tip: money into the Contingencies Fund leaves the CRF; money out of it goes on spending.",
 "|Use the following information:\nGoods from head office ... ₦13,400\nReturns to head office ... ₦118\nRates and insurance paid ... ₦188\nWages paid ... ₦913\nCash remitted to head office ... ₦16,625\nStock at 1/1/2000 ... ₦1,875\nRent paid ... ₦325\nStock at 31/12/2000 ... ₦1,975\nSundry expenses ... ₦200\n\nThe profit realized from the trade is|₦5,069|₦1,817|₦1,440|₦1,328": "Here the branch's expenses are taken as paid out of its takings, and the rest remitted to head office.\nStep 1: Sales = cash remitted 16,625 + expenses 1,626 = ₦18,251.\nStep 2: Cost of sales = opening stock 1,875 + goods 13,400 − returns 118 − closing stock 1,975 = ₦13,182.\nStep 3: Trading (gross) profit = 18,251 − 13,182 = ₦5,069: A.\nThe trap: ₦1,817 (B) is the net profit if head office paid the expenses, a different figure from the profit on trading. Memory tip: profit from trade = sales − cost of goods sold.",
 "|Use the following information:\nGoods from head office ... ₦13,400\nReturns to head office ... ₦118\nRates and insurance paid ... ₦188\nWages paid ... ₦913\nCash remitted to head office ... ₦16,625\nStock at 1/1/2000 ... ₦1,875\nRent paid ... ₦325\nStock at 31/12/2000 ... ₦1,975\nSundry expenses ... ₦200\n\nWhat is the total expenses?|₦1,875|₦1,764|₦1,626|₦1,426": "Expenses are the costs of running the branch that are written off against profit: rates and insurance ₦188, wages ₦913, rent ₦325 and sundry expenses ₦200.\nStep 1: 188 + 913 = 1,101.\nStep 2: 1,101 + 325 = 1,426.\nStep 3: 1,426 + 200 = ₦1,626: C.\nGoods from head office, returns, stocks and cash remitted are trading or cash items, not expenses. The trap: ₦1,426 (D) leaves out the ₦200 sundry expenses. Memory tip: list the expense items and add them one at a time.",
 "|The necessary accounts to be opened on the issue of ordinary share capital are|preference share capital, cash and allotment accounts|bank and ordinary share capital accounts|cash and ordinary share capital accounts|nominal value and ordinary share capital accounts": "When a company issues ordinary shares, money comes in and the company owes that capital to its shareholders. Application money is paid to the company's bankers, so the double entry is debit bank account and credit ordinary share capital account; where shares are paid in instalments, application and allotment and call accounts are used in between: B. The trap: C (cash and ordinary share capital) looks almost the same, but share money is received through the bank, not as office cash. Preference share capital has nothing to do with an issue of ordinary shares. Memory tip: shares in, bank debited, share capital credited.",
 "|The amount of each invoiced goods sent to the branch is debited to|branch expense account and credited to branch debtors' account|branch stock account and credited to the goods sent to branch account|branch debtors' account and credited to branch expense account|goods sent to branch account and credited to branch expense account": "When a head office invoices goods to a branch (the branch stock or \"stock and debtors\" system), the entry is debit branch stock account, because the branch now holds the goods, and credit goods sent to branch account, which reduces head office purchases available for its own trading: B. Branch expenses and branch debtors are separate accounts used for other transactions. At the year end the goods sent to branch account is closed to head office trading account (or purchases). Memory tip: goods go to the branch, so the branch stock is debited.",
 "|Use the following information:\nZEBRA PLC: Balance sheet as at 31st March, 2002\nCapital ... ₦100,000\nCurrent liabilities:\nCreditors ... ₦30,000\nTotal ... ₦130,000\nFixed assets:\nLand and buildings ... ₦50,000\nFurniture ... ₦10,000 ... ₦60,000\nCurrent assets:\nStock ... ₦30,000\nDebtors ... ₦30,000\nCash ... ₦10,000 ... ₦70,000\nTotal ... ₦130,000\nThe business was acquired on 1st April, 2002 at a purchase consideration of ₦120,000 by SOZ. All assets and liabilities were taken over except the cash. To open the new firm's bank account, additional ₦20,000 was paid into the bank.\n\nThe goodwill on purchase is|₦90,000|₦30,000|₦19,000|₦18,000": "Goodwill is the excess of the purchase consideration over the value of the net assets taken over.\nStep 1: Assets taken over (all except cash): land and buildings 50,000 + furniture 10,000 + stock 30,000 + debtors 30,000 = ₦120,000.\nStep 2: Less creditors taken over ₦30,000: net assets = ₦90,000.\nStep 3: Goodwill = 120,000 − 90,000 = ₦30,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦90,000 (A) is the net assets, not the goodwill. Memory tip: goodwill = price paid − net assets bought.",
 "|Use the following information:\nZEBRA PLC: Balance sheet as at 31st March, 2002\nCapital ... ₦100,000\nCurrent liabilities:\nCreditors ... ₦30,000\nTotal ... ₦130,000\nFixed assets:\nLand and buildings ... ₦50,000\nFurniture ... ₦10,000 ... ₦60,000\nCurrent assets:\nStock ... ₦30,000\nDebtors ... ₦30,000\nCash ... ₦10,000 ... ₦70,000\nTotal ... ₦130,000\nThe business was acquired on 1st April, 2002 at a purchase consideration of ₦120,000 by SOZ. All assets and liabilities were taken over except the cash. To open the new firm's bank account, additional ₦20,000 was paid into the bank.\n\nCalculate the net worth of the business|₦120,000|₦90,000|₦80,000|₦100,000": "Net worth is what the owners have in the business: total assets less outside liabilities. In ZEBRA PLC's balance sheet total assets are ₦130,000 (fixed assets 60,000 + current assets 70,000) and the only liability is creditors ₦30,000, so net worth = 130,000 − 30,000 = ₦100,000: D. This equals the capital shown, as the accounting equation says it must. The trap: ₦90,000 (B) is the net assets SOZ took over after leaving out the ₦10,000 cash. Memory tip: net worth = assets − liabilities = capital.",
 "|Gross profit in the branch adjustment account is transferred to the branch|trading account|receipts and payments account|profit and loss account|income and expenditure account": "In the branch stock system with goods sent at selling price, the branch adjustment (or mark-up) account holds the profit loading on goods. At the year end the loading on closing stock is carried down, and the rest, the gross profit realised on goods sold, is transferred to the branch profit and loss account, where branch expenses are charged against it: C. Receipts and payments and income and expenditure accounts belong to clubs, not branches. Memory tip: branch adjustment = the branch's gross profit, sent on to branch P&L.",
 "|Mamza Nigeria Limited issued 20,000 ordinary shares of ₦1.50 per share on 2nd July, 2002. 1,500 ordinary shares were fully subscribed for and their monies received as below:\nOn application ... ₦0.40\nOn allotment ... ₦0.20\nFirst call (including premium of ₦0.50) ... ₦0.70\nThe last and final call was yet to be made. What is the balance of the ordinary share capital account after the first call was received?|₦300|₦1,050|₦1,200|₦1,950": "Step 1: Each share has a nominal value of ₦1.00 and a premium of ₦0.50 (issue price ₦1.50).\nStep 2: Received so far per share: 0.40 + 0.20 + 0.70 = ₦1.30, of which ₦0.50 is premium, so ₦0.80 is share capital.\nStep 3: Share capital = 1,500 × 0.80 = ₦1,200: C.\nThe premium of 1,500 × 0.50 = ₦750 goes to the share premium account. The trap: ₦1,950 (D) is all money received, including the premium. Memory tip: share capital takes only the nominal value; premium has its own account.",
 "|The accounting principle that is applied to check arbitrary actions on the part of accountants is|Consistency|Materiality|Objectivity|Realization": "The objectivity concept says accounts should be based on facts that can be checked by anyone, such as invoices, receipts and agreed prices, not on an accountant's personal opinion or guesses. Because every figure must be backed by evidence, an accountant cannot simply pick values that suit him; this checks arbitrary actions: C. Consistency means using the same methods from year to year, materiality lets small items be treated simply, and realisation says revenue is recognised when goods or services pass to the customer. Memory tip: Objectivity = Objective evidence, no opinions.",
 "|The loss of equipment in a fire disaster results in|a decrease in current asset|an increase in liabilities and assets|a decrease in assets and capital|an increase in liabilities and a decrease in assets": "Use the accounting equation, Assets = Capital + Liabilities. When equipment is burnt and not replaced by insurance, the asset side falls by its book value. Nobody is owed anything new, so liabilities do not change; instead the loss is written off to the profit and loss account, which lowers profit and therefore the owner's capital. Both sides fall by the same amount: C. Equipment is a fixed asset, not a current asset, so A is wrong, and nothing increases liabilities. Memory tip: a loss with no one to pay = less assets, less capital.",
 "|A source document is used for verifying the|Amount due to creditors|Actual cost of an asset|Selling price of goods|Amount due from debtors": "Source documents are the first written evidence of a transaction: invoices, receipts, credit notes, debit notes, cheque counterfoils and so on. They are used to check and support the entries in the books of original entry. A sales invoice sent to a customer shows the goods sold, the quantity and the price charged, so it is the document used to verify the selling price of goods: C. Other documents back up other facts, but the question links source documents with prices on invoices. Memory tip: no document, no entry; the invoice proves the price.",
 "|The rule of accounting equation requires that accounts payable should be placed under|Liabilities|Equities|Assets|Capital": "The accounting equation is Assets = Capital + Liabilities. Accounts payable (trade creditors) are amounts the business owes to suppliers for goods or services bought on credit. Anything the business owes to outsiders is a liability, so accounts payable are placed under liabilities: A. Equity and capital mean the owner's interest in the business, and assets are what the business owns, such as cash, stock and debtors (accounts receivable). Today the balance sheet is called the statement of financial position, but payables are still liabilities. Memory tip: payable = you must pay = liability; receivable = you will receive = asset.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCash Book\nCapital ... ₦6,600 | Purchases ... ₦3,500\nSales ... ₦3,000 | Stationery ... ₦1,300\nDebtors ... ₦2,500 | Wages ... ₦2,700\nThe owner wishes to maintain an amount equal to 1/3 of capital as drawings.\n\nThe amount withdrawn is|₦2,100|₦2,200|₦4,400|₦6,400": "The owner wants drawings equal to one-third of capital.\nStep 1: Capital = ₦6,600.\nStep 2: Drawings = 1/3 × ₦6,600 = ₦2,200: B.\nDrawings are what the owner takes out of the business for private use; they reduce capital and are credited in the cash book (debited to the drawings account). The other figures do not come from one-third of ₦6,600 (₦4,400 is two-thirds). Memory tip: drawings are based on capital only, not on sales or other receipts.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCash Book\nCapital ... ₦6,600 | Purchases ... ₦3,500\nSales ... ₦3,000 | Stationery ... ₦1,300\nDebtors ... ₦2,500 | Wages ... ₦2,700\nThe owner wishes to maintain an amount equal to 1/3 of capital as drawings.\n\nThe cash book closing balance will be|₦900|₦2,400|₦4,200|₦4,600": "Step 1: Total receipts (debit side) = capital ₦6,600 + sales ₦3,000 + debtors ₦2,500 = ₦12,100.\nStep 2: Total payments (credit side) = purchases ₦3,500 + stationery ₦1,300 + wages ₦2,700 + drawings ₦2,200 = ₦9,700.\nStep 3: Closing balance = ₦12,100 − ₦9,700 = ₦2,400 (a debit balance, cash in hand): B.\nThe trap: ₦4,600 (D) is what you get if you forget to take out the ₦2,200 drawings from Question 5. Memory tip: balance = receipts − all payments, including what the owner takes out.",
 "|A debit in the suspense account will appear in the balance sheet as|a current asset|a current liability|capital|drawings": "A suspense account is opened when the trial balance does not agree, to hold the difference until the errors are found. If the errors are not all found by the time the final accounts are prepared, the balance stays in the balance sheet. A debit balance is treated like something owed to the business and shown among the current assets; a credit balance is shown among the current liabilities: A. Capital and drawings are the owner's accounts and do not hold suspense balances. Memory tip: debit suspense sits with assets; credit suspense sits with liabilities.",
 "|The recipient whose name appears on cheque is called a|Payer|Drawer|Drawee|Payee": "A cheque has three parties. The drawer is the account holder who writes and signs the cheque; the drawee is the bank ordered to pay; and the payee is the person whose name is written on the cheque to receive the money: D. The trap: drawee (C) sounds like \"the one it is drawn for\", but the drawee is the bank, not the receiver. A payer is simply someone who pays and is not the technical term here. Memory tip: payee gets Paid; drawer Draws (writes) the cheque; drawee is the bank.",
 "|The imprest account is subsidiary to the|ledger account|bank account|cash book|petty cash": "Under the imprest system the petty cashier gets a fixed float, pays small expenses (stamps, transport, cleaning) and records them in the petty cash book. At the end of the period the main cashier refunds the exact amount spent, restoring the float. The petty cash book is therefore a subsidiary of the main cash book, which supplies and controls the imprest: C. The trap: \"petty cash\" (D) is the imprest itself, so it cannot be what the imprest is subsidiary to. Memory tip: imprest = small cash book that answers to the big cash book.",
 "|The three-column cash book differs from the two-column cash book in|Cash column|Discount column|Bank column|Folio column": "A two-column cash book has a cash column and a bank column on each side, recording money in hand and money at the bank. The three-column cash book adds a third column on each side for discounts: discount allowed on the debit side and discount received on the credit side. The discount columns are memorandum totals, posted to the discount accounts in the ledger: B. The cash and bank columns are in both, and a folio column is not counted as one of the money columns. Memory tip: three columns = Cash, Bank, Discount.",
 "|Zakari started business in January 2000. He bought a shop costing ₦54,000 and stock worth ₦7,600. Profit for the year amounted to ₦22,100. His closing capital was ₦73,800. Zakari's personal drawings amounted to|₦2,300|₦9,900|₦17,100|₦19,500": "Step 1: Opening capital = shop ₦54,000 + stock ₦7,600 = ₦61,600.\nStep 2: Closing capital = opening capital + profit − drawings, so ₦73,800 = ₦61,600 + ₦22,100 − drawings.\nStep 3: ₦61,600 + ₦22,100 = ₦83,700; drawings = ₦83,700 − ₦73,800 = ₦9,900: B.\nThe trap: ₦2,300 (A) comes from leaving out the ₦7,600 stock when working out opening capital (₦54,000 + ₦22,100 − ₦73,800). Memory tip: closing capital = opening capital + profit − drawings (+ any new capital).",
 "|The balance on the provision for depreciation account is|added to fixed assets on the balance sheet|deducted from fixed assets on the balance sheet|deducted from the profit and loss account|added to the current liabilities of the account": "Depreciation for each year is charged to the profit and loss account and credited to the provision for depreciation (accumulated depreciation) account. The balance on this provision account builds up over the years and is shown in the balance sheet as a deduction from the cost of the fixed assets, giving their net book value: B. The trap: C is close because each year's depreciation is charged in the profit and loss account, but the question is about the accumulated balance, which belongs in the balance sheet. Memory tip: Cost − accumulated depreciation = net book value.",
 "|Given: \nRates paid during the year amounted to ₦2,250 at ₦125 per month and ₦1,035 was paid for electricity in the same period at ₦115 per month. Determine the amount of prepaid rates.|₦345|₦750|₦1,380|₦1,500": "Step 1: Months of rates paid = ₦2,250 ÷ ₦125 = 18 months.\nStep 2: Only 12 months belong to this year, so 18 − 12 = 6 months are paid in advance.\nStep 3: Prepaid rates = 6 × ₦125 = ₦750: B.\nThe trap: ₦345 (A) is the electricity figure: ₦1,035 ÷ ₦115 = 9 months paid, so 3 × ₦115 = ₦345 is electricity accrued (owing), not rates prepaid. Prepaid rates are a current asset; accrued electricity is a current liability. Memory tip: paid more months than the year has = prepayment; fewer = accrual.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Receipt (units) ... Issued (units)\nMay 2 ... 80 @ ₦3.00 ... -\nMay 10 ... 50 @ ₦3.20 ... -\nMay 17 ... - ... 20\nMay 25 ... - ... 60\nMay 31 ... 20 @ ₦2.90 ... -\n\nWhat is the value of raw materials issued on May 17 based on the LIFO method?|₦60|₦61|₦62|₦64": "LIFO (last in, first out) issues the most recently received units first.\nStep 1: Before May 17 the store holds 80 units at ₦3.00 (May 2) and 50 units at ₦3.20 (May 10).\nStep 2: The latest batch is May 10, so the 20 units come from it: 20 × ₦3.20 = ₦64: D.\nThe trap: ₦60 (A) is 20 × ₦3.00, the FIFO answer. Note that LIFO is no longer allowed under IAS 2 (IFRS), which permits only FIFO and weighted average. Memory tip: LIFO = Last In goes First Out = newest price.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Receipt (units) ... Issued (units)\nMay 2 ... 80 @ ₦3.00 ... -\nMay 10 ... 50 @ ₦3.20 ... -\nMay 17 ... - ... 20\nMay 25 ... - ... 60\nMay 31 ... 20 @ ₦2.90 ... -\n\nBased on the FIFO method of valuation, the total cost of raw materials issued is|₦150|₦160|₦240|₦250": "FIFO (first in, first out) issues the oldest units first.\nStep 1: May 17 issue of 20 units comes from the May 2 batch: 20 × ₦3.00 = ₦60. Units left from May 2: 60.\nStep 2: May 25 issue of 60 units also comes from the May 2 batch: 60 × ₦3.00 = ₦180.\nStep 3: Total cost of issues = ₦60 + ₦180 = ₦240: C.\nThe May 31 receipt comes after all the issues, so it does not affect them. Memory tip: FIFO = oldest price first, like a queue.",
 "|Where closing stock is undervalued, the effect is|an increase in gross profit|a decrease in gross profit|an increase in purchases|a decrease in purchases": "In the trading account, cost of sales = opening stock + purchases − closing stock. If closing stock is undervalued (shown too low), less is deducted, so cost of sales comes out too high, and gross profit = sales − cost of sales comes out too low: B. Purchases are not affected by how closing stock is valued. The trap: A is the effect of overvaluing closing stock. Remember too that this year's closing stock is next year's opening stock, so next year's profit will be overstated. Memory tip: closing stock and gross profit move together: stock down, profit down.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOpening balance ... ₦10,640\nPurchases ... ₦26,912\nReturns inwards ... ₦492\nReturns outwards ... ₦810\nCash discount received ... ₦1,348\nCash discount allowed ... ₦1,560\nCash paid to suppliers ... ₦20,808\nAt the year-end, the purchases ledger showed a debit balance of ₦108.\n\nThe total on the debit side of the purchases ledger control account will be:|₦22,966|₦22,156|₦21,834|₦21,054": "The purchases ledger (creditors') control account has the creditors' balance on the credit side; items that reduce what we owe go on the debit side.\nStep 1: Cash paid to suppliers ₦20,808.\nStep 2: Returns outwards ₦810.\nStep 3: Cash discount received ₦1,348.\nTotal = ₦20,808 + ₦810 + ₦1,348 = ₦22,966: A.\nReturns inwards and discount allowed concern customers and go to the sales ledger control account. (The closing credit balance c/d, found in Question 18, is entered separately.) Memory tip: payments, returns out and discounts received cut creditors, so they are debits.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOpening balance ... ₦10,640\nPurchases ... ₦26,912\nReturns inwards ... ₦492\nReturns outwards ... ₦810\nCash discount received ... ₦1,348\nCash discount allowed ... ₦1,560\nCash paid to suppliers ... ₦20,808\nAt the year-end, the purchases ledger showed a debit balance of ₦108.\n\nThe balancing figure in the purchases ledger control account is|₦15,828|₦15,720|₦14,940|₦14,694": "Step 1: Credit side = opening balance ₦10,640 + purchases ₦26,912 + closing debit balance c/d ₦108 = ₦37,660.\nStep 2: Debit side items (from Question 17) = ₦22,966.\nStep 3: Balancing figure (closing credit balance c/d) = ₦37,660 − ₦22,966 = ₦14,694: D.\nThe small debit balance of ₦108 is carried down on the credit side because it is the opposite of the normal creditor balance. Leaving it out would give ₦14,586, which is not an option, so include every item. Memory tip: a debit balance at the end is entered as a balance c/d on the credit side.",
 "|In a sales ledger, contra entry is found on the|debit side of the debtors' control account|debit side of the creditors' control account|credit side of the debtors' control account|credit side of the creditors' control account": "A contra entry (set-off) arises when a customer is also a supplier: instead of paying each other, the amounts are set against each other. In the individual accounts we debit the supplier's account in the purchases ledger and credit the customer's account in the sales ledger. In the control accounts this means a credit in the sales ledger (debtors') control account and a debit in the purchases ledger (creditors') control account: C. The trap: B is the same set-off seen from the purchases ledger side, but the question asks about the sales ledger. Memory tip: a contra always reduces both debtors and creditors.",
 "|The beginning and ending accounts receivable balances were ₦20,000 and ₦30,000 respectively. The collection from customers during the period was ₦70,000. What was the total sales on account?|₦20,000|₦60,000|₦80,000|₦120,000": "Use the debtors (accounts receivable) account: opening balance + credit sales − cash collected = closing balance.\nStep 1: ₦20,000 + credit sales − ₦70,000 = ₦30,000.\nStep 2: Credit sales = ₦70,000 + ₦30,000 − ₦20,000 = ₦80,000: C.\nThe trap: ₦60,000 (B) comes from subtracting the increase in debtors instead of adding it. Since debtors rose by ₦10,000, sales on account were more than the cash collected. Memory tip: credit sales = cash collected + closing debtors − opening debtors.",
 "|Given: \nGeneral expenses account\nPaid by cheque ... ₦671\nPaid by cash ... ₦70\nAccrued b/f ... ₦65\nAccrued c/f ... ₦115\n\nDetermine how much to be charged to profit and loss account.|₦606|₦786|₦791|₦856": "The amount charged to profit and loss is the expense that belongs to the year, not just the cash paid.\nStep 1: Total paid = cheque ₦671 + cash ₦70 = ₦741.\nStep 2: Deduct accrued b/f, which belonged to last year: ₦741 − ₦65 = ₦676.\nStep 3: Add accrued c/f, which belongs to this year but is unpaid: ₦676 + ₦115 = ₦791: C.\nThe trap: ₦856 (D) is ₦741 + ₦115, forgetting to deduct the ₦65 owed from last year. Memory tip: charge = paid − opening accrual + closing accrual.",
 "|In converting single entry to final account, the balancing figures on the income and expenses accounts are transferred to the|revenue and expenditure account|receipts and payments account|profit and loss appropriation account|profit and loss account": "When a trader keeps incomplete (single entry) records, the accountant rebuilds double entry by opening accounts for each income and expense, such as rent, wages and commission. Each account is adjusted for amounts owing and prepaid, and the balancing figure, the amount that belongs to the year, is transferred to the profit and loss account to find net profit: D. Receipts and payments and income and expenditure accounts are used by clubs, and the appropriation account is used to share profit in partnerships and companies. Memory tip: a trader's income and expenses all end up in profit and loss.",
 "|Use the following information: \nBeginning inventory ... ?\nPurchases ... ?\nEnding inventory ... ₦4,800\nSales ... ₦12,800\nWages ... ₦1,040\nGross profit ... ₦5,900\nThe beginning inventory was estimated at 80% of sales.\n\nDetermine the amount for the beginning inventory.|₦20,140|₦10,240|₦8,140|₦7,240": "The question tells us the beginning inventory was estimated at 80% of sales.\nStep 1: Sales = ₦12,800.\nStep 2: Beginning inventory = 80% × ₦12,800 = 0.8 × ₦12,800 = ₦10,240: B.\nRead every condition in an estimate carefully: here the percentage is applied to sales, not to gross profit or ending inventory. Memory tip: \"80% of sales\" means multiply sales by 0.8.",
 "|Cost classification in relation to charges is determined by the level of|raw materials|labour|activity|profitability": "Cost behaviour classification groups costs by how they change as the level of activity (output or sales volume) changes. Fixed costs, such as rent, stay the same whatever the output; variable costs, such as raw materials, rise in direct proportion to output; semi-variable costs, such as telephone bills, have a fixed part and a variable part: C. Raw materials and labour are kinds of cost (elements), and profitability is a result, not the basis of the classification. Memory tip: fixed, variable or semi-variable? Ask \"what happens when activity changes?\"",
 "|Use the following information: \nMaterials consumed ... ₦16,600\nDirect cost ... ₦5,400\nFactory rent ... ₦2,300\nFactory lighting ... ₦1,200\nCost of production to be transferred at cost plus 20% mark-up.\n\nThe gross profit on manufacture is|₦6,120|₦6,100|₦5,100|₦5,000": "Step 1: Prime cost = materials consumed ₦16,600 + direct cost ₦5,400 = ₦22,000.\nStep 2: Add factory overheads: rent ₦2,300 + lighting ₦1,200 = ₦3,500, so cost of production = ₦25,500.\nStep 3: Manufacturing (gross) profit = 20% × ₦25,500 = ₦5,100: C.\nThe goods are transferred to the trading account at ₦30,600, and the ₦5,100 mark-up is credited to profit and loss as profit on manufacture. Memory tip: mark-up is a percentage of cost, so find the full production cost first.",
 "|Use the following information: \nMaterials consumed ... ₦16,600\nDirect cost ... ₦5,400\nFactory rent ... ₦2,300\nFactory lighting ... ₦1,200\nCost of production to be transferred at cost plus 20% mark-up.\n\nThe market value of goods produced is|₦30,500|₦30,600|₦31,600|₦31,620": "Step 1: Cost of production = ₦16,600 + ₦5,400 + ₦2,300 + ₦1,200 = ₦25,500.\nStep 2: Mark-up = 20% × ₦25,500 = ₦5,100.\nStep 3: Market (transfer) value = ₦25,500 + ₦5,100 = ₦30,600: B.\nThis transfer value is entered in the trading account in place of purchases of finished goods. The trap: ₦30,500 (A) is very close; it differs by only ₦100, so add the figures carefully. Memory tip: transfer value = cost × 1.2 for a 20% mark-up.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOn January 1, 2000, a club's assets and liabilities were as follows:\nClub house ... ₦8,000\nSubscription in arrears ... ₦300\nCash ... ₦1,000\nWages owed ... ₦100\nThe club has 50 members and annual subscription is ₦100 per member. Subscription received is ₦4,000 and this includes 1999 arrears of ₦200.\n\nThe accumulated fund is|₦9,400|₦9,300|₦9,200|₦9,100": "The accumulated fund is a club's capital: total assets minus total liabilities at the start of the period.\nStep 1: Assets = club house ₦8,000 + subscription in arrears ₦300 + cash ₦1,000 = ₦9,300.\nStep 2: Liabilities = wages owed ₦100.\nStep 3: Accumulated fund = ₦9,300 − ₦100 = ₦9,200: C.\nThe trap: ₦9,300 (B) is total assets, forgetting to deduct the wages owed. Memory tip: accumulated fund = assets − liabilities, exactly like a sole trader's capital.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOn January 1, 2000, a club's assets and liabilities were as follows:\nClub house ... ₦8,000\nSubscription in arrears ... ₦300\nCash ... ₦1,000\nWages owed ... ₦100\nThe club has 50 members and annual subscription is ₦100 per member. Subscription received is ₦4,000 and this includes 1999 arrears of ₦200.\n\nIn the balance sheet, the subscription in arrears will be|₦200|₦300|₦1,200|₦1,300": "Step 1: This year's subscriptions due = 50 members × ₦100 = ₦5,000.\nStep 2: Received for this year = ₦4,000 − ₦200 (old arrears) = ₦3,800, so this year's arrears = ₦5,000 − ₦3,800 = ₦1,200.\nStep 3: Old arrears: ₦300 owed at the start, only ₦200 paid, so ₦100 is still owed.\nStep 4: Subscriptions in arrears at the year end = ₦1,200 + ₦100 = ₦1,300: D.\nThe trap: ₦1,200 (C) leaves out the ₦100 of old arrears still unpaid. Memory tip: arrears c/f = all subscriptions still owed, old and new.",
 "|Where the debit side of the income and expenditure account is higher than the credit side, the difference is a|Deficit|surplus|Gain|loss": "An income and expenditure account is the not-for-profit version of a profit and loss account: income on the credit side, expenditure on the debit side. When income is greater, the difference is a surplus (like a profit); when expenditure on the debit side is greater, the difference is a deficit (like a loss): A. Clubs and societies do not aim at profit, so the terms \"gain\" and \"loss\" are not used for the balance of their income and expenditure account. Memory tip: Surplus = income more; Deficit = Debit side more.",
 "|In the balance sheet of a not-for-profit making organization, subscription paid in advance is regarded as a|current asset|current liability|capital|reserve": "Members sometimes pay next year's subscription early. The club has received the money but still owes the members a year of services, so subscriptions in advance are shown in the balance sheet as a current liability, and are carried forward to the credit of next year's subscriptions account: B. The trap: subscriptions in arrears, which members owe the club, are a current asset (A), so the two are easily mixed up. Memory tip: paid in advance = club owes a service = liability; in arrears = members owe = asset.",
 "|A rapidly growing business organization with multiple units that is interested in comparing the performances and weakness of each unit should adopt|manufacturing accounts|consolidated accounts|departmental accounts|joint venture accounts": "Departmental accounts prepare a separate trading and profit and loss account for each department or unit of one business, with shared costs apportioned on a fair basis. Management can then compare the gross and net profit of each unit, find weak ones and decide where to expand or cut back: C. Consolidated accounts combine a group of companies into one set, which hides the results of the separate units; manufacturing accounts find production cost; joint venture accounts record a short-term partnership. Memory tip: departments compared = departmental accounts.",
 "|Use the following information: \nItem ... Dept. S ... Dept. T ... Total\nGross profit b/d ... ₦6,000 ... ₦4,000 ... ₦10,000\nLess: Salaries & wages ... ₦1,800 ... ₦1,200 ... ₦3,000\nElectricity ... ? ... ? ... ₦2,000\nDepreciation ... ₦600 ... ? ... ₦1,000\nNet profit c/d ... ? ... ₦1,600 ... ₦4,000\nIt is the tradition of the organization to apportion expenses in the proportion 60% : 40% for S and T respectively.\n\nWhat is the net profit by department S?|₦3,600|₦3,000|₦2,400|₦2,000": "Expenses are apportioned 60% to S and 40% to T.\nStep 1: S's electricity = 60% × ₦2,000 = ₦1,200 (T gets ₦800).\nStep 2: S's depreciation is given as ₦600.\nStep 3: S's net profit = ₦6,000 − ₦1,800 − ₦1,200 − ₦600 = ₦2,400: C.\nCheck: T = ₦4,000 − ₦1,200 − ₦800 − ₦400 = ₦1,600, and ₦2,400 + ₦1,600 = ₦4,000 total. The trap: ₦3,600 (A) forgets S's share of electricity. Memory tip: always check that the departments add up to the total column.",
 "|Use the following information: \nItem ... Dept. S ... Dept. T ... Total\nGross profit b/d ... ₦6,000 ... ₦4,000 ... ₦10,000\nLess: Salaries & wages ... ₦1,800 ... ₦1,200 ... ₦3,000\nElectricity ... ? ... ? ... ₦2,000\nDepreciation ... ₦600 ... ? ... ₦1,000\nNet profit c/d ... ? ... ₦1,600 ... ₦4,000\nIt is the tradition of the organization to apportion expenses in the proportion 60% : 40% for S and T respectively.\n\nThe depreciation to be charged to department T is|₦300|₦400|₦500|₦600": "Step 1: Total depreciation = ₦1,000.\nStep 2: Department S is charged ₦600 (60%).\nStep 3: Department T's depreciation = ₦1,000 − ₦600 = ₦400, which is 40% × ₦1,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦600 (D) is S's charge, not T's. The given net profit of T (₦1,600) confirms it: ₦4,000 − ₦1,200 − ₦800 − ₦400 = ₦1,600. Memory tip: in a departmental table, a missing figure is the total minus the other department.",
 "|How are credit sales by a department treated?|the same way as normal debtors' transactions|in a different way from normal debtors' transactions|as an addition to the sales manager's account|as an addition to the production manager's account": "In departmental accounts, every department's credit sales are recorded in the normal way: the customer's personal account is debited and the sales account (in that department's column of the sales day book) is credited. Analysis columns simply show which department made the sale, so the department's gross profit can be worked out: A. Credit sales are never added to a manager's personal account. Memory tip: departmental accounting changes the analysis, not the double entry.",
 "|Discounts granted to branch customers are treated as a debit to branch|stock account and a credit to branch debtors' account|discount account and a credit to branch bank account|stock account and a credit to branch bank account|discount account and a credit to branch debtors' account": "When a branch allows a cash discount to a customer who pays promptly, the customer's debt is reduced and the business bears an expense. The entry is debit branch discount (allowed) account and credit branch debtors' account: D. The discount is not a payment out of the bank, so the bank account is not credited, and branch stock is not reduced by a discount. Memory tip: discount allowed = Dr discount allowed, Cr debtors, at head office or branch alike.",
 "|Transfers from the head office to branches are best carried out at|cost price|selling price|cost plus mark-up|market price": "Head office often sends goods to its branches at cost plus a fixed mark-up (invoice price) or at selling price. Using cost plus mark-up lets head office control branch stock, because the branch must account for every item at a known price, and it keeps the real cost and profit margin hidden from branch staff: C. Cost price gives head office less control over branch stock and profit. Memory tip: branch goods travel at \"cost plus\", and the mark-up is removed later through a stock reserve (adjustment) account.",
 "|On dissolution, the final distribution of cash to partners is based on|partnership agreement|articles of association|goodwill|capital balances": "On dissolution, assets are sold, liabilities are paid, and any profit or loss on realisation is shared among the partners in their profit-sharing ratio. Partners' loans are repaid next. What is left is paid to each partner according to the final balance on his capital account after all these adjustments: D. The partnership agreement sets the profit ratio but not the final payout, articles of association belong to companies, and goodwill is just one asset to be realised. Memory tip: last cash out = final capital balances.",
 "|Use the following information: \nJumoke and Kunle have an original investment of ₦25,000 and ₦15,000 respectively in a partnership. The articles of partnership provide 3% interest on capital and salaries of ₦1,500 and ₦1,000 respectively for the partners. The profit and loss are to be shared in the ratio 3:2. Miscellaneous expenses of ₦2,500 were incurred with gross profit of ₦6,500 during the financial year.\n\nThe balance of profit and loss account will be|₦4,000|₦2,800|₦1,500|₦300": "Step 1: Net profit = gross profit ₦6,500 − miscellaneous expenses ₦2,500 = ₦4,000.\nStep 2: This ₦4,000 is the balance of the profit and loss account, carried to the appropriation account: A.\nIn the appropriation account it is then shared: interest on capital ₦750 + ₦450 = ₦1,200, salaries ₦1,500 + ₦1,000 = ₦2,500, leaving ₦300 for the profit-sharing ratio. The trap: ₦300 (D) is the residual profit left after interest and salaries, not the profit and loss balance. Memory tip: profit and loss gives net profit; appropriation shares it out.",
 "|Use the following information: \nJumoke and Kunle have an original investment of ₦25,000 and ₦15,000 respectively in a partnership. The articles of partnership provide 3% interest on capital and salaries of ₦1,500 and ₦1,000 respectively for the partners. The profit and loss are to be shared in the ratio 3:2. Miscellaneous expenses of ₦2,500 were incurred with gross profit of ₦6,500 during the financial year.\n\nWhat is Kunle's share of the profit?|₦600|₦300|₦180|₦120": "Step 1: Net profit = ₦6,500 − ₦2,500 = ₦4,000.\nStep 2: Interest on capital at 3%: Jumoke ₦25,000 × 3% = ₦750; Kunle ₦15,000 × 3% = ₦450; total ₦1,200.\nStep 3: Salaries: ₦1,500 + ₦1,000 = ₦2,500.\nStep 4: Residual profit = ₦4,000 − ₦1,200 − ₦2,500 = ₦300.\nStep 5: Kunle's share (2 of 5 parts) = 2/5 × ₦300 = ₦120: D.\nThe trap: ₦180 (C) is Jumoke's 3/5 share. Memory tip: share the residue in the ratio; 3:2 means 5 parts.",
 "|When a new partner is admitted to a partnership there is a need to revalue the|capital of the business|capital and liabilities of the business|assets and capital of the business|assets and liabilities of the business": "When a new partner joins, the assets and liabilities are revalued to their current values through a revaluation account. Any gain or loss belongs to the old partners and is shared in their old profit-sharing ratio, so the new partner does not gain or lose from changes that happened before he joined: D. Capital is not revalued directly; it changes as a result of the revaluation. Memory tip: revalue what the business owns and owes, then credit or debit the old partners.",
 "|The accounting entry to record the premium on goodwill is to debit|cash and credit old partners' capital|goodwill and credit revaluation|assets and credit capital|capital and credit assets": "A premium for goodwill is cash that an incoming partner pays to the old partners for a share of the goodwill they built up. Under the premium method no goodwill account is opened: the entry is debit cash (bank), credit the old partners' capital accounts in their sacrificing ratio: A. Debiting goodwill (B) is the entry when goodwill is raised in the books, which is a different method, and a revaluation account deals with assets and liabilities, not with a premium paid in cash. Memory tip: premium = cash in, old partners credited.",
 "|The expenses incurred in promoting a company are:|promoters' expenses|floating expenses|preliminary expenses|the board's expenses": "Preliminary (formation) expenses are the costs of bringing a company into existence: legal fees for drafting the memorandum and articles of association, registration fees at the Corporate Affairs Commission, stamp duties and printing: C. Promoters may pay them first and be refunded, but the name for these costs is preliminary expenses. Older texts showed them as a fictitious asset; today they are written off as an expense when incurred. Companies are now governed by the Companies and Allied Matters Act 2020. Memory tip: preliminary = before the company begins.",
 "|Debentures can be redeemed out of|existing share capital reserve|existing general reserve|proceeds from new issue of shares|withholding tax": "Debentures are long-term loans that must be repaid (redeemed). The money can come from profits: the company keeps back profits, for example by using the general reserve or transferring profits to a debenture redemption reserve or sinking fund, and uses them to repay the loan: B. Debentures can also be redeemed out of the proceeds of a new issue of shares or debentures, so C is also accepted. Withholding tax is a tax deducted at source, not a source of redemption money. Memory tip: redeem from profits saved or from fresh money raised.",
 "|Use the following information: \nShare capital ... Issued\n600,000 10% preference shares of ₦0.50 each ... -\n600,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each ... ₦500,000\nCapital reserves ... ₦250,000\nRevenue reserves ... ₦207,000\nLong-term liabilities ... ₦400,000\nTotal ... ₦1,357,000\n\nWhat is the value of the authorized share capital?|₦500,000|₦600,000|₦750,000|₦900,000": "Authorised share capital is the maximum the company may issue, valued at nominal value.\nStep 1: Preference shares = 600,000 × ₦0.50 = ₦300,000.\nStep 2: Ordinary shares = 600,000 × ₦1 = ₦600,000.\nStep 3: Authorised capital = ₦300,000 + ₦600,000 = ₦900,000: D.\nThe trap: ₦600,000 (B) counts only the ordinary shares, forgetting that the preference shares are authorised even though none has been issued. Memory tip: authorised = everything allowed, issued or not.",
 "|Use the following information: \nShare capital ... Issued\n600,000 10% preference shares of ₦0.50 each ... -\n600,000 ordinary shares of ₦1 each ... ₦500,000\nCapital reserves ... ₦250,000\nRevenue reserves ... ₦207,000\nLong-term liabilities ... ₦400,000\nTotal ... ₦1,357,000\n\nThe value of the owner's equity is|₦400,000|₦600,000|₦957,000|₦1,357,000": "Owners' equity (shareholders' funds) = issued share capital + all reserves.\nStep 1: Issued ordinary share capital = ₦500,000 (no preference shares issued).\nStep 2: Reserves = capital reserves ₦250,000 + revenue reserves ₦207,000 = ₦457,000.\nStep 3: Owners' equity = ₦500,000 + ₦457,000 = ₦957,000: C.\nThe trap: ₦1,357,000 (D) wrongly includes the ₦400,000 long-term liabilities, which are owed to lenders, not owners. Memory tip: equity = capital + reserves; loans are never equity.",
 "|Share premium is an example of|revenue reserves|capital reserves|general reserves|loan capital": "Share premium arises when a company issues shares for more than their nominal value, for example ₦1 shares sold at ₦1.50 give ₦0.50 premium per share. It comes from capital raised, not from trading profit, so it is a capital reserve and cannot be paid out as cash dividends; it may be used, for instance, to issue bonus shares: B. Revenue and general reserves come from retained profits, and loan capital is borrowed money such as debentures. Memory tip: premium comes from shareholders' capital, so it is a capital reserve.",
 "|According to the provisions of the 1999 constitution, all revenue generated by the Federal Government must be paid into the|Consolidated Revenue Fund|Federation Account|Revenue Mobilization Allocation and Fiscal Commission's account|Equalization Fund": "Section 80 of the 1999 Constitution says that all revenues or other moneys raised or received by the Federation must be paid into one Consolidated Revenue Fund of the Federation, except money a law sends to a special fund. Spending from it needs an appropriation law: A. The trap: the Federation Account (B) is close. Under section 162, revenue collected by the Federal Government, such as oil revenue and company tax, first goes into the Federation Account to be shared among the three tiers; the Federal Government's own share then goes into its Consolidated Revenue Fund. Memory tip: CRF = the Federal Government's main purse.",
 "|The budget statement presented to the legislature by the Chief Executive is called|an appropriation act|a budget bill|an appropriation bill|a budget proposal": "Each year the President (or a Governor for a state) presents the budget to the legislature in the form of an Appropriation Bill, which sets out how much may be spent on each item. After the National Assembly (or House of Assembly) passes it and the Chief Executive signs it, it becomes the Appropriation Act, the legal authority to spend: C. The trap: an appropriation act (A) is the same document after it has been passed, not when it is first presented. Memory tip: Bill before passing, Act after signing.",
 "|The statement of assets and liabilities in the public sector can be described as the|income and expenditure account of government|appropriation account of government|balance sheet of government|funds flow statement of government": "A statement that lists what an entity owns (assets) and owes (liabilities) at a date is a balance sheet, whether in a business or in government. In government accounting it shows items such as cash, investments, advances and loans against deposits, public debt and other liabilities: C. The income and expenditure account shows revenue and spending over a period, and the appropriation account compares spending with what the legislature approved. Today Nigerian governments follow IPSAS, which calls it the statement of financial position. Memory tip: assets and liabilities together = balance sheet.",
 "|The items that are contained in the bought ledger control account are|dishonoured cheques, returns inwards and sales|returns outwards, bad debts and purchases|discount received, returns outwards and purchases|discount allowed, bad debts and purchases": "A control account is a summary of a whole ledger. The bought ledger (purchases ledger) holds the suppliers' accounts, so its control account carries only supplier items. On the credit side go the opening creditors and credit purchases (what we owe goes up); on the debit side go cash and cheques paid, discount received and returns outwards (what we owe goes down). Discount received, returns outwards and purchases are all supplier items: C. Option A lists sales ledger items (customers), and bad debts and discount allowed in B and D also belong to the sales ledger. Memory tip: bought ledger = suppliers = purchases, returns outwards, discount received.",
 "|A rent received by a trader for sub-letting part of his business premises should be added to the|total revenue|net profit|total profit|gross profit": "A trader's main income is from selling goods, and the trading account shows this as gross profit. Other income that does not come from trading, such as rent received from sub-letting, commission received or discount received, is added to gross profit at the top of the profit and loss account. Expenses are then deducted to give net profit. So rent received is added to gross profit: D. It is not added to net profit, because net profit is found only after it has been included. The trap: B, net profit, catches students who forget that other income comes in before expenses are deducted. Memory tip: gross profit + other income − expenses = net profit.",
 "|Use the following information: \nA piece of equipment costing ₦120,000 was purchased on 1/1/1996. Depreciation was provided at 20% per annum on a straight-line basis. It was sold on 30/6/1999 for ₦31,500.\n\nThe accumulated depreciation provision at the time of sale was|₦72,000|₦84,000|₦60,000|₦12,000": "Straight-line depreciation charges the same amount every year. Step 1: annual charge = 20% × ₦120,000 = ₦24,000. Step 2: time held = 1/1/1996 to 30/6/1999 = 3 full years (1996, 1997, 1998) plus 6 months of 1999 = 3½ years. Step 3: accumulated depreciation = ₦24,000 × 3.5 = ₦84,000: B. The trap: A (₦72,000) is what you get if you count only 3 full years and forget the half year before the sale. Memory tip: on a time basis, count months, not just whole years.",
 "|Use the following information: \nA piece of equipment costing ₦120,000 was purchased on 1/1/1996. Depreciation was provided at 20% per annum on a straight-line basis. It was sold on 30/6/1999 for ₦31,500.\n\nThe net book value of the piece of equipment at the time of sale was|₦24,000|₦96,000|₦48,000|₦36,000": "Net book value (NBV), also called written-down value or carrying amount, is cost less accumulated depreciation. Step 1: cost = ₦120,000. Step 2: accumulated depreciation to 30/6/1999 = ₦24,000 × 3.5 = ₦84,000. Step 3: NBV = ₦120,000 − ₦84,000 = ₦36,000: D. The equipment was sold for ₦31,500, so there was a loss on disposal of ₦36,000 − ₦31,500 = ₦4,500. The trap: C (₦48,000) is the NBV after only 3 years. Memory tip: NBV = cost − accumulated depreciation; proceeds − NBV = profit (or loss) on disposal.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPurchases ledger balance ... ₦4,000\nPurchases for the period ... ₦50,000\nCash refunded by suppliers ... ₦290\nCheques paid to suppliers ... ₦42,300\nReturns outwards ... ₦2,000\nDiscount received from suppliers ... ₦400\n\nThe balance of the purchases ledger control account is|₦9,590|₦9,010|₦10,390|₦9,990": "The purchases ledger control account follows the creditors' accounts: increases on the credit side, decreases on the debit side. Step 1, credit side: opening balance ₦4,000 + purchases ₦50,000 + cash refunded by suppliers ₦290 = ₦54,290 (a refund from a supplier is debited to cash and credited here). Step 2, debit side: cheques paid ₦42,300 + returns outwards ₦2,000 + discount received ₦400 = ₦44,700. Step 3: balance = ₦54,290 − ₦44,700 = ₦9,590 credit: A. The trap: B (₦9,010) is what you get by putting the ₦290 refund on the debit side. Memory tip: a refund from a supplier reverses a payment, so it goes on the opposite side to payments.",
 "|Use the following information: \nMusa Merchants Trading and Profit and Loss Account for the year ended Dec. 31st 2003\nStock (1/1/2003) ... ₦60,000\nPurchases ... ₦90,000\nCost of goods available for sale ... ₦150,000\nStock (31/12/2003) ... ?\nCost of goods sold ... ?\nGross profit ... ₦180,000\nTotal ... ₦290,000\nSales ... ₦290,000\n\nDetermine the closing stock|₦50,000|₦20,000|₦30,000|₦40,000": "Work backwards through the trading account. Step 1: cost of goods sold = sales − gross profit = ₦290,000 − ₦180,000 = ₦110,000. Step 2: cost of goods available for sale = opening stock + purchases = ₦60,000 + ₦90,000 = ₦150,000. Step 3: closing stock = goods available − cost of goods sold = ₦150,000 − ₦110,000 = ₦40,000: D. Check: ₦150,000 − ₦40,000 = ₦110,000; ₦290,000 − ₦110,000 = ₦180,000. Memory tip: opening stock + purchases − closing stock = cost of goods sold.",
 "|Use the following information: \n... Jan. 2003 ... Dec. 2003\nProvision for bad debt ... ₦1,000 ... –\nDebtors ... – ... ₦20,000\nBad debts to be written off ... – ... ₦2,000\n\nThe provision for bad debt stands at 10% of debtors. How much is to be charged to profit and loss account as provision for bad debt?|₦1,000|₦1,800|₦2,000|₦800": "The provision for bad debts is based on the debtors that remain after bad debts are written off, and only the change in the provision goes to profit and loss. Step 1: good debtors = ₦20,000 − ₦2,000 = ₦18,000. Step 2: new provision = 10% × ₦18,000 = ₦1,800. Step 3: old provision = ₦1,000, so the increase charged to profit and loss = ₦1,800 − ₦1,000 = ₦800: D. The ₦2,000 bad debts are charged separately as bad debts. The trap: A (₦1,000) comes from taking 10% of ₦20,000 before writing off the bad debts. Memory tip: write off first, then provide; charge only the increase.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Particulars ... Quantity (units) ... Unit price (₦)\nJan. ... Received ... 100 ... 2.00\nFeb. ... Received ... 110 ... 2.50\nMarch ... Issued ... 80\nApril ... Received ... 200 ... 3.00\nMay ... Issued ... 100\n\nDetermine the closing stock under LIFO method|₦375|₦575|₦275|₦500": "LIFO (last in, first out) issues the newest units first. Step 1: Jan 100 @ ₦2.00, Feb 110 @ ₦2.50. Step 2: March issue 80 comes from February, leaving 100 @ ₦2.00 and 30 @ ₦2.50. Step 3: April receipt 200 @ ₦3.00. Step 4: May issue 100 comes from April, leaving 100 @ ₦3.00. Step 5: closing stock = 100 × ₦2.00 + 30 × ₦2.50 + 100 × ₦3.00 = ₦200 + ₦75 + ₦300 = ₦575: B. Today IFRS (IAS 2) no longer allows LIFO, but it is still taught for comparison. Memory tip: LIFO = last in, first out, so the oldest prices stay in closing stock.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Particulars ... Quantity (units) ... Unit price (₦)\nJan. ... Received ... 100 ... 2.00\nFeb. ... Received ... 110 ... 2.50\nMarch ... Issued ... 80\nApril ... Received ... 200 ... 3.00\nMay ... Issued ... 100\n\nCalculate the closing stock under FIFO method.|₦300|₦600|₦875|₦675": "FIFO (first in, first out) issues the oldest units first. Step 1: total issued = 80 + 100 = 180 units. Step 2: these come from the oldest stock: all 100 Jan units @ ₦2.00 and 80 Feb units @ ₦2.50. Step 3: left are 30 Feb units @ ₦2.50 and 200 April units @ ₦3.00 (230 units). Step 4: closing stock = 30 × ₦2.50 + 200 × ₦3.00 = ₦75 + ₦600 = ₦675: D. The trap: B (₦600) counts only the April units and forgets the 30 February units still in stock. Memory tip: FIFO = first in, first out, so closing stock carries the latest prices.",
 "|Which of the following items is found on the credit side of the sales ledger control account?|dishonoured cheques|credit sales|bills receivable|discount received": "The sales ledger control account follows the customers (debtors). Items that increase what customers owe are debits: credit sales, dishonoured cheques, interest charged. Items that reduce what they owe are credits: cash and cheques received, discount allowed, returns inwards, bad debts written off and bills receivable accepted. When a customer gives a bill of exchange, his debt is replaced by the bill, so bills receivable are credited: C. Discount received belongs to the purchases ledger control account, not the sales ledger. Memory tip: anything that settles a customer's debt goes on the credit side of the sales ledger control.",
 "|Use the following information: \nA sole trader provided the following balances for the financial year ended 30th June, 2003.\nCapital ... ₦8,400\nNet profit ... ₦1,500\n(Subtotal ... ₦9,900)\nDrawings ... ₦900\n(Subtotal ... ₦9,000)\nCreditors ... ₦2,500\nAccruals ... ₦1,500\nTotal ... ₦13,000\nLand and Building ... ₦4,500\nFurniture ... ₦3,400\nStock ... ₦2,800\nDebtors ... ₦2,200\nCash ... ₦100\nTotal ... ₦13,000\n\nThe capital employed is|₦9,900|₦9,000|₦8,400|₦13,000": "Capital employed here means the owner's capital invested at the end of the year. Step 1: capital ₦8,400 + net profit ₦1,500 = ₦9,900. Step 2: less drawings ₦900 = ₦9,000. Step 3: check: total assets ₦13,000 − current liabilities (creditors ₦2,500 + accruals ₦1,500 = ₦4,000) = ₦9,000. So capital employed is ₦9,000: B. The trap: A (₦9,900) forgets to deduct drawings. Memory tip: closing capital = opening capital + profit − drawings = assets − current liabilities.",
 "|Goods withdrawn from business for private use is credited to|sales|capital|purchases|drawings": "When the owner takes goods for private use, the business loses stock it bought for resale, and the owner's claim falls. The entry is debit drawings (the owner has taken value) and credit purchases (to remove the goods from the cost of goods bought for resale), at cost price: C. Crediting sales would wrongly show a sale and a profit. Drawings is the account debited, not credited. Memory tip: goods for own use: Dr Drawings, Cr Purchases.",
 "|The head office of Emeka Stores maintains the financial accounts of all its branches. The branch stock account in the head office serves as the branch|trading account|debtor's account|cash account|expenses account": "When head office keeps all the branch records, it opens a branch stock account (often kept at selling price or cost), a branch debtors account and a branch expenses account. The branch stock account is debited with goods sent to the branch and credited with branch sales and closing stock; the balancing figure is the branch gross profit. That is exactly what a trading account does, so the branch stock account serves as the branch trading account: A. Branch debtors and expenses have their own accounts. Memory tip: branch stock account = branch trading account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nBalance Sheet (Extract)\nCapital ... ₦40,000\nNet profit ... ₦12,000\n(Subtotal ... ₦52,000)\nCreditors ... ₦8,000\nWages owed ... ₦4,000\nRent owed ... ₦3,000\nTotal ... ₦67,000\nBuilding ... ₦25,000\nEquipment ... ₦12,000\nStock ... ₦10,000\nDebtors ... ₦15,000\nCash ... ₦5,000\nTotal ... ₦67,000\n\nThe quick ratio is|1.7:1|3:1|1:2|1.3:1": "The quick (acid test) ratio leaves out stock, because stock takes time to turn into cash. Step 1: quick assets = debtors ₦15,000 + cash ₦5,000 = ₦20,000. Step 2: current liabilities = creditors ₦8,000 + wages owed ₦4,000 + rent owed ₦3,000 = ₦15,000. Step 3: quick ratio = ₦20,000 ÷ ₦15,000 ≈ 1.33, i.e. 1.3:1: D. Memory tip: quick ratio = (current assets − stock) ÷ current liabilities; about 1:1 is thought healthy.",
 "|Use the following information: \nBalance Sheet (Extract)\nCapital ... ₦40,000\nNet profit ... ₦12,000\n(Subtotal ... ₦52,000)\nCreditors ... ₦8,000\nWages owed ... ₦4,000\nRent owed ... ₦3,000\nTotal ... ₦67,000\nBuilding ... ₦25,000\nEquipment ... ₦12,000\nStock ... ₦10,000\nDebtors ... ₦15,000\nCash ... ₦5,000\nTotal ... ₦67,000\n\nThe current ratio is|3.5:1|1:1|2:1|2.8:1": "The current ratio compares all current assets with current liabilities. Step 1: current assets = stock ₦10,000 + debtors ₦15,000 + cash ₦5,000 = ₦30,000. Step 2: current liabilities = ₦8,000 + ₦4,000 + ₦3,000 = ₦15,000. Step 3: current ratio = ₦30,000 ÷ ₦15,000 = 2:1: C. Building and equipment are fixed assets and are left out. Memory tip: current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities; 2:1 is the textbook ideal.",
 "|The tier of government that uses financial memorandum is the|local government|federal government|municipal government|state government": "Each tier of government in Nigeria has its own financial rule book. The federal government uses the Financial Regulations, state governments use Financial Instructions (or their own regulations), and local governments use the Financial Memoranda, which set out how local councils collect revenue, spend, keep records and account for funds. So the tier that uses the Financial Memoranda is local government: A. \"Municipal government\" is not a separate tier in Nigeria. The trap: B, because federal \"Financial Regulations\" sound very similar. Memory tip: FG = Financial Regulations, LG = Financial Memoranda.",
 "|The movement of goods from head office to a branch is treated in the head office accounts as|requisition|purchases|issue|sale": "Goods that head office sends to its own branch are not sold, because ownership stays with the same business. Head office therefore records them as goods issued (sent) to the branch: debit branch account (or branch stock) and credit goods sent to branches account, which is later deducted from purchases in the head office trading account: C. Treating them as a sale would create a false profit. Memory tip: goods to a branch are moved, not sold.",
 "|A company paid cash in redemption of ₦10,000 debentures. The accounting entry to record this will be to debit|debenture and credit cash|capital and debit cash|cash and credit debenture|cash and credit capital": "A debenture is a long-term loan to a company, shown as a liability. When the company redeems (repays) it, the liability goes down and cash goes down. The entry is debit debentures account (reduce the liability) and credit cash (money paid out): A. Debentures are not part of share capital, so the capital account is not involved. Memory tip: borrowing = Dr Cash, Cr Debentures; repaying = Dr Debentures, Cr Cash.",
 "|Profit or loss in a partnership is usually arrived at after deducting from gross profit all expenses including|partners' drawings|interest on loans|interest on capital|partners' salaries": "Partnership net profit is found in the profit and loss account by deducting all business expenses from gross profit. Interest on a loan, including interest on a loan a partner has made to the firm, is a business expense, so it is charged before net profit: B. Partners' drawings, interest on capital and partners' salaries are not expenses; they are ways of sharing the profit and appear in the appropriation account and the current accounts. The trap: D (partners' salaries) looks like an ordinary wages expense but is an appropriation of profit. Memory tip: loan interest is an expense; capital interest and salaries are shares of profit.",
 "|Earnings per share is a measure of|leverage|liquidity|profitability|solvency": "Earnings per share (EPS) = profit after tax and preference dividends ÷ number of ordinary shares in issue. It tells shareholders how much profit the company earned for each share they hold, so it measures profitability: C. Liquidity is measured by the current and quick ratios, leverage (gearing) by debt compared with equity, and solvency by the ability to meet long-term debts. Memory tip: EPS, P/E and ROCE are profitability (investor) ratios.",
 "|In reconciling the branch and head office accounts, remittance in transit in the branch books is treated as a|contra entry|debit entry|reversal entry|credit entry": "When a branch sends cash to head office, the branch credits cash and debits the head office current account. If head office has not yet received it at the year end, the two current accounts disagree. In the branch books the remittance is already there as a debit entry in the head office account; in reconciling, head office brings it into its books by debiting cash in transit and crediting the branch account. Nothing is reversed, so B (debit entry) is the best answer. JAMB's key gives C (reversal entry), so this one is debatable. Memory tip: items in transit are adjusted in the books of the receiver, usually head office.",
 "|When assets are realized during a partnership dissolution, the entries for the proceeds are to debit|asset account and credit cash account|realisation account and credit cash account|asset account and credit realisation account|cash account and credit realisation account": "On dissolution, assets are transferred to a realisation account at book value. When they are sold, the cash received is debited to cash (or bank), because money comes in, and credited to the realisation account, which compares the proceeds with the book values to find the profit or loss on realisation: D. Option C credits realisation correctly but debits the asset account, which has already been closed. Memory tip: proceeds: Dr Cash, Cr Realisation.",
 "|The accounting method that reports incomes when earned and expenses when incurred is called|accrual accounting|fund accounting|cash accounting|commitment accounting": "Accrual accounting records income in the period it is earned and expenses in the period they are incurred, regardless of when cash changes hands. This is why we adjust for accruals and prepayments at the year end: A. Cash accounting records only cash received and paid. Fund accounting is used by governments and non-profit bodies to keep separate funds, and commitment accounting records orders when they are committed. Memory tip: accrual = earned and incurred; cash basis = received and paid.",
 "|Use the following information: \nJide and Haruna contribute ₦12,000 each as capital and share profit equally. Jide draws ₦1,500 and Haruna ₦1,700 half-yearly. The profit for the year is ₦10,000. Jide receives a salary of ₦2,000.\n\nCalculate the profit available for appropriation|₦7,000|₦12,000|₦10,000|₦8,000": "In the appropriation account we start with net profit of ₦10,000. Step 1: deduct Jide's salary ₦2,000 (there is no interest on capital or drawings given). Step 2: ₦10,000 − ₦2,000 = ₦8,000 is the profit left to be shared equally, the figure the question calls the profit available for appropriation: D. But a partner's salary is itself an appropriation of profit, so strictly the whole ₦10,000 is available for appropriation (C) and ₦8,000 is the residual profit; both readings are accepted. Memory tip: net profit − salaries − interest on capital + interest on drawings = residue to share.",
 "|Use the following information: \nJide and Haruna contribute ₦12,000 each as capital and share profit equally. Jide draws ₦1,500 and Haruna ₦1,700 half-yearly. The profit for the year is ₦10,000. Jide receives a salary of ₦2,000.\n\nThe balances of current account of Jide and Haruna are respectively|₦4,000 and ₦4,000|₦6,000 and ₦5,000|₦3,000 and ₦600|₦3,000 and ₦3,400": "Step 1: residual profit = ₦10,000 − ₦2,000 salary = ₦8,000, shared equally: ₦4,000 each. Step 2: drawings are half-yearly, so for the year Jide draws ₦1,500 × 2 = ₦3,000 and Haruna ₦1,700 × 2 = ₦3,400. Step 3: Jide's current account = salary ₦2,000 + share ₦4,000 − drawings ₦3,000 = ₦3,000. Step 4: Haruna's = ₦4,000 − ₦3,400 = ₦600: C. The trap: D (₦3,000 and ₦3,400) mixes up Haruna's drawings with his balance. Memory tip: half-yearly drawings means two lots in a year; double them.",
 "|The concept that has much influence over asset valuation and income determination is|matching|conservatism|entity|realisation": "Conservatism (prudence) tells the accountant to anticipate no profit but provide for all known losses. It affects asset valuation (stock at the lower of cost and net realisable value, provisions for bad debts, depreciation) and income determination (profits are not recognised until realised), so it has much influence over both: B. Matching mainly concerns income measurement, realisation concerns when revenue is recognised, and entity separates the business from its owner. Under IFRS today prudence is described as caution, not deliberate understatement. Memory tip: prudence = \"never overstate assets or profit\".",
 "|Use the following information: \nRent ... ₦10,000\nRates ... ₦5,000\nCapital ... ₦50,000\nSalaries ... ₦14,000\nCash ... ₦26,000\nCreditors ... ₦5,000\n\nThe total trial balance is|₦55,000|₦45,000|₦65,000|₦110,000": "A trial balance lists debit balances (assets and expenses) against credit balances (liabilities, capital and income). Step 1, debits: rent ₦10,000 + rates ₦5,000 + salaries ₦14,000 + cash ₦26,000 = ₦55,000. Step 2, credits: capital ₦50,000 + creditors ₦5,000 = ₦55,000. Step 3: the two sides agree, so the trial balance totals ₦55,000: A. The trap: D (₦110,000) adds both sides together. Memory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits = Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits = Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|Yahya's bank account shows a credit balance of ₦1,500,000. He paid ₦1,700,000 into the bank. What is his expected balance?|debit balance of ₦3,200,000|credit balance of ₦3,200,000|credit balance of ₦200,000|debit balance of ₦200,000": "\"Yahya's bank account\" is the account the bank keeps for him. In the bank's books a customer's money is a liability of the bank, so a credit balance means Yahya has ₦1,500,000 in the bank. Paying in ₦1,700,000 increases what the bank owes him: ₦1,500,000 + ₦1,700,000 = ₦3,200,000 credit balance: B. The trap: D (debit ₦200,000) is the result if you read the credit balance as an overdraft in Yahya's own cash book, where the sides are the other way round. Memory tip: the bank statement is the mirror image of your cash book.",
 "|The double entry principle of accounting was developed by|Luca Pacioli|Frank Wood|William Pickles|Akintola Williams": "Luca Pacioli, an Italian Franciscan friar and mathematician, published \"Summa de arithmetica\" in Venice in 1494. One section described the Venetian method of double-entry bookkeeping, so he is called the father of accounting: A. Frank Wood wrote the well-known \"Business Accounting\" textbooks, and Akintola Williams founded the first indigenous Nigerian accounting firm (1952) and helped found ICAN. Memory tip: Pacioli, 1494, Venice: every debit has a credit.",
 "|What are the appropriate recording procedures for entries in the trial balance?|cash account, ledgers and trial balance|source documents, ledgers and trial balance|ledgers, trial balance and source documents|ledgers, source documents and trial balance": "The accounting cycle starts with source documents (invoices, receipts, credit notes) as evidence of transactions. They are entered in the books of original entry, posted to the ledgers, and the ledger balances are extracted into the trial balance: B. Options C and D contain the same three items in the wrong order, and A starts with the cash account instead of the source documents. Memory tip: Document → Day book → Ledger → Trial balance → Final accounts.",
 "|Use the following information: \nFive hundred naira monthly is allowed on an imprest system. The following transactions took place.\nPeriod 1:\nCash advanced to petty cashier ... ₦500\nPetty cashier paid out: Stamp ... ₦200\nNewspaper ... ₦250\nPeriod 2:\nCash to petty cashier ... ₦450\nCashier paid for writing materials ... ₦350\n\nHow much should the petty cashier receive after period 2?|₦350|₦500|₦150|₦250": "Under the imprest system the petty cashier starts each period with a fixed float, here ₦500, and is reimbursed exactly what was spent. Period 1: ₦500 − ₦450 spent = ₦50; the ₦450 given at the start of period 2 restores the float to ₦500. Period 2: ₦350 spent on writing materials, leaving ₦150. To restore the ₦500 float after period 2, the petty cashier should receive ₦350: A. The trap: C (₦150) is the cash in hand, not the amount needed. Memory tip: imprest reimbursement = amount spent.",
 "|Use the following information: \nFive hundred naira monthly is allowed on an imprest system. The following transactions took place.\nPeriod 1:\nCash advanced to petty cashier ... ₦500\nPetty cashier paid out: Stamp ... ₦200\nNewspaper ... ₦250\nPeriod 2:\nCash to petty cashier ... ₦450\nCashier paid for writing materials ... ₦350\n\nWhat was the balance of cash with the petty cashier at the end of period 1?|₦200|₦450|₦50|₦30": "Step 1: the petty cashier received ₦500 at the start of period 1. Step 2: payments were stamps ₦200 + newspaper ₦250 = ₦450. Step 3: balance at the end of period 1 = ₦500 − ₦450 = ₦50: C. This matches the ₦450 reimbursed at the start of period 2, which brings the float back to ₦500. The trap: B (₦450) is the amount spent and reimbursed, not the cash left. Memory tip: float − spending = cash in hand.",
 "|Use the following information: \nLiabilities ... ₦23,700\nCurrent assets ... ₦20,300\nFixed assets ... ₦64,500\n\nWhat is the capital introduced by the proprietor?|₦88,200|₦40,800|₦108,500|₦61,100": "The accounting equation is Assets = Capital + Liabilities, so Capital = Assets − Liabilities. Step 1: total assets = fixed assets ₦64,500 + current assets ₦20,300 = ₦84,800. Step 2: capital = ₦84,800 − ₦23,700 = ₦61,100: D. The trap: B (₦40,800) forgets the current assets and works with fixed assets only. Memory tip: capital is what is left for the owner after paying everyone else.",
 "|Mr. Bassey purchased a motor vehicle for use in his business and debited the purchases account with the same value. This is an error of|principle|omission|original entry|commission": "An error of principle occurs when an amount is posted to the right side of the wrong class of account, such as treating capital expenditure as revenue expenditure. A motor vehicle for use in the business is a fixed asset, but it was debited to purchases (an expense for goods for resale). The debit is still a debit, so the trial balance agrees, but the class of account is wrong: A. An error of commission is a wrong account of the same class (e.g. the wrong customer). The trap: D, commission, because both put the amount in the wrong account. Memory tip: wrong class = principle; wrong person in the same class = commission.",
 "|In a three-column cash book, the discount allowed is shown on the|credit side|memorandum column|debit side|folio column": "A three-column cash book has discount, cash and bank columns on each side. Discount allowed is given to customers when they pay, so it is written beside the receipt on the debit side; its total is posted to the debit of discount allowed account. Discount received sits on the credit side, beside payments to suppliers: C. The discount columns are memorandum columns, but the question asks which side. Memory tip: Allowed with receipts (debit); Received with payments (credit).",
 "|The journal entries for the purchase of stock on credit are to debit|suspense account and cash account|purchase account and credit liability account|stock account and credit cash account|stock account and credit liability account": "In the traditional (periodic) system used in Nigerian syllabuses, goods bought for resale are debited to purchases account, not to stock. Buying on credit creates a liability to the supplier, so the supplier's (creditor's) account is credited: B. C would be a cash purchase. The trap: D (stock and liability) is close, because in a perpetual system goods can be debited to an inventory account, but the purchases account is the standard answer. Memory tip: credit purchase: Dr Purchases, Cr Creditor.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscription ... ₦5,240\nCanopy rent ... ₦400\nWages ... ₦1,100\nPostages ... ₦300\nLoss from bar ... ₦160\nPurchase of new furniture ... ₦120\nPremises ... ₦4,000\nFurniture at the beginning ... ₦1,120\n\nWhat is the cash balance in the receipts and payments account?|₦4,120|₦2,520|₦2,600|₦3,000": "A receipts and payments account is a summary of cash only. Step 1, receipts: subscriptions ₦5,240 + canopy rent ₦400 = ₦5,640. Step 2, cash payments: wages ₦1,100 + postages ₦300 + new furniture ₦120 = ₦1,520. Step 3: balance = ₦5,640 − ₦1,520 = ₦4,120: A. The loss from bar is not a payment; it is worked out in the bar trading account and goes to the income and expenditure account. Premises and opening furniture are assets, not cash. Memory tip: receipts and payments = cash only.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscription ... ₦5,240\nCanopy rent ... ₦400\nWages ... ₦1,100\nPostages ... ₦300\nLoss from bar ... ₦160\nPurchase of new furniture ... ₦120\nPremises ... ₦4,000\nFurniture at the beginning ... ₦1,120\n\nDetermine the accumulated fund value.|₦6,320|₦4,200|₦3,000|₦9,360": "The accumulated fund is the club's equivalent of capital: total assets less liabilities. With no liabilities given, add up the assets at the end of the year. Step 1: cash ₦4,120 (from the receipts and payments account). Step 2: premises ₦4,000. Step 3: furniture ₦1,120 + new furniture ₦120 = ₦1,240. Step 4: total = ₦4,120 + ₦4,000 + ₦1,240 = ₦9,360: D. Memory tip: accumulated fund = assets − liabilities, just like a trader's capital.",
 "|Gamji Memorial Club carried out an activity which yielded a profit of ₦10,000. This amount is to be transferred to the|credit side of its profit and loss account|debit side of its profit and loss account|credit side of its income and expenditure account|debit side of its income and expenditure account": "Not-for-profit organisations such as clubs prepare an income and expenditure account, not a profit and loss account. A profit from a club activity (a dance, a bar, a raffle) is worked out separately and the net profit is income, so it is entered on the credit side of the income and expenditure account: C. A loss would go on the debit side. Memory tip: clubs have income and expenditure, surplus and deficit, accumulated fund, not profit and loss and capital.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDirect labour ... ₦50,000\nBeginning work-in-progress ... ₦4,000\nRaw materials used ... ₦18,000\nIndirect labour ... ₦4,000\nEnding finished goods ... ₦16,000\nEnding work-in-progress ... ₦9,000\nFactory supply used ... ₦7,000\nSelling expenses ... ₦25,000\nBeginning finished goods ... ₦21,000\nOther factory overheads ... ₦19,000\n\nWhat is the cost of goods manufactured?|₦97,000|₦117,000|₦93,000|₦104,000": "Step 1: prime cost = raw materials used ₦18,000 + direct labour ₦50,000 = ₦68,000. Step 2: factory overheads = indirect labour ₦4,000 + factory supply ₦7,000 + other overheads ₦19,000 = ₦30,000. Step 3: ₦68,000 + ₦30,000 = ₦98,000. Step 4: add opening WIP ₦4,000 and deduct closing WIP ₦9,000: ₦98,000 + ₦4,000 − ₦9,000 = ₦93,000: C. Selling expenses and finished goods stocks belong in the trading and profit and loss account. Memory tip: prime cost + factory overheads + opening WIP − closing WIP = cost of production.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDirect labour ... ₦50,000\nBeginning work-in-progress ... ₦4,000\nRaw materials used ... ₦18,000\nIndirect labour ... ₦4,000\nEnding finished goods ... ₦16,000\nEnding work-in-progress ... ₦9,000\nFactory supply used ... ₦7,000\nSelling expenses ... ₦25,000\nBeginning finished goods ... ₦21,000\nOther factory overheads ... ₦19,000\n\nCalculate the prime cost.|₦75,000|₦70,000|₦50,000|₦68,000": "Prime cost is the total of direct costs: direct materials, direct labour and direct expenses. Step 1: raw materials used ₦18,000. Step 2: direct labour ₦50,000. Step 3: prime cost = ₦18,000 + ₦50,000 = ₦68,000: D. Indirect labour, factory supplies and other factory overheads are added after prime cost. The trap: A (₦75,000) adds factory supplies, an overhead, into prime cost. Memory tip: Prime = direct only.",
 "|If the assets and liabilities of a firm were valued at ₦17,200 and ₦5,120 respectively, the capital of the firm would be|₦5,120|₦12,080|₦17,200|₦22,320": "Using the accounting equation, Capital = Assets − Liabilities. Step 1: assets ₦17,200. Step 2: liabilities ₦5,120. Step 3: capital = ₦17,200 − ₦5,120 = ₦12,080: B. Capital is the owner's claim on the business, what would be left for the owner if every asset were turned into cash at these values and every creditor were paid. A (₦5,120) is just the liabilities and C (₦17,200) just the assets. The trap: D (₦22,320) adds the two instead of subtracting. Memory tip: A = C + L, so C = A − L.",
 "|A company incurred ₦21,000 in running its four departments, namely J, K, L and M with a corresponding number of employees as 1500, 2000, 3000 and 500 respectively. What is the share of department K?|₦6,000|₦1,500|₦9,000|₦4,500": "Costs are shared among departments on a fair basis; for staff-related costs this is the number of employees. Step 1: total employees = 1,500 + 2,000 + 3,000 + 500 = 7,000. Step 2: K's share = 2,000/7,000 × ₦21,000 = ₦6,000: A. Check: J ₦4,500, K ₦6,000, L ₦9,000, M ₦1,500, total ₦21,000. The trap: D (₦4,500) is J's share, and C (₦9,000) is L's. Memory tip: apportion = department's units ÷ total units × cost.",
 "|The not-for-profit-making organisations generate their income mainly from|pools|bar sales|subscriptions|rent": "Clubs, societies and associations exist to serve members, not to make profit. Their main and regular source of income is members' subscriptions (annual dues and entrance fees): C. Bar sales, rents, donations, pools and fund-raising events add to income but are secondary; a bar is run as a separate trading activity, and its profit or loss is only one line in the income and expenditure account. Subscriptions due but unpaid at the year end are shown as subscriptions in arrears, and those paid early as subscriptions in advance. Memory tip: no members' subscriptions, no club.",
 "|Use the following information: \nZazzau Company\nCash in hand ... ₦50\nCash in bank ... ₦1,250\nStock ... ₦4,880\nBuilding ... ₦5,500\nDebtors ... ₦1,750\nCreditors ... ₦2,150\nFurniture ... ₦960\nDrawings of ₦125 were made by the shareholders of the company.\n\nThe capital of the company stands at|₦2,510|₦2,150|₦12,115|₦12,240": "Capital is always assets less liabilities. Step 1: assets = cash in hand ₦50 + cash in bank ₦1,250 + stock ₦4,880 + building ₦5,500 + debtors ₦1,750 + furniture ₦960 = ₦14,390. Step 2: liabilities = creditors ₦2,150. Step 3: capital = ₦14,390 − ₦2,150 = ₦12,240: D. The drawings of ₦125 have already been made, so they are reflected in these balances and are not deducted again. The trap: C (₦12,115) deducts the ₦125 drawings a second time. Memory tip: capital = assets − liabilities, always.",
 "|Use the following information: \nZazzau Company\nCash in hand ... ₦50\nCash in bank ... ₦1,250\nStock ... ₦4,880\nBuilding ... ₦5,500\nDebtors ... ₦1,750\nCreditors ... ₦2,150\nFurniture ... ₦960\nDrawings of ₦125 were made by the shareholders of the company.\n\nThe company's net current assets amount to|₦5,870|₦6,580|₦7,930|₦5,780": "Net current assets (working capital) = current assets − current liabilities. Step 1: current assets = cash in hand ₦50 + cash in bank ₦1,250 + stock ₦4,880 + debtors ₦1,750 = ₦7,930. Step 2: current liabilities = creditors ₦2,150. Step 3: ₦7,930 − ₦2,150 = ₦5,780: D. Building and furniture are fixed assets and are left out. The trap: A (₦5,870) swaps two digits, and C (₦7,930) is total current assets before deducting creditors. Memory tip: \"net\" means after taking away current liabilities.",
 "|Departmental accounts are prepared to aid the comparison of the|daily profits|previous year's transactions|management performances|workers performances": "Departmental accounts split a business's trading and profit and loss account into one column for each department, sharing expenses on fair bases. This shows which departments are profitable, so management can compare the performance of each department and the staff working in it, and reward them (for example by commission): D. They do not show daily profits, and comparison with previous years is a general use of all accounts. Memory tip: departmental accounts answer \"which section is doing well?\".",
 "|A component of an overhead manufacturing cost is|labour|rent|cost of work-in-progress|cost of finished goods": "Manufacturing overheads (factory overheads) are the indirect costs of production that cannot be traced to a particular unit: factory rent, factory power, indirect wages, depreciation of plant. Rent of the factory is therefore a component of overhead: B. Labour on its own is usually direct labour, part of prime cost. Work-in-progress and finished goods are stocks, adjusted in the accounts but not overheads. Memory tip: if it keeps the factory running but is not in the product itself, it is an overhead.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPurchases: Raw materials ... ₦15,000\nFinished goods ... ₦11,400\nStock (1/7/01): Raw materials ... ₦2,250\nWork-in-progress ... ₦1,875\nWages: Direct ... ₦17,100\nIndirect ... ₦5,400\nStock (30/06/02): Raw materials ... ₦3,000\nWork-in-progress ... ₦3,375\n\nWhat is the cost of manufactured goods?|₦48,750|₦49,125|₦35,250|₦37,625": "Step 1: raw materials consumed = ₦2,250 + ₦15,000 − ₦3,000 = ₦14,250. Step 2: prime cost = ₦14,250 + direct wages ₦17,100 = ₦31,350. Step 3: add indirect wages ₦5,400 (factory overhead) = ₦36,750. Step 4: add opening WIP ₦1,875 and deduct closing WIP ₦3,375: ₦36,750 + ₦1,875 − ₦3,375 = ₦35,250: C. Finished goods purchases go to the trading account. Memory tip: WIP: add the opening, deduct the closing.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPurchases: Raw materials ... ₦15,000\nFinished goods ... ₦11,400\nStock (1/7/01): Raw materials ... ₦2,250\nWork-in-progress ... ₦1,875\nWages: Direct ... ₦17,100\nIndirect ... ₦5,400\nStock (30/06/02): Raw materials ... ₦3,000\nWork-in-progress ... ₦3,375\n\nThe cost of raw materials consumed is|₦15,000|₦14,250|₦15,750|₦17,250": "Raw materials consumed (used) = opening stock of raw materials + purchases of raw materials − closing stock of raw materials. Step 1: ₦2,250 + ₦15,000 = ₦17,250 available. Step 2: ₦17,250 − ₦3,000 = ₦14,250: B. Finished goods purchases are not raw materials. The trap: D (₦17,250) forgets to deduct the closing stock. Memory tip: opening + purchases − closing = used, for any stock.",
 "|Fatima withdraws goods from the business for personal use. The accounting treatment is to debit|stock account and credit profit and loss account|drawings account and credit stock account|profit and loss account and credit drawings account|stock account and credit drawings account": "When the owner takes goods out of the business for private use, two things happen. The owner's claim on the business falls, which is drawings, so drawings account is debited. The business also has fewer goods for sale, so the goods are credited out, usually to purchases account (some books say stock account). The paper's version is debit drawings, credit stock: B. Profit and loss is not touched, because taking goods is not an expense of running the business. The trap: D reverses the entry (debit stock, credit drawings), which is what students write when they forget that drawings is always debited. Memory tip: owner takes, drawings debits; goods leave, purchases/stock credits.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOn January 1, 2005, a machine was bought for ₦56,000 to last for 5 years with a residual value of ₦1,000.\n\nCalculate the yearly depreciation expense using the straight line method.|11,000|11,200|11,400|11,300": "The straight line method charges the same amount of depreciation every year over the asset's useful life.\nStep 1: Depreciable amount = cost − residual value = ₦56,000 − ₦1,000 = ₦55,000.\nStep 2: Yearly charge = ₦55,000 ÷ 5 years = ₦11,000: A.\nThe trap: B (₦11,200) is ₦56,000 ÷ 5, which is what you get if you forget to deduct the residual (scrap) value first. Memory tip: straight line = (Cost − Scrap) ÷ Life, the same every year.",
 "|Use the following information: \nOn January 1, 2005, a machine was bought for ₦56,000 to last for 5 years with a residual value of ₦1,000.\n\nThe rate of the yearly depreciation expense would be|50%|40%|30%|20%": "The depreciation rate under the straight line method is the yearly charge as a percentage of the amount to be written off.\nStep 1: Yearly charge = ₦11,000 (from the previous question).\nStep 2: Rate = ₦11,000 ÷ ₦55,000 × 100 = 20%.\nA quicker way: a life of 5 years means one-fifth is written off each year, and 1/5 = 20%: D. A 50% rate would suit a 2-year life, 40% a 2½-year life. Memory tip: rate = 100% ÷ useful life (5 years → 20%, 4 years → 25%, 10 years → 10%).",
 "|The objective of the purchases ledger control account is to establish the|credit purchases|net profit|total purchases|gross profit": "A purchases ledger control account (creditors' control account) summarises all transactions with credit suppliers in total: credit purchases from the purchases day book on the credit side, and payments, returns outwards and discounts received on the debit side. Its balance shows the total owed to creditors and checks the accuracy of the purchases ledger. Because cash purchases never pass through a supplier's account, the control account can only establish credit purchases, not total purchases: A. This answer is debatable because the question is loosely worded, but \"total purchases\" would wrongly include cash purchases. Net and gross profit come from the final accounts. Memory tip: control accounts deal only with CREDIT transactions with suppliers or customers.",
 "|The objective of a trading account is to establish the|cost of goods sold|gross profit|manufacturing profit|net profit": "Final accounts are prepared in stages. The trading account matches net sales against the cost of goods sold (opening stock + purchases + carriage inwards − closing stock) to find the gross profit: B. The profit and loss account then deducts expenses from gross profit to find the net profit, and a manufacturing account finds the cost of production (not \"manufacturing profit\", unless goods are transferred at a mark-up). Cost of goods sold is worked out inside the trading account, but it is a step, not its objective. Memory tip: Trading → Gross; Profit and Loss → Net.",
 "|A seller of vegetables and fruits should be encouraged to value his stock at the end of the period using the|weighted average method|simple average method|FIFO method|LIFO method": "FIFO (First In, First Out) assumes the goods bought first are sold first, so closing stock is made up of the latest purchases. For perishable goods like vegetables and fruits this matches what actually happens: a trader sells the older produce before it spoils, so the method gives a realistic value for what is left: C. LIFO would assume the freshest goods were sold and the oldest left, which is unrealistic for perishables, and averages blur the actual flow. Stock is now called inventory under IFRS, and IAS 2 still allows FIFO. Memory tip: perishables? First In, First Out, before they rot.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦?\nProfit and loss ... ₦3,500\nCurrent assets ... ₦?\nCurrent liabilities ... ₦2,000\nFixed assets ... ₦11,000\nThe company maintains a current ratio of 3:1.\n\nThe capital balance is|₦9,500|₦12,500|₦11,500|₦10,000": "Use the balance sheet equation: capital + profit = fixed assets + current assets − current liabilities.\nStep 1: Current ratio 3:1 means current assets = 3 × ₦2,000 = ₦6,000.\nStep 2: Net assets = ₦11,000 + ₦6,000 − ₦2,000 = ₦15,000.\nStep 3: Capital + ₦3,500 = ₦15,000, so capital = ₦11,500: C.\nCheck: capital ₦11,500 + profit ₦3,500 + current liabilities ₦2,000 = ₦17,000 = fixed assets ₦11,000 + current assets ₦6,000. Memory tip: Capital + Profit = Net assets.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦?\nProfit and loss ... ₦3,500\nCurrent assets ... ₦?\nCurrent liabilities ... ₦2,000\nFixed assets ... ₦11,000\nThe company maintains a current ratio of 3:1.\n\nDetermine the current assets.|₦7,000|₦6,000|₦8,000|₦9,000": "The current ratio compares current assets with current liabilities: current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities.\nStep 1: Ratio 3:1 means current assets are three times current liabilities.\nStep 2: Current assets = 3 × ₦2,000 = ₦6,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦8,000 (C) is what you get by adding the ₦2,000 liabilities to ₦6,000, as if the ratio described the total rather than a comparison. Memory tip: a 3:1 current ratio = ₦3 of current assets for every ₦1 of current liabilities.",
 "|The difference in the sales ledger control account is transferred to the|profit and loss account|sales account|balance sheet|trading account": "A sales ledger control account (debtors' control account) collects in total all the entries made in the individual debtors' accounts. When it is balanced, the difference between its two sides is the total amount still owed by credit customers, the closing debtors. That balance is carried down and shown under current assets in the balance sheet: C. The sales account and trading account deal with the total sales for the period, not the amount still owing, and profit and loss only receives items such as bad debts. The balance sheet is now called the statement of financial position. Memory tip: the balance of a control account is a person-type balance, so it goes to the balance sheet.",
 "|Tunde purchased goods from Femi by cash worth ₦1,000 with 10% cash discount. Tunde's purchases ledger control account is to be|debited by ₦100|credited by ₦100|credited by ₦1,000|debited by ₦1,000": "A cash discount is a reduction allowed for prompt payment. To the buyer (Tunde) it is discount received.\nStep 1: Discount = 10% × ₦1,000 = ₦100.\nStep 2: Strictly, a purchase paid for in cash never enters the purchases ledger control account, since Tunde never owed Femi anything, so no option is fully correct. The question means the discount received on a supplier's account: discount received reduces what is owed, so it is debited to the purchases ledger control account (and credited to discount received account): A.\nThe trap: B (credited by ₦100) treats the discount as if it increased the debt; in the creditors' control account, purchases are credited and anything that reduces the debt (payments, returns, discounts received) is debited. Memory tip: in the creditors' control account, whatever cuts the debt goes on the debit side.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Stock ... Qty (units) ... Cost/Price (₦)\nJul. ... Bal. b/d ... 2,000 ... 2.00\nAug. ... Purchases ... 10,000 ... 2.50\nSept. ... Purchases ... 20,000 ... 3.00\nOct. ... Sales ... 8,000 ... 3.50\nNov. ... Sales ... 23,000 ... 4.00\n\nAssuming the closing stock was valued at ₦19,000 at the end of November, what was the gross profit?|₦30,000|₦50,000|₦70,000|₦100,000": "Step 1: Sales = 8,000 × ₦3.50 + 23,000 × ₦4.00 = ₦28,000 + ₦92,000 = ₦120,000.\nStep 2: Opening stock = 2,000 × ₦2 = ₦4,000; purchases = 10,000 × ₦2.50 + 20,000 × ₦3 = ₦25,000 + ₦60,000 = ₦85,000.\nStep 3: Cost of goods sold = ₦4,000 + ₦85,000 − ₦19,000 = ₦70,000.\nStep 4: Gross profit = ₦120,000 − ₦70,000 = ₦50,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦70,000 (C) is the cost of goods sold, not the gross profit. Memory tip: Gross profit = Sales − (Opening stock + Purchases − Closing stock).",
 "|Use the following information: \nDate ... Stock ... Qty (units) ... Cost/Price (₦)\nJul. ... Bal. b/d ... 2,000 ... 2.00\nAug. ... Purchases ... 10,000 ... 2.50\nSept. ... Purchases ... 20,000 ... 3.00\nOct. ... Sales ... 8,000 ... 3.50\nNov. ... Sales ... 23,000 ... 4.00\n\nDetermine the value of closing stock using the LIFO method.|₦2,000|₦2,500|₦4,000|₦3,500": "Step 1: Units available = 2,000 + 10,000 + 20,000 = 32,000; units sold = 8,000 + 23,000 = 31,000; units left = 1,000.\nStep 2: Under LIFO (Last In, First Out) the latest goods are issued first. October's 8,000 come from September's batch; November's 23,000 take the remaining 12,000 at ₦3, all 10,000 at ₦2.50 and 1,000 of the opening stock.\nStep 3: The 1,000 units left are from the opening stock at ₦2.00 = ₦2,000: A.\nThe trap: ₦2,500 (B) values the 1,000 units at the August price. Today IAS 2 (used in Nigeria under IFRS) no longer allows LIFO. Memory tip: LIFO leaves the OLDEST prices in closing stock.",
 "|After receiving the financial statement, the Auditor-General of the Federation must submit a report to the National Assembly within|60 days|30 days|120 days|90 days": "Section 85 of the 1999 Constitution of Nigeria sets out the work of the Auditor-General for the Federation. The Accountant-General prepares the financial statements of the Federation, and under section 85(5) the Auditor-General must, within 90 days of receiving them, submit his report to each House of the National Assembly, where the Public Accounts Committees examine it: D. The same 90-day rule applies to a State Auditor-General and the House of Assembly (section 125(5)). Memory tip: Auditor-General, Ninety days, National Assembly.",
 "|Use the following information: \nGross earnings ... ₦950,000\nCurrent liabilities ... ₦100,000\nOperating expenses ... ₦350,000\n10% debentures ... ₦400,000\nShareholders' funds ... ₦1,300,000\nCorporate tax is 30%\n\nFrom the data above, calculate the returns on equity.|33%|35%|20%|30%": "Return on equity = profit after interest and tax ÷ shareholders' funds × 100.\nStep 1: Operating profit = ₦950,000 − ₦350,000 = ₦600,000.\nStep 2: Debenture interest = 10% × ₦400,000 = ₦40,000, so profit before tax = ₦560,000.\nStep 3: Tax at 30% = ₦168,000, so profit after tax = ₦392,000.\nStep 4: ROE = ₦392,000 ÷ ₦1,300,000 × 100 = 30.2% ≈ 30%: D.\nThe trap: 33% (A) comes from forgetting the debenture interest (₦420,000 ÷ ₦1,300,000 = 32.3%). Current liabilities are not needed. Memory tip: equity holders are paid last, after lenders and tax.",
 "|Use the following information: \nTurnover ... ₦120,000\nNet profit before tax ... ₦40,000\nNet profit b/f ... ₦68,000\nProposed dividends:\n  Ordinary shares ... ₦8,000\n  Preference shares ... ₦6,000\nGeneral reserves ... ₦10,000\nCorporate tax is 50%\n\nThe undistributed profit carried forward is|₦68,000|₦20,000|₦74,000|₦34,000": "The appropriation account shares out the profit after tax.\nStep 1: Tax = 50% × ₦40,000 = ₦20,000; profit after tax = ₦20,000.\nStep 2: Add profit brought forward: ₦20,000 + ₦68,000 = ₦88,000.\nStep 3: Deduct proposed dividends (₦8,000 + ₦6,000 = ₦14,000) and the transfer to general reserve (₦10,000): ₦88,000 − ₦24,000 = ₦64,000.\n₦64,000 is not among the options. JAMB's answer is ₦68,000: A, but that is just the profit brought forward, so it is not fully correct; ₦74,000 would come only if the general reserve were not a transfer this year. Memory tip: profit after tax + balance b/f − dividends − reserves = balance c/f.",
 "|Use the following information: \nTurnover ... ₦120,000\nNet profit before tax ... ₦40,000\nNet profit b/f ... ₦68,000\nProposed dividends:\n  Ordinary shares ... ₦8,000\n  Preference shares ... ₦6,000\nGeneral reserves ... ₦10,000\nCorporate tax is 50%\n\nWhat is the tax charged to profit and loss account?|₦14,000|₦60,000|₦34,000|₦20,000": "Companies pay corporate (company income) tax on their profit, and the charge for the year is shown in the profit and loss account after net profit before tax.\nStep 1: Net profit before tax = ₦40,000.\nStep 2: Tax = 50% × ₦40,000 = ₦20,000: D.\nTurnover is not the tax base, so 50% × ₦120,000 = ₦60,000 (B) is wrong; ₦14,000 is the total proposed dividend. The tax is deducted before dividends and reserves are appropriated. Memory tip: tax comes off profit before tax, never off turnover.",
 "|Accrual accounting differs from cash accounting because it recognizes|cash and creditors|debtors and creditors|cash receipts and cash payments|prepayment and cash": "Under cash accounting, income is recorded only when cash is received and expenses only when cash is paid. Under accrual accounting, income is recorded when it is earned and expenses when they are incurred, whether or not cash has moved. That is why accrual accounting recognises amounts owed by customers (debtors) and owed to suppliers (creditors), as well as accruals and prepayments: B. Cash receipts and payments are what cash accounting records; options mixing in \"cash\" miss the point of the accrual basis. Memory tip: accrual = when earned or incurred; cash basis = when cash moves.",
 "|A private company is different from a public company because|it does not restrict the right to transfer its shares|it cannot invite members of the public to subscribe for its shares|its shares are owned by one person|it can only offer its shares to members of the public for subscription": "A private company (Ltd) may not invite the public to subscribe for its shares or debentures, and its articles restrict the transfer of its shares. A public company (Plc) may offer shares to the public and its shares can be freely transferred: B. Option A is the opposite of the truth, and D describes a public company. Under the Companies and Allied Matters Act 2020, which replaced CAMA 1990, a private company may now have just one member, but that is a permission, not what makes it private. Memory tip: Private = Limited, no public offer; Public = Plc, offers to the public.",
 "|The use of a three-column cash book is determined by|cash discounts|trade discounts|cash transactions|bank transactions": "A two-column cash book has cash and bank columns. A three-column cash book adds a discount column on each side: discount allowed on the debit side and discount received on the credit side. The reason for the extra column is to record cash discounts given or received when debts are settled promptly: A. The trap: trade discounts (B) also reduce prices, but they are deducted on the invoice before any entry is made, so they never appear in the books. Cash and bank transactions are recorded in a two-column book too. Memory tip: third column = cash discount column.",
 "|A major factor that enhanced the early development of accounting is the|Industrial Revolution|growth of trade and commerce|development of arithmetic|invention of writing": "Accounting grew out of the needs of trade. As merchants in places like Venice and Genoa traded more widely and on credit, and formed partnerships, they needed records of goods, debtors, creditors and each partner's share. This led to double-entry bookkeeping, described by Luca Pacioli in 1494: B. Writing and arithmetic were necessary tools, but they existed for thousands of years before accounting developed; the Industrial Revolution came much later and pushed cost and company accounting forward. Memory tip: trade came first; accounting followed the merchant.",
 "|Use the following information: \nS. Danbuzu's Bank Statement\nDate ... Particulars ... Debit ... Credit ... Balance\nJan. 1 ... Opening balance ... – ... – ... 10,000\nJan. 3 ... Bashir A. K. ... 1,000 ... – ... 9,000\nJan. 10 ... Usman A. D. ... 1,500 ... – ... 7,500\nJan. 12 ... Sule A. S. ... 500 ... – ... 7,000\nJan. 20 ... Aliyu A. K. ... – ... 3,000 ... 10,000\nJan. 30 ... X ... 1,500 ... – ... Y\n\nThe value of Y is|₦11,500|₦8,500|₦13,000|₦10,000": "On a bank statement the bank shows the account from its own point of view: deposits are credits (increase the balance) and payments are debits (reduce it). Running through: ₦10,000 → ₦9,000 → ₦7,500 → ₦7,000 → ₦10,000 after the ₦3,000 credit.\nStep: On 30 January, X is a debit of ₦1,500, so Y = ₦10,000 − ₦1,500 = ₦8,500: B.\nThe trap: ₦11,500 (A) adds the ₦1,500, treating the bank's debit as if it were a deposit, as it would be in the cash book. Memory tip: on a bank statement, debit = money out of your account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nS. Danbuzu's Bank Statement\nDate ... Particulars ... Debit ... Credit ... Balance\nJan. 1 ... Opening balance ... – ... – ... 10,000\nJan. 3 ... Bashir A. K. ... 1,000 ... – ... 9,000\nJan. 10 ... Usman A. D. ... 1,500 ... – ... 7,500\nJan. 12 ... Sule A. S. ... 500 ... – ... 7,000\nJan. 20 ... Aliyu A. K. ... – ... 3,000 ... 10,000\nJan. 30 ... X ... 1,500 ... – ... Y\n\nX represents|bank charges|an unpresented cheque|dividends|an uncredited cheque": "X is a debit entry on the bank statement with no customer's name, a deduction made by the bank itself. Items like this, which the customer learns of only from the statement, include bank charges, commission and standing orders: A. Dividends collected by the bank would be credits (increasing the balance). Unpresented cheques and uncredited cheques (lodgements not yet credited) are timing differences that appear in the cash book but NOT yet on the bank statement. Memory tip: bank charges show on the statement first; unpresented cheques show in the cash book first.",
 "|Which of the following is a source document for the preparation of debtors' ledger control account?|Debit note|General journal|Purchases invoice|Sales invoice": "The debtors' (sales) ledger control account takes its figures from the books of original entry, which in turn come from source documents. Credit sales, the main debit item, come from the sales day book, which is written up from copies of sales invoices: D. A purchases invoice supports the creditors' control account, and the general journal is a book of original entry, not a source document. The trap: a debit note (A) is also a source document, but it records returns to suppliers, which go to the creditors' side. Memory tip: sales invoice → sales day book → debtors' control account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nKhalifa's Cash Book\nDebit side (Date ... Particulars ... Cash ... Bank ... Discount):\n1/1 ... Bal. b/d ... 4,000 ... 5,000 ... –\n1/3 ... Khadijah ... – ... 3,000 ... –\n1/10 ... Sales ... 1,000 ... 1,500 ... 400\n1/29 ... Sales ... 800 ... – ... –\nCredit side (Date ... Particulars ... Cash ... Bank ... Discount):\n1/11 ... A. Lilisco ... 200 ... – ... –\n1/15 ... NEPA bill ... 500 ... – ... –\n1/28 ... Purchases ... 1,500 ... – ... 200\nOn January 30th, Khalifa withdrew ₦2,500 from the bank for office use.\n\nThe cash balance at the end of the month was|₦1,600|₦4,100|₦6,600|₦6,100": "Step 1: Cash debits: ₦4,000 + ₦1,000 + ₦800 = ₦5,800.\nStep 2: Cash credits: ₦200 + ₦500 + ₦1,500 = ₦2,200, leaving ₦3,600.\nStep 3: The ₦2,500 withdrawn from the bank for office use is a contra entry: debit cash, credit bank. Cash = ₦3,600 + ₦2,500 = ₦6,100: D.\nThe discounts (₦400, ₦200) are memorandum columns and do not change cash. The trap: ₦6,600 (C) is ₦500 too much, the result of leaving out the NEPA bill. Memory tip: a withdrawal for office use is a contra: cash up, bank down.",
 "|Use the following information: \nKhalifa's Cash Book\nDebit side (Date ... Particulars ... Cash ... Bank ... Discount):\n1/1 ... Bal. b/d ... 4,000 ... 5,000 ... –\n1/3 ... Khadijah ... – ... 3,000 ... –\n1/10 ... Sales ... 1,000 ... 1,500 ... 400\n1/29 ... Sales ... 800 ... – ... –\nCredit side (Date ... Particulars ... Cash ... Bank ... Discount):\n1/11 ... A. Lilisco ... 200 ... – ... –\n1/15 ... NEPA bill ... 500 ... – ... –\n1/28 ... Purchases ... 1,500 ... – ... 200\nOn January 30th, Khalifa withdrew ₦2,500 from the bank for office use.\n\nWhat was the bank balance at the end of the month?|₦7,000|₦6,500|₦7,500|₦9,000": "Step 1: Bank debits: ₦5,000 (balance) + ₦3,000 (Khadijah) + ₦1,500 (sales) = ₦9,500.\nStep 2: No payments were made by cheque during the month.\nStep 3: The ₦2,500 withdrawn for office use is credited to bank (the contra entry), so the bank balance = ₦9,500 − ₦2,500 = ₦7,000: A.\nThe discount column (₦400) is a memorandum figure and never changes the bank balance. Memory tip: in a contra entry the same amount appears on both sides of the cash book, cash on one, bank on the other.",
 "|Stationery which will be used over a long period of time is usually recorded as an expense instead of an asset. This concept is called|entity|materiality|realization|accrual": "Materiality says that only items large enough to affect the users' judgement need strict treatment. Stationery, small tools or a stapler may last several years, but they are so cheap that capitalising and depreciating them would cost more effort than it is worth, so they are written off as expenses: B. The entity concept separates the owner from the business; realisation is about when revenue is recognised; accrual matches income and expenses to periods. Memory tip: small items, small fuss: materiality.",
 "|Adaobi mistakenly entered ₦7,000 as credit sales in Abba's account instead of Baba's account. This is|an error of principle|a compensating error|an error of commission|an error of omission": "An error of commission is an entry made in the correct class of account but the wrong individual account, such as posting a credit sale to Abba's account instead of Baba's; both are debtors' (personal) accounts: C. The trial balance still agrees because the debit and credit are equal. An error of principle (A) would put the item in the wrong class of account, such as treating a fixed asset purchase as an expense. The trap: A is close, but the class (debtor) is right here, only the person is wrong. Memory tip: Commission = wrong person, same class; Principle = wrong class.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦60,000\nBank ... ₦12,500\nGeneral expenses ... ₦5,250\nCreditors ... ₦8,025\nStock ... ₦17,150\nSales ... ₦98,500\nFixed assets ... ₦42,800\nInsurance and rates ... ₦18,500\nPurchases ... ₦63,850\n\nWhat is the cash balance on the debit side if it is the only item omitted?|6,745|6,754|6,475|6,574": "Step 1: Debit balances (assets, expenses, purchases): bank ₦12,500 + general expenses ₦5,250 + stock ₦17,150 + fixed assets ₦42,800 + insurance and rates ₦18,500 + purchases ₦63,850 = ₦160,050.\nStep 2: Credit balances (capital, liabilities, income): capital ₦60,000 + creditors ₦8,025 + sales ₦98,500 = ₦166,525.\nStep 3: Missing cash (a debit) = ₦166,525 − ₦160,050 = ₦6,475: C.\nThe trap: 6,745 (A) and 6,574 (D) swap digits of the right answer, so read your figure carefully. Memory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits are Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits are Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦60,000\nBank ... ₦12,500\nGeneral expenses ... ₦5,250\nCreditors ... ₦8,025\nStock ... ₦17,150\nSales ... ₦98,500\nFixed assets ... ₦42,800\nInsurance and rates ... ₦18,500\nPurchases ... ₦63,850\n\nDetermine the total of the trial balance.|123,325|160,050|166,525|326,575": "Once the missing cash balance of ₦6,475 is added to the debit side, both sides of the trial balance agree.\nStep 1: Credit side = ₦60,000 + ₦8,025 + ₦98,500 = ₦166,525.\nStep 2: Debit side = ₦160,050 + ₦6,475 = ₦166,525.\nSo the trial balance totals ₦166,525: C.\nThe trap: ₦160,050 (B) is the debit side before the cash balance is added. Adding every figure on both sides gives about ₦326,575 (D), which double-counts. Memory tip: a trial balance total is the total of ONE side, and both sides must be equal.",
 "|Which of the following is a journal as well as a ledger?|Nominal ledger|Sales day book|General ledger|Cash book": "The cash book is special. It is a book of original entry, because cash and bank transactions are recorded in it first (it acts as a journal). It is also part of the ledger, because the cash and bank columns are the cash account and bank account themselves, balanced and used in the trial balance: D. The sales day book is only a book of original entry; the nominal and general ledgers are only ledgers. Memory tip: the cash book wears two hats: journal and ledger.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock of raw materials 1/1/04 ... ₦11,150\nPurchases of raw materials ... ₦112,550\nRaw materials returned ... ₦31,150\nCarriage on raw materials ... ₦1,150\nStock of raw materials 31/12/04 ... ₦27,150\n\nCalculate the cost of raw materials consumed.|156,000|38,300|183,150|66,550": "Cost of raw materials consumed:\nStep 1: Opening stock ₦11,150 + purchases ₦112,550 = ₦123,700.\nStep 2: Less returns outwards ₦31,150 = ₦92,550.\nStep 3: Add carriage on raw materials (carriage inwards) ₦1,150 = ₦93,700.\nStep 4: Less closing stock ₦27,150 = ₦66,550: D.\nReturns outwards reduce purchases, while carriage inwards adds to the cost of materials. Memory tip: Opening + Purchases − Returns + Carriage in − Closing = consumed.",
 "|Use the following information: \n... Stationery Dept. ... Video Dept.\nSales ... ₦10,600 ... ₦15,175\nCost of goods sold ... ₦5,247 ... ₦6,704\nThe expenses incurred were rent ₦4,500, motor repairs ₦1,200 and telephone ₦900. The basis of cost apportionment is stationery 2/3 and video 1/3.\n\nDetermine the net profit of the business.|6,600|6,271|5,353|7,224": "Step 1: Stationery gross profit = ₦10,600 − ₦5,247 = ₦5,353.\nStep 2: Video gross profit = ₦15,175 − ₦6,704 = ₦8,471.\nStep 3: Total gross profit = ₦13,824.\nStep 4: Expenses = ₦4,500 + ₦1,200 + ₦900 = ₦6,600.\nStep 5: Net profit = ₦13,824 − ₦6,600 = ₦7,224: D.\nFor the whole business the apportionment does not matter: it only splits the same ₦6,600 between departments. ₦6,600 (A) is total expenses and ₦5,353 (C) is the stationery gross profit. Memory tip: business net profit = total gross profit − total expenses.",
 "|Use the following information: \n... Stationery Dept. ... Video Dept.\nSales ... ₦10,600 ... ₦15,175\nCost of goods sold ... ₦5,247 ... ₦6,704\nThe expenses incurred were rent ₦4,500, motor repairs ₦1,200 and telephone ₦900. The basis of cost apportionment is stationery 2/3 and video 1/3.\n\nFind the total expenses allocated to the stationery department.|3,600|1,400|4,400|3,800": "Departmental accounts share common expenses on a fair basis, here 2/3 to stationery and 1/3 to video.\nStep 1: Total expenses = ₦4,500 + ₦1,200 + ₦900 = ₦6,600.\nStep 2: Stationery share = 2/3 × ₦6,600 = ₦4,400: C.\nThe video department takes the other 1/3, ₦2,200. Check: ₦4,400 + ₦2,200 = ₦6,600. With this charge the stationery department's net profit is ₦5,353 − ₦4,400 = ₦953. Memory tip: apportion the total first, then take each department's fraction.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock 1/1/2002 ... ₦40,000\nGoods received from head office ... ₦800,000\nGoods returned to head office ... ₦34,000\nSales ... ₦916,000\nStock 31/12/2002 ... ₦72,000\n\nFrom the data above, determine the cost of goods credited to the head office trading account.|766,000|840,000|734,000|806,000": "In branch accounts kept by the head office, goods sent to the branch are debited to the branch account and credited to a \"goods sent to branch\" account. At the year end that account is closed by transfer to the head office trading account, where it is credited (or deducted from purchases), so that the head office's own trading account is not overstated.\nStep: Net goods sent = ₦800,000 − returns ₦34,000 = ₦766,000: A.\nThis answer is debatable: ₦734,000 (C) is the branch's cost of goods sold (₦40,000 + ₦766,000 − ₦72,000), which belongs to the branch trading account, not the amount credited to head office. Memory tip: goods sent to branch (less returns) go back to head office trading account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPrime cost ... ₦12,100\nLighting ... ₦10,000\nHeating ... ₦15,000\nFactory overhead ... ?\nThe production department share of factory overhead is 1/4 and 1/5 of lighting and heating respectively.\n\nThe factory overhead is|5,050|7,250|2,750|5,500": "Overheads shared between production (factory) and office are apportioned in the given fractions.\nStep 1: Factory share of lighting = 1/4 × ₦10,000 = ₦2,500.\nStep 2: Factory share of heating = 1/5 × ₦15,000 = ₦3,000.\nStep 3: Factory overhead = ₦2,500 + ₦3,000 = ₦5,500: D.\nFactory overhead is added to prime cost to give production cost: ₦12,100 + ₦5,500 = ₦17,600. Match each fraction to its own expense: 1/4 goes with lighting, 1/5 with heating. Memory tip: Prime cost + Factory overhead = Production cost.",
 "|Use the following information: \nPrime cost ... ₦12,100\nLighting ... ₦10,000\nHeating ... ₦15,000\nFactory overhead ... ?\nThe production department share of factory overhead is 1/4 and 1/5 of lighting and heating respectively.\n\nDetermine the amount of office lighting and heating.|19,500|25,000|7,500|12,000": "Whatever is not charged to the factory belongs to the office (the profit and loss account).\nStep 1: Office lighting = ₦10,000 − ₦2,500 = ₦7,500 (3/4 of ₦10,000).\nStep 2: Office heating = ₦15,000 − ₦3,000 = ₦12,000 (4/5 of ₦15,000).\nStep 3: Office lighting and heating = ₦7,500 + ₦12,000 = ₦19,500: A.\nThe trap: ₦7,500 (C) and ₦12,000 (D) are each only one of the two items. Check: factory ₦5,500 + office ₦19,500 = ₦25,000 = total lighting and heating (B). Memory tip: factory share + office share = total.",
 "|Interest on the drawings of partners is treated in the|profit and loss appropriation account|goodwill account|profit and loss account|trading account": "Interest on drawings is charged to partners to discourage them from withdrawing too much. It is not trading income, so it does not go to the trading or profit and loss account. Instead, it is added to net profit in the profit and loss appropriation account (increasing the profit to be shared) and debited to each partner's current account: A. Interest on capital and partners' salaries are also appropriations. The trap: profit and loss account (C) sounds right, but items between the firm and its partners are appropriations of profit, not expenses or income. Memory tip: anything between partners and the firm goes to the appropriation account.",
 "|Departmentalisation of accounts is useful because it shows the|overall performance of a firm|price per unit of a product|cost per unit of a product|overall performance of a division": "In departmental accounts, the trading and profit and loss account is split into columns for each department, with sales, cost of sales and expenses for each. This shows the profit or loss of each department or division, so management can see which sections are profitable, decide where to spend, and reward managers: D. The ordinary final accounts already show the overall performance of the firm (A). Cost per unit and price per unit come from costing, not departmental accounts. The trap: A sounds close, but departmental accounts add value by breaking the overall result down by division. Memory tip: department accounts = performance of each department.",
 "|When goodwill is not retained in the business, the entries in the new partner's books will be to debit|goodwill account and credit partner's capital account|partners' capital account and credit goodwill account|cash account and credit goodwill account|goodwill account and credit cash account": "When a new partner is admitted and goodwill is first raised in the books, the old partners' capital accounts are credited (debit goodwill). If the partners agree that goodwill should not remain in the books, it is written off: debit the partners' capital accounts (in the new profit-sharing ratio) and credit goodwill account: B. Goodwill is an intangible asset, so cash is not involved when it is written off. The trap: A is the entry for raising goodwill, the reverse of writing it off. Memory tip: raise goodwill = Dr Goodwill, Cr old partners; write off = Dr all partners (new ratio), Cr Goodwill.",
 "|Use the following information: \n... January ... December\nAssets ... ₦28,320 ... ₦25,320\nCreditors ... ₦5,620 ... ₦10,320\nThe proprietor made drawings of ₦755 per month for personal use.\n\nThe profit for the period is|₦3,000|₦1,360|₦9,060|₦7,700": "With incomplete records, profit is found from the change in capital (net assets).\nStep 1: Opening capital = ₦28,320 − ₦5,620 = ₦22,700.\nStep 2: Closing capital = ₦25,320 − ₦10,320 = ₦15,000.\nStep 3: Drawings = ₦755 × 12 = ₦9,060.\nStep 4: Profit = closing capital − opening capital + drawings = ₦15,000 − ₦22,700 + ₦9,060 = ₦1,360: B.\nThe trap: ₦7,700 (D) is only the fall in capital; drawings must be added back. Memory tip: Profit = Closing capital − Opening capital + Drawings − New capital.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscriptions received ... ₦200,000\nSubscriptions owed ... ₦10,000\nSubscriptions in advance ... ₦25,000\n\nCalculate the subscriptions in the income and expenditure account.|₦200,000|₦235,000|₦185,000|₦225,000": "The income and expenditure account shows subscriptions belonging to the year, not just cash received.\nStep 1: Cash received = ₦200,000.\nStep 2: Add subscriptions owing for this year (still to be received) ₦10,000 = ₦210,000.\nStep 3: Deduct subscriptions received in advance for next year ₦25,000 = ₦185,000: C.\nThe trap: ₦235,000 (B) adds the advance instead of deducting it. Memory tip: subscriptions income = received + owing at end − in advance at end (and the reverse for opening figures).",
 "|Boro Limited apportions 1/6 of its rent for factory and 10% of the balance for warehouse. If rent paid is ₦48,600, how much is paid for warehouse?|₦8,150|₦4,860|₦4,100|₦4,050": "Step 1: Factory share = 1/6 × ₦48,600 = ₦8,100.\nStep 2: Balance = ₦48,600 − ₦8,100 = ₦40,500.\nStep 3: Warehouse share = 10% × ₦40,500 = ₦4,050: D.\nThe trap: ₦4,860 (B) is 10% of the whole rent, ignoring \"of the balance\". Memory tip: when the question says \"of the balance\", take out the first share before applying the percentage.",
 "|Head office sends goods to its branch at cost plus mark-up of 25%. A debit of 40,000 to the branch stock account in the head office books will indicate that the head office has sent goods with a profit of|4,000|6,000|10,000|8,000": "When goods are sent at cost plus a mark-up, the branch stock account is debited at selling price, so the figure includes the profit loading.\nStep 1: Mark-up of 25% on cost: cost ₦100 → selling price ₦125, so profit is 25/125 = 1/5 of selling price.\nStep 2: Profit = 1/5 × ₦40,000 = ₦8,000 (cost = ₦32,000): D.\nThe trap: ₦10,000 (C) takes 25% of ₦40,000, treating the mark-up as if it were a margin on selling price. Memory tip: mark-up 1/4 on cost = margin 1/5 on selling price.",
 "|Use the following information: \nMotor van ... ₦10,000\nFixtures ... ₦5,000\nCash ... ₦2,500\nCreditors ... ₦7,000\nLoan ... ?\nLoan is 30% of creditors.\n\nWhat is the capital balance?|8,400|6,900|10,500|15,000": "Capital = total assets − total liabilities.\nStep 1: Loan = 30% × ₦7,000 = ₦2,100.\nStep 2: Assets = ₦10,000 + ₦5,000 + ₦2,500 = ₦17,500.\nStep 3: Liabilities = ₦7,000 + ₦2,100 = ₦9,100.\nStep 4: Capital = ₦17,500 − ₦9,100 = ₦8,400: A.\nThe trap: ₦10,500 (C) deducts only the creditors and forgets the loan. Memory tip: Capital = Assets − Liabilities (all of them).",
 "|Use the following information: \nMotor van ... ₦10,000\nFixtures ... ₦5,000\nCash ... ₦2,500\nCreditors ... ₦7,000\nLoan ... ?\nLoan is 30% of creditors.\n\nThe amount of the loan is|1,000|2,100|900|1,200": "The loan is given as a percentage of creditors.\nStep: Loan = 30% × ₦7,000 = 30/100 × ₦7,000 = ₦2,100: B.\nThis figure is then treated as a liability (long-term) when finding capital: ₦17,500 − ₦7,000 − ₦2,100 = ₦8,400. Options ₦1,000, ₦900 and ₦1,200 do not come from the creditors figure at all. Memory tip: \"X% of creditors\" means multiply the creditors figure by X/100.",
 "|A company has departments S, T and U. The sales are ₦20,000, ₦40,000 and ₦60,000 respectively. If the sales commission paid is ₦12,000, how much is T's share?|₦8,000|₦2,000|₦4,000|₦6,000": "Expenses that vary with sales, such as sales commission and carriage outwards, are apportioned in the ratio of the departments' sales.\nStep 1: Total sales = ₦20,000 + ₦40,000 + ₦60,000 = ₦120,000; ratio 1:2:3.\nStep 2: T's share = 40,000/120,000 × ₦12,000 = ₦4,000: C.\nS takes ₦2,000 and U ₦6,000. Check: ₦2,000 + ₦4,000 + ₦6,000 = ₦12,000. Memory tip: sales-based costs follow the sales ratio.",
 "|MPOBA Club's annual donations from members amount to 112% of its internally generated revenue from the following:\nSale of drinks ... ₦212,500\nAccrual dues ... ₦112,000\n\nHow much is the total receipts of the club?|563,440|687,940|363,440|524,500": "Step 1: Internally generated revenue = sale of drinks ₦212,500 + dues ₦112,000 = ₦324,500.\nStep 2: Donations = 112% × ₦324,500 = ₦363,440.\nStep 3: Total receipts = ₦324,500 + ₦363,440 = ₦687,940: B.\nThe trap: ₦363,440 (C) is only the donations; the question asks for all receipts, so the club's own revenue must be added. Memory tip: read the last line of the question: \"total\" means add every source.",
 "|In converting a partnership to a company, the loss on asset revaluation is|debited to the revaluation account|debited to the profit and loss account|credited to the revaluation account|credited to the profit and loss account": "When a partnership is converted into a company (or a partner is admitted or retires), assets are revalued. Increases in value are gains and are credited to the revaluation (realisation) account; decreases are losses and are debited to it, with the asset account credited. The net result is then shared among the partners' capital accounts in their profit-sharing ratio: A. The profit and loss account is not used, because revaluation is not a trading result. The trap: C (credited to revaluation account) is the treatment of a gain, the reverse. Memory tip: revaluation: losses Debit, gains Credit.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts and Payments Account\nReceipts: Balance b/d ... ?\nSubscriptions received ... ?\nGate-fee collections ... ₦50,000\nPayments: Rent and rates ... ₦30,000\nGeneral expenses ... ₦100,000\nElectricity ... ₦20,000\nInsurance ... ₦150,000\nBalance c/d ... ?\nThe subscriptions received represent 80% of total payments and the opening balance is 30% of gate-fee collections.\n\nWhat is the closing cash balance?|₦35,000|₦211,100|₦5,000|₦10,000": "Step 1: Payments = rent ₦30,000 + general expenses ₦100,000 + electricity ₦20,000 + insurance ₦150,000 = ₦300,000.\nStep 2: Subscriptions = 80% × ₦300,000 = ₦240,000.\nStep 3: Opening balance = 30% × ₦50,000 = ₦15,000.\nStep 4: Total receipts = ₦15,000 + ₦240,000 + ₦50,000 = ₦305,000.\nStep 5: Closing balance = ₦305,000 − ₦300,000 = ₦5,000: C.\nA receipts and payments account is simply a summarised cash book, so its closing balance is cash in hand. Memory tip: closing cash = opening cash + receipts − payments.",
 "|Use the following information: \nReceipts and Payments Account\nReceipts: Balance b/d ... ?\nSubscriptions received ... ?\nGate-fee collections ... ₦50,000\nPayments: Rent and rates ... ₦30,000\nGeneral expenses ... ₦100,000\nElectricity ... ₦20,000\nInsurance ... ₦150,000\nBalance c/d ... ?\nThe subscriptions received represent 80% of total payments and the opening balance is 30% of gate-fee collections.\n\nThe subscriptions received would be|₦200,000|₦240,000|₦300,000|₦24,000": "The receipts and payments account is a cash summary, and the subscriptions are tied to total payments.\nStep 1: Total payments = ₦30,000 + ₦100,000 + ₦20,000 + ₦150,000 = ₦300,000.\nStep 2: Subscriptions received = 80% × ₦300,000 = ₦240,000: B.\nThe trap: ₦300,000 (C) is the total of payments itself, and ₦24,000 (D) slips a zero. Note this is cash received; the income and expenditure account would adjust it for amounts owing and in advance. Memory tip: read which total a percentage refers to before multiplying.",
 "|One of the major features of book keeping is that it|provides permanent records for all financial transactions|is futuristic in nature|accepts responsibility for all wrong postings|does not give room for double entry procedure": "Bookkeeping is the systematic recording of a business's financial transactions in books of account. Its great value is that it gives a permanent, orderly record of every transaction, which can be checked later and used to prepare final accounts. It records what has already happened, so it is not \"futuristic\"; it does not accept responsibility for wrong postings (people do); and it is based on double entry, not against it. So the major feature is a permanent record: A. Memory tip: bookkeeping = recording the past, permanently and in order.",
 "|Which of the following accounting conventions suggests that accountants should use a method of valuation that understates rather than overstates results?|Conservatism|Historical|Monetary|Cost": "The convention of conservatism, also called prudence, tells the accountant to anticipate no profit but provide for all possible losses. When two values are possible, choose the one that gives the lower profit and the lower asset figure. That is why stock is valued at the lower of cost and net realisable value. A method that understates rather than overstates results is therefore conservatism: A. Historical cost and money measurement are different concepts: they say what basis and unit to use, not which way to lean. Memory tip: conservative = careful = choose the lower figure.",
 "|A financial analyst needs accounting information to|maintain the production section of the business|know why transactions cause increases and decreases in assets|advise on how to manage the business|know how to record transactions in T account": "Different users need accounting information for different purposes. A financial analyst reads and interprets the accounts, works out ratios and trends, and then advises owners, investors and managers on how the business should be run and financed. Running the production section, learning why transactions change assets, or learning how to post to T accounts are jobs for managers, students and bookkeepers, not analysts. So the analyst needs the information to advise on how to manage the business: C. Memory tip: analyst = analyse, then advise.",
 "|One basic assumption of the double entry theory is that it allows|two credit entries at the same time|two debit entries at the same time|debit and credit entries in the same account|debit and credit entries in corresponding accounts": "The double entry principle says every transaction has two sides: one account receives value (debit) and another gives value (credit), for the same amount. The two entries are made in two corresponding accounts, never both in the same account. For example, buying goods for cash: debit purchases, credit cash. So the basic assumption is debit and credit entries in corresponding accounts: D. Two credits or two debits alone would leave the books out of balance. Memory tip: for every debit there is an equal credit in another account.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSales ... ₦4,000\nLight expenses ... ₦2,900\nRent ... ₦15,000\nPurchases ... ₦7,500\nDebtors ... ₦6,000\nCreditors ... ₦29,325\nDrawings ... ₦25\nBank ... ₦400\nGeneral expenses ... ₦1,500\n\nCalculate the total of the trial balance.|₦64,300|₦46,825|₦41,850|₦33,325": "A trial balance lists debit balances (assets, expenses, drawings) and credit balances (liabilities, capital, income). Step 1, credit side: sales ₦4,000 + creditors ₦29,325 = ₦33,325. Step 2, debit side: light expenses ₦2,900 + rent ₦15,000 + purchases ₦7,500 + debtors ₦6,000 + drawings ₦25 + bank ₦400 + general expenses ₦1,500 = ₦33,325. Step 3: the two sides agree, so the total is ₦33,325: D. Adding every figure together (₦66,650) is not a trial balance total. Memory tip: DEAD CLIC: Debits are Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credits are Liabilities, Income, Capital.",
 "|Bariga, a stock broker, bought stationery for ₦12,000 by cash. To record this transaction, debit|cash and credit stationery|purchases and credit stationery|stationery and credit cash|stationery and credit purchases": "For a stock broker, stationery is a business expense, not goods bought for resale, so it never goes to the purchases account. The stationery account receives the value, so it is debited; cash gives the value, so the cash account is credited. Debit stationery ₦12,000, credit cash ₦12,000: C. Option A reverses the entry. The trap: D (credit purchases) catches students who think any buying goes through purchases; purchases is only for goods bought to resell. Memory tip: debit the receiver, credit the giver.",
 "|Use the following information: \nI. Cash at bank\nII. Cash in hand\nIII. Premises\nIV. Land\nV. Creditors\nVI. Loan from friends/bank\n\nDetermine the fixed assets.|I and V|II and IV|II and VI|III and IV": "Fixed (non-current) assets are long-term assets bought for use in the business, not for resale, and kept for more than a year. From the list, premises (III) and land (IV) are fixed assets: D. Cash at bank and cash in hand are current assets; creditors and loans are liabilities. Today, under IFRS, fixed assets are called property, plant and equipment or non-current assets. Memory tip: fixed assets stay; current assets circulate.",
 "|Use the following information: \nI. Cash at bank\nII. Cash in hand\nIII. Premises\nIV. Land\nV. Creditors\nVI. Loan from friends/bank\n\nWhat are the current assets?|I and II|I and IV|II and IV|III and V": "Current assets are cash and items that will become cash within a year, such as stock, debtors, cash at bank and cash in hand. From the list, cash at bank (I) and cash in hand (II) are current assets: A. Land and premises (III, IV) are fixed assets, while creditors (V) and the loan (VI) are liabilities, not assets at all. Memory tip: current assets in order of liquidity: stock, debtors, bank, cash.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCash Book\nDebit side: Capital ... ₦65,000\nSales ... ₦30,000\nCredit side: Purchases ... ₦12,250\nRent ... ₦9,100\nMotor vehicle ... ₦2,400\n\nFind the bank balance.|₦71,250 credit|₦71,250 debit|₦95,000 credit|₦95,000 debit": "Balance an account by comparing the two sides. Step 1, receipts (debit): capital ₦65,000 + sales ₦30,000 = ₦95,000. Step 2, payments (credit): purchases ₦12,250 + rent ₦9,100 + motor vehicle ₦2,400 = ₦23,750. Step 3: balance = ₦95,000 − ₦23,750 = ₦71,250. More came in than went out, so the balance brought down is a debit balance, money held in the bank: B. The trap: A (₦71,250 credit) catches students who look at where the balance c/d is written rather than the balance b/d. Memory tip: a debit balance in the cash book means money in hand or at bank.",
 "|An entry made in the bank and cash columns of the cash book to record movement of cash between the office and the bank is the|real entry|special entry|direct entry|contra entry": "When cash in the office is paid into the bank, or cash is withdrawn from the bank for office use, both entries are made in the same two-column (or three-column) cash book: one in the cash column and one in the bank column. Such an entry is called a contra entry and is marked \"C\" in the folio column: D. For example, paying office cash into the bank: debit bank column, credit cash column. Memory tip: contra means \"against\": the cash book posts against itself.",
 "|Abba offered 5% cash discount on goods worth ₦10,000. The discount allowed account is to be|debited with ₦10,000|credited with ₦10,000|debited with ₦500|credited with ₦500": "Cash discount is a reduction given for prompt payment. Step 1: discount = 5% × ₦10,000 = ₦500. Step 2: for the seller (Abba) this is discount allowed, an expense, so the discount allowed account is debited with ₦500, and the customer's account is credited. So the answer is debited with ₦500: C. The ₦10,000 is the value of the goods, not the discount. The trap: D (credited with ₦500) has the right figure on the wrong side; discount received is the one credited. Memory tip: discount allowed = expense = debit; discount received = income = credit.",
 "|Commission-on-turnover is charged on|current accounts only|savings accounts only|fixed deposit accounts only|all bank accounts": "Commission on turnover (COT) was the charge Nigerian banks made on the total value of withdrawals (debit turnover) on a customer's current account. Only current accounts are operated by cheque with frequent withdrawals, so COT applied to current accounts only: A. Savings and fixed deposit accounts earn interest and do not attract COT. Today COT has been phased out by the Central Bank of Nigeria and replaced by other account maintenance charges. Memory tip: COT = Current account, Only.",
 "|The medium that enables the ATM to read the account details and process transactions directly with the account held in the bank is the|smart card|communication network|magnetic strip|computerised account": "An automated teller machine (ATM) lets a customer withdraw cash or make transfers without a cashier. The customer inserts the ATM card, a smart card that carries the account details, and enters a PIN; the machine reads the card and processes the transaction against the account in the bank. So the medium is the smart card: A. The communication network links the machine to the bank, but it is the card that gives the account details. Memory tip: card + PIN = access to your account.",
 "|Given that the balance as per cash book after adjustment was ₦6,315, unpresented cheques ₦1,000 and the bank statement balance ₦3,240, what was the balance of the uncredited cheques?|₦4,075|₦4,240|₦3,315|₦3,075": "A bank reconciliation starts from the bank statement and adjusts it to agree with the cash book. Step 1: adjusted cash book balance = bank statement + uncredited cheques − unpresented cheques. Step 2: ₦6,315 = ₦3,240 + X − ₦1,000. Step 3: X = ₦6,315 − ₦3,240 + ₦1,000 = ₦4,075: A. Check: ₦3,240 + ₦4,075 − ₦1,000 = ₦6,315. The trap: D (₦3,075) is what you get by subtracting the unpresented cheques instead of adding them back. Memory tip: uncredited lodgements are added to the bank statement; unpresented cheques are deducted.",
 "|Use the following information: \nTrading Account (Extract)\nOpening stock ... ₦19,500\nAdd purchases ... ₦68,700\n(Subtotal ... ₦88,200)\nLess closing stock ... ?\nCost of goods sold ... ?\nGross profit c/d ... ?\nTotal ... ?\nSales ... ₦96,000\nTotal ... ?\nThe gross profit margin is 20%.\n\nWhat is the gross profit?|₦19,200|₦12,900|₦88,200|₦76,800": "Gross profit margin is gross profit as a percentage of sales (not of cost). Step 1: margin = 20%. Step 2: gross profit = 20% × ₦96,000 = ₦19,200: A. Note that ₦76,800 (D) is the cost of goods sold, which is the other 80% of sales, and ₦88,200 is the cost of goods available for sale. Memory tip: margin is on sales; mark-up is on cost.",
 "|Use the following information: \nTrading Account (Extract)\nOpening stock ... ₦19,500\nAdd purchases ... ₦68,700\n(Subtotal ... ₦88,200)\nLess closing stock ... ?\nCost of goods sold ... ?\nGross profit c/d ... ?\nTotal ... ?\nSales ... ₦96,000\nTotal ... ?\nThe gross profit margin is 20%.\n\nDetermine the closing stock.|₦76,800|₦67,800|₦14,100|₦11,400": "Work backwards through the trading account. Step 1: gross profit = 20% × ₦96,000 = ₦19,200. Step 2: cost of goods sold = sales − gross profit = ₦96,000 − ₦19,200 = ₦76,800. Step 3: closing stock = goods available ₦88,200 − cost of goods sold ₦76,800 = ₦11,400: D. Check: ₦88,200 − ₦11,400 = ₦76,800. The trap: C (₦14,100) uses the same digits as the answer in a different order. Memory tip: opening stock + purchases − closing stock = cost of goods sold.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦12,700\nDebtors ... ₦4,000\nSales ... ₦2,400\nPurchases ... ₦7,100\nStationery ... ₦1,900\nWages ... ₦1,230\nThe owner wishes to maintain an account equal to ¾ of capital as drawings.\n\nWhat is the amount withdrawn?|₦9,525|₦9,000|₦7,100|₦6,400": "The owner wants drawings equal to three-quarters of capital. Step 1: capital = ₦12,700. Step 2: drawings = ¾ × ₦12,700 = ₦12,700 × 3 ÷ 4 = ₦38,100 ÷ 4 = ₦9,525: A. Drawings are money or goods taken out by the owner for private use; they reduce capital and are not a business expense. Memory tip: \"¾ of\" means multiply by 3, then divide by 4.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital ... ₦12,700\nDebtors ... ₦4,000\nSales ... ₦2,400\nPurchases ... ₦7,100\nStationery ... ₦1,900\nWages ... ₦1,230\nThe owner wishes to maintain an account equal to ¾ of capital as drawings.\n\nDetermine the closing balance of the cash book.|₦655 credit|₦655 debit|₦130 credit|₦4,870 debit": "Only cash items enter the cash book. Step 1, receipts: capital ₦12,700 + cash sales ₦2,400 = ₦15,100. Debtors (₦4,000) are amounts still owed by customers, not cash received. Step 2, payments: purchases ₦7,100 + stationery ₦1,900 + wages ₦1,230 = ₦10,230. Step 3: balance = ₦15,100 − ₦10,230 = ₦4,870 debit: D. This treats the ¾ drawings as the amount the owner wishes to set aside, not yet withdrawn; if ₦9,525 were paid out, the balance would be ₦4,655 credit, which is not offered, so the question is debatable. Memory tip: cash book = cash in, cash out; credit balances like debtors stay out.",
 "|The factors that determine the periodic depreciation charge are|asset cost, market value and useful life|asset cost, market value and residual value|asset value, cash value and useful life|asset cost, salvage and useful life": "Depreciation is the part of a fixed asset's cost used up in a period. To work it out you need three things: the cost of the asset, its expected salvage (residual or scrap) value, and its useful life. Straight line: (cost − salvage value) ÷ useful life. So the factors are asset cost, salvage and useful life: D. Market value is not used, because fixed assets are held for use, not for sale. The trap: B lists cost and residual value but swaps useful life for market value. Memory tip: depreciation = (Cost − Scrap) ÷ Life.",
 "|Use the following information: \nAug. 1 ... Received 20 units at ₦60 each\nAug. 6 ... Received 20 units at ₦68 each\nAug. 10 ... Issued 16 units\nAug. 20 ... Received 40 units at ₦80 each\nAug. 31 ... Issued 48 units\n\nUsing the simple average method, what is the cost per unit of the closing stock?|₦80|₦74|₦64|₦60": "In the simple average method, each issue is priced at the simple average of the unit prices of the batches still in stock, and batches are used up in date order. Step 1: Aug 10 issues 16 units, all from the ₦60 batch, leaving 4 @ ₦60 and 20 @ ₦68. Step 2: Aug 20 adds 40 @ ₦80. Step 3: Aug 31 issues 48 units: the last 4 @ ₦60, all 20 @ ₦68 and 24 @ ₦80. Step 4: the 16 units left are all from the ₦80 batch, so the price still in stock is ₦80 a unit: A. The trap: B (₦74) averages ₦68 and ₦80 and forgets that the ₦60 batch was still there on 31 August. Memory tip: in simple average, track which batches are still in stock.",
 "|Use the following information: \nAug. 1 ... Received 20 units at ₦60 each\nAug. 6 ... Received 20 units at ₦68 each\nAug. 10 ... Issued 16 units\nAug. 20 ... Received 40 units at ₦80 each\nAug. 31 ... Issued 48 units\n\nUsing the FIFO method, what is the value of the closing stock?|₦1,280|₦1,184|₦1,024|₦960": "FIFO (first in, first out) issues the oldest units first, so closing stock is valued at the latest prices. Step 1: units received = 20 + 20 + 40 = 80; units issued = 16 + 48 = 64; units left = 16. Step 2: the 16 left are from the last batch, received Aug 20 at ₦80. Step 3: closing stock = 16 × ₦80 = ₦1,280: A. Memory tip: FIFO leaves the newest goods on the shelf, at the newest prices.",
 "|In a period of inflation, which of the following methods normally gives a closing stock value very close to the market value?|FIFO|LIFO|Simple average|Periodic simple average": "When prices are rising (inflation), FIFO issues the older, cheaper units first, so the units left in closing stock are the most recent purchases at the latest prices. That makes the FIFO closing stock value very close to current market value: A. LIFO does the opposite, leaving old, low prices in closing stock. Today IAS 2 no longer allows LIFO. Memory tip: FIFO = fresh prices in closing stock.",
 "|The main purpose of control account is to|detect errors and irregularities|assist purchases|assist sales|check management incompetence": "A control account is a summary account that records the totals of all entries in a ledger (for example, the sales ledger). If the control account balance agrees with the total of the individual customer balances, the ledger is arithmetically correct; if not, there is an error to find. Control accounts also help to detect fraud because a different person can keep them. So the main purpose is to detect errors and irregularities: A. Memory tip: a control account is a check on a whole ledger.",
 "|Which of the following is a credit item in creditors ledger control account?|Purchases returns|Bills payable|Purchases|Cash receipts": "The creditors (purchases) ledger control account mirrors the suppliers' accounts. Amounts owed go up on the credit side: opening creditors and credit purchases. Amounts owed go down on the debit side: payments, discount received, returns outwards (purchases returns) and bills payable accepted. So purchases is the credit item: C. The trap: A (purchases returns) is related to purchases but reduces what we owe, so it is a debit item. Memory tip: in the creditors control account, what we owe more is credit, what we owe less is debit.",
 "|Discount allowed as represented in the control account appears on the|credit side of the purchases control account|debit side of the purchases control account|credit side of the sales control account|debit side of the sales control account": "Discount allowed is given to customers (debtors), so it belongs in the sales ledger control account, not the purchases control account. It reduces what customers owe, so it is entered on the credit side: C. Debit side items in the sales control account are opening debtors, credit sales and dishonoured cheques. The trap: D puts it in the right account but on the wrong side. Memory tip: allowed = to customers = sales control, credit side.",
 "|Use the following information: \nItem ... 31 Dec. 05 ... 31 Dec. 06\nTotal fixed assets ... ₦7,800 ... ₦6,600\nTotal current assets ... ₦12,100 ... ₦17,800\nTotal current liabilities ... ₦4,700 ... ₦3,800\nDrawings for 2006 is ₦5,200.\n\nDetermine the capital balance as at Dec. 31, 2005.|₦20,600|₦15,200|₦13,800|₦11,600": "For incomplete records, capital equals net assets: assets minus liabilities. Step 1: total assets at 31 Dec 2005 = fixed ₦7,800 + current ₦12,100 = ₦19,900. Step 2: less current liabilities ₦4,700. Step 3: capital = ₦19,900 − ₦4,700 = ₦15,200: B. This is the statement of affairs method. Memory tip: capital = assets − liabilities.",
 "|Use the following information: \nItem ... 31 Dec. 05 ... 31 Dec. 06\nTotal fixed assets ... ₦7,800 ... ₦6,600\nTotal current assets ... ₦12,100 ... ₦17,800\nTotal current liabilities ... ₦4,700 ... ₦3,800\nDrawings for 2006 is ₦5,200.\n\nWhat is the closing capital for 2006?|₦15,400|₦16,000|₦12,600|₦10,600": "Closing capital is found the same way as opening capital: closing net assets. Step 1: assets at 31 Dec 2006 = ₦6,600 + ₦17,800 = ₦24,400. Step 2: less current liabilities ₦3,800 = ₦20,600. So the closing capital is ₦20,600, which is not among the options. JAMB's answer ₦15,400 takes the drawings off again (₦20,600 − ₦5,200), but drawings have already reduced the 2006 assets, so this is not fully correct. The answer marked is therefore JAMB's, shown as not fully correct: A. Drawings are used to find profit: ₦20,600 − ₦15,200 + ₦5,200 = ₦10,600. Memory tip: profit = closing capital − opening capital + drawings − new capital.",
 "|The floor space occupied by manufacturing and administrative departments in a company is 200 sqm and 300 sqm respectively. ₦20,000 rent incurred is shared using floor space occupied. How much is the rent chargeable to manufacturing?|₦8,000|₦10,000|₦12,000|₦18,000": "Shared overheads are apportioned on a fair basis; for rent the usual basis is floor area. Step 1: total floor space = 200 + 300 = 500 sqm. Step 2: manufacturing share = 200 ÷ 500 = ⅖. Step 3: rent charged to manufacturing = ⅖ × ₦20,000 = ₦8,000: A. The other ₦12,000 goes to administration in the profit and loss account. The trap: C (₦12,000) is the administration share. Memory tip: share of cost = own area ÷ total area × cost.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock of materials 1/1 ... ₦10,000\nPurchase of raw materials ... ₦160,000\nManufacturing wages ... ₦420,000\nRoyalties ... ₦3,000\nStock of raw materials 31/12 ... ₦14,000\n\nWhat is the cost of raw materials consumed?|₦173,000|₦170,000|₦160,000|₦156,000": "Raw materials consumed is the first figure in the manufacturing account. Step 1: opening stock of raw materials ₦10,000 + purchases ₦160,000 = ₦170,000 available. Step 2: less closing stock ₦14,000. Step 3: materials consumed = ₦170,000 − ₦14,000 = ₦156,000: D. The trap: B (₦170,000) is the materials available, before deducting closing stock. Memory tip: opening + purchases − closing = consumed.",
 "|Use the following information: \nStock of materials 1/1 ... ₦10,000\nPurchase of raw materials ... ₦160,000\nManufacturing wages ... ₦420,000\nRoyalties ... ₦3,000\nStock of raw materials 31/12 ... ₦14,000\n\nCalculate the prime cost.|₦597,000|₦579,000|₦576,000|₦567,000": "Prime cost is the total of direct costs: direct materials, direct labour and direct expenses. Step 1: raw materials consumed = ₦10,000 + ₦160,000 − ₦14,000 = ₦156,000. Step 2: direct (manufacturing) wages = ₦420,000. Step 3: royalties are a direct expense = ₦3,000. Step 4: prime cost = ₦156,000 + ₦420,000 + ₦3,000 = ₦579,000: B. The trap: A (₦597,000) swaps two digits of the right answer. Memory tip: prime cost = direct materials + direct labour + direct expenses.",
 "|Receipts and payments account discloses|debtors|creditors|prepayment|cash balances for a period": "A receipts and payments account is a summary of the cash book of a club or society. It starts with the opening cash and bank balance, adds all receipts, deducts all payments and ends with the closing balance. It ignores accruals, prepayments, debtors and creditors, which appear in the income and expenditure account and balance sheet. So it discloses cash balances for a period: D. Memory tip: receipts and payments = cash only; income and expenditure = accruals basis.",
 "|Use the following information: \nSubscription in arrears 01/01/06 ... ₦2,000\nSubscription in advance 01/01/06 ... ₦1,500\nSubscription paid during the year ... ₦7,000\nSubscription in arrears 31/12/06 ... ₦3,000\n\nDetermine the amount of subscription included in the income and expenditure account for the year.|₦3,500|₦7,000|₦9,500|₦13,500": "The income and expenditure account takes only the subscriptions that belong to this year. Step 1: cash received ₦7,000. Step 2: deduct arrears at the start (₦2,000), which were last year's income. Step 3: add subscriptions paid in advance last year for this year (₦1,500). Step 4: add arrears at the end (₦3,000), this year's income not yet received. Step 5: ₦7,000 − ₦2,000 + ₦1,500 + ₦3,000 = ₦9,500: C. The trap: B (₦7,000) is just the cash received. Memory tip: received − opening arrears + opening advance + closing arrears − closing advance.",
 "|The main reason for adopting a departmental account is to|record transactions for each department for better documentation|tackle any recording problem as a result of expansion|continuously evaluate the profitability of different departments|ensure the departmental managers are adequately monitored": "A business with several departments (e.g. groceries, clothing, electronics) prepares departmental accounts so that the gross and net profit of each department is shown separately. Management can then continually compare the departments, expand profitable ones and correct or close unprofitable ones. So the main reason is to continuously evaluate the profitability of the departments: C. Better documentation and supervision are side benefits, not the main purpose. Memory tip: departmental accounts answer \"which department pays?\"",
 "|Use the following information: \nDepartmental Trading Account (Extract)\nItem ... Total ... P ... Q\nStock ... ₦3,000 ... ₦2,000 ... ₦1,000\nPurchases ... ₦4,000 ... ₦2,500 ... ₦1,500\nSales ... ₦10,000 ... ₦6,000 ... ₦4,000\nClosing stock ... ₦2,000 ... ₦1,500 ... ₦500\nGoods worth ₦300 were transferred from department Q to P. Similarly, P's total expenses for the period were ₦200.\n\nWhat was department Q's gross profit?|₦2,500|₦2,300|₦2,200|₦1,700": "Department Q transferred ₦300 of goods to P, so Q's cost of goods is reduced. Step 1: opening stock ₦1,000 + purchases ₦1,500 = ₦2,500. Step 2: less goods transferred out ₦300 = ₦2,200. Step 3: less closing stock ₦500: cost of goods sold = ₦1,700. Step 4: gross profit = sales ₦4,000 − ₦1,700 = ₦2,300: B. The trap: C (₦2,200) comes from adding the transfer to Q instead of deducting it. Memory tip: goods transferred out reduce the sending department's cost of sales.",
 "|Use the following information: \nDepartmental Trading Account (Extract)\nItem ... Total ... P ... Q\nStock ... ₦3,000 ... ₦2,000 ... ₦1,000\nPurchases ... ₦4,000 ... ₦2,500 ... ₦1,500\nSales ... ₦10,000 ... ₦6,000 ... ₦4,000\nClosing stock ... ₦2,000 ... ₦1,500 ... ₦500\nGoods worth ₦300 were transferred from department Q to P. Similarly, P's total expenses for the period were ₦200.\n\nDepartment P's net profit was|₦5,200|₦3,000|₦2,800|₦2,500": "Department P received ₦300 of goods from Q, so P's cost of goods sold rises. Step 1: opening stock ₦2,000 + purchases ₦2,500 + transfer in ₦300 = ₦4,800. Step 2: less closing stock ₦1,500: cost of goods sold = ₦3,300. Step 3: gross profit = ₦6,000 − ₦3,300 = ₦2,700. Step 4: net profit = ₦2,700 − expenses ₦200 = ₦2,500: D. The trap: C (₦2,800) is the net profit if the ₦300 transfer is ignored. Memory tip: gross profit − expenses = net profit, department by department.",
 "|Given: \nI. Cost price\nII. Selling price\nIII. Cost plus fixed percentage\nIV. Price above cost and selling price\n\nFrom the information above, the prices used in charging goods to the branches are|I and II|I, II and III|I, II, III and IV|III and IV": "Head office can invoice (charge) goods sent to its branches at: cost price; selling price, so the branch stock account controls the branch; or cost plus a fixed percentage (a mark-up). These are I, II and III: B. Charging at a price above the selling price is not a recognised method, since the branch could not sell at a profit. Memory tip: branch goods go out at cost, at selling price, or at cost plus.",
 "|If a GSM operator based in Lagos supplies recharge cards to its branch in Akure, the head office can charge the goods at|selling price|Akure price|company price|Lagos price": "Recharge cards have a fixed face value, the price printed on them, which is the same everywhere in Nigeria. When the Lagos head office sends cards to its Akure branch, it can therefore invoice them at selling price; the branch must then account for every card in cash or in stock, which makes control easy. So the goods are charged at selling price: A. Memory tip: fixed retail price goods are usually sent to branches at selling price.",
 "|Use the following information: \nCapital: G ... ₦5,000\nT ... ₦8,000\nDrawings: G ... ₦2,000\nT ... ₦1,000\nInterest on capital 10%\nInterest on drawings 5%\n\nFind the interest on drawings of G.|₦50|₦100|₦200|₦250": "Interest on drawings is charged to a partner to discourage heavy withdrawals; it is added to the profit available for sharing. Step 1: G's drawings = ₦2,000. Step 2: interest at 5% = 5% × ₦2,000 = ₦100: B. Interest on capital (10%) is a different item: G would receive 10% × ₦5,000 = ₦500. The trap: A (₦50) is 5% of T's drawings of ₦1,000. Memory tip: interest on capital is paid to partners; interest on drawings is paid by partners.",
 "|The entry in the partnership books of accounts for interest on drawings is to|credit partners capital account|debit partners capital account|credit drawings account|debit drawings account": "Interest on drawings is a charge against the partner and income to the partnership. The double entry: debit the partner's capital account (or current account, if kept) and credit the profit and loss appropriation account. So the entry is to debit the partners' capital account: B. The trap: A (credit partners' capital account) is the entry for interest on capital, which is paid to partners. Memory tip: partner pays = debit partner; partner receives = credit partner.",
 "|In partnership dissolution, an asset taken over by a partner is debited to|partners capital account and credited to realization account|partners capital account and credited to asset account|assets account and credited to realization account|realization account and credited to capital account": "On dissolution, assets are transferred to the realisation account and sold. When a partner takes over an asset instead of cash, it is as if the asset were sold to him: debit the partner's capital account (he owes the firm the agreed value) and credit the realisation account (the \"sale proceeds\"): A. Option D reverses the entry. Memory tip: asset taken by a partner = debit capital, credit realisation.",
 "|Goodwill can be in the balance sheet as|fixed tangible asset|current asset|fictitious asset|liquid asset": "Goodwill is the value of a business's reputation, customers and location above its net assets. It has no physical form, so it is not a tangible asset, and it cannot be sold separately, so it is not current or liquid. Older Nigerian texts grouped it with fictitious assets: C. Today, IAS 38 and IFRS 3 treat purchased goodwill as an intangible non-current asset, tested for impairment each year. Memory tip: goodwill cannot be touched or spent on its own.",
 "|Given: \nI. Ascertainment of the particulars of the proposed company\nII. Preparation of the incorporation documents\nIII. Filing of the documents\nIV. Registration of the company\n\nFrom the information above, the stages involved in the formation of a company are|I, II and III|I, II, III and IV|I, II and IV|II, III and IV": "Forming a company is a legal process. Under the Companies and Allied Matters Act, the promoters prepare the incorporation documents (memorandum and articles of association and the statutory forms), file them with the Corporate Affairs Commission, and the company is then registered and given a certificate of incorporation. These are II, III and IV: D. Ascertaining the particulars (I) is the promoters' groundwork, so it is debatable whether it counts as a stage. Today CAMA 2020 governs formation, and filing is done online. Memory tip: prepare, file, register.",
 "|Cash received from the subscribed shares is recorded by debiting|application, allotment and crediting cash|allotment and crediting cash|cash and crediting subscribed shares|cash, crediting application and allotment account": "When a company receives money from people applying for shares, cash (bank) is increased, so it is debited; the application and allotment account is credited with the money received. Later, the application and allotment account is debited and the share capital account credited when shares are allotted. So the entry is debit cash, credit application and allotment account: D. Memory tip: money in = debit bank; share money = credit application and allotment.",
 "|Ordinary shareholders received dividend before|creditors|debtors|preference shareholders|deferred shareholders": "Shareholders are paid dividends in a fixed order. Preference shareholders come first, at their fixed rate; ordinary shareholders come next; deferred (founders') shareholders come last, only after the others have been paid. So ordinary shareholders receive dividends before deferred shareholders: D. Creditors are paid before any shareholder, and debtors receive nothing. Memory tip: preference, then ordinary, then deferred.",
 "|Use the following information: \nUju Nig. Ltd. Balance Sheet (Extract)\nCapital ... ₦200,000\nNet profit ... ₦50,000\n5% debenture ... ₦100,000\nCreditors ... ₦80,000\nTotal ... ₦430,000\nLand and building ... ₦250,000\nStock ... ₦120,000\nDebtors ... ₦50,000\nBank ... ₦10,000\nTotal ... ₦430,000\n\nThe debt ratio is approximately|43%|42%|41%|23%": "The debt ratio shows how much of the assets are financed by borrowing: total liabilities ÷ total assets. Step 1: total liabilities = 5% debenture ₦100,000 + creditors ₦80,000 = ₦180,000. Step 2: total assets = ₦430,000. Step 3: ₦180,000 ÷ ₦430,000 × 100 = 41.9%, about 42%: B. The answer often given, 23%, divides the debentures alone by total assets and leaves out the creditors; some texts call that a long-term debt ratio, so the point is debatable. The trap: C (41%) cuts 41.9% down instead of rounding it up. Memory tip: debt ratio = all debts ÷ all assets.",
 "|Use the following information: \nUju Nig. Ltd. Balance Sheet (Extract)\nCapital ... ₦200,000\nNet profit ... ₦50,000\n5% debenture ... ₦100,000\nCreditors ... ₦80,000\nTotal ... ₦430,000\nLand and building ... ₦250,000\nStock ... ₦120,000\nDebtors ... ₦50,000\nBank ... ₦10,000\nTotal ... ₦430,000\n\nWhich of the following is true of Uju Nig. Ltd?|It is a highly geared company|It has no liquidity problem in the short term|It is adequately secured in fixed assets|Its working capital is zero": "Test each statement. Step 1, liquidity: current assets = stock ₦120,000 + debtors ₦50,000 + bank ₦10,000 = ₦180,000; current liabilities = creditors ₦80,000; current ratio = 2.25:1 and working capital ₦100,000, so short-term debts can be met: B is true and D (zero working capital) is false. Step 2, gearing: debentures ₦100,000 against shareholders' funds ₦250,000 is low, so A is false. Option C is vague. So the true statement is that it has no short-term liquidity problem: B. Memory tip: current ratio above 2:1 suggests no short-term liquidity problem.",
 "|A source of revenue that is compulsory payment without any derivable benefit is|fees|taxes|fines|royalties": "Government revenue comes from taxes, fees, fines, royalties, rents and others. A tax is a compulsory payment to government for which the taxpayer receives no direct benefit in return: the money funds public services for everyone. Fees and royalties buy a particular service or right, and a fine is a penalty for an offence, not a regular source of revenue paid by everyone. So the answer is taxes: B. The trap: C (fines) is also compulsory and buys nothing, but it is a punishment, not the definition of a tax. Memory tip: tax = compulsory, no quid pro quo.",
 "|Which of the following is among the categories of government cash book?|Receipts cash book|Payment cash book|Petty cash book|Treasury cash book": "In government accounting, all receipts and payments of public funds are recorded in the treasury cash book, kept at the treasury and sub-treasuries (the main cash book), with departmental cash books in the ministries. So the category listed is the treasury cash book: D. Receipts and payments are columns or sides of a cash book, and the petty cash book belongs to imprest accounting. Memory tip: government cash = treasury cash book.",
 "|The salary of the Chief Justice of the Federation is paid from the|consolidated revenue fund|development fund|contingency fund|special fund": "Under the 1999 Constitution, the salaries and allowances of certain independent office holders, including the Chief Justice of the Federation, the Auditor-General and INEC members, are a direct charge on the Consolidated Revenue Fund. This means they are paid without waiting for the yearly appropriation, which protects their independence. So the answer is the consolidated revenue fund: A. Memory tip: independent offices are paid from the CRF directly.",
 "|The Auditor-General of the Federation can be removed by the|National Assembly|Chief Justice|President|Head of service": "The Auditor-General for the Federation audits the public accounts and reports to the National Assembly. To protect his independence, section 87 of the 1999 Constitution says he can be removed only by the President, acting on an address supported by a two-thirds majority of the Senate, for inability to perform or misconduct. So the President removes him: C. The trap: A (National Assembly) catches students because the Senate must support the removal, but the President is the one who removes. Memory tip: the President appoints and removes, with the Senate's support."
};
